window.SIMBANK=[{"i":"q1","t":"Basics","q":"What is a shell, and how is it different from the kernel?","a":"A shell is a command-language interpreter that starts programs and provides features such as variables, redirection and pipelines. The kernel handles core system resources and interfaces. Bash is a shell, not the Linux kernel.","e":"type cd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["command-language","pipelines","redirection","interpreter","handles","type","features"]},{"i":"q2","t":"Basics","q":"What does an absolute path mean?","a":"An absolute path starts at the root directory /. A relative path is interpreted from the current working directory. The shell expands ~ to a home directory in applicable contexts; ~ is not the filesystem root.","e":"cd /var/log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["absolute","interpreted","var/log","applicable","relative","path","contexts"]},{"i":"q3","t":"Basics","q":"Why quote a pathname in the shell?","a":"Unquoted expansions can undergo word splitting and filename expansion. Double quotes preserve spaces in a variable value while allowing variable expansion. Single quotes preserve literal text.","e":"cd \"$HOME\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["unquoted","undergo","splitting","preserve","filename","literal","home"]},{"i":"q4","t":"Basics","q":"What is an environment variable?","a":"An environment variable is a name/value passed to a process. In Bash, export marks a variable for inheritance by subsequently started child processes. A child cannot directly change its parent shell environment.","e":"export APP_ENV=development","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["name/value","export","app_env","development","inheritance","subsequently","variable"]},{"i":"q5","t":"Basics","q":"What is an exit status?","a":"Commands return a status to the shell. Zero conventionally indicates success; nonzero meanings depend on the command. In Bash, $? contains the status of the most recent foreground pipeline.","e":"printf \"%s\\n\" \"$?\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["conventionally","nonzero","printf","foreground","meanings","zero","indicates"]},{"i":"q6","t":"Shell","q":"What is a pipeline?","a":"A pipe connects the standard output of one command to the standard input of the next. It does not automatically include standard error. In Bash, pipeline status normally comes from the last command unless pipefail is enabled.","e":"printf \"%s\\n\" beta alpha | sort","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["beta","alpha","pipe","printf","pipeline","pipefail","sort"]},{"i":"q7","t":"Shell","q":"What is the difference between > and >>?","a":"Both redirect standard output to a file. > normally creates or truncates it, while >> creates or appends. Check the destination before redirecting because data can be overwritten.","e":"printf \"%s\\n\" \"note\" >> ./practice-notes.txt","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["practice-notes.txt","note","overwritten","printf","redirect","truncates","appends"]},{"i":"q8","t":"Shell","q":"What are stdin, stdout and stderr?","a":"They are conventional process streams: standard input (descriptor 0), standard output (1) and standard error (2). The shell can redirect them independently. Error output is not automatically captured by a stdout-only pipe.","e":"grep -F \"ERROR\" ./app.log 2> ./grep-errors.txt","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["stdout-only","grep-errors.txt","descriptor","redirect","captured","independently","conventional"]},{"i":"q9","t":"Shell","q":"What is a shell builtin?","a":"A builtin is implemented inside the shell. Commands such as cd need to affect the current shell state. Use type to inspect how a name resolves instead of assuming every command is a separate binary.","e":"type cd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["builtin","assuming","implemented","resolves","type","shell","inspect"]},{"i":"q10","t":"Files","q":"What does an inode store?","a":"An inode represents file metadata such as type, permissions, owner, link count and timestamps, plus filesystem-specific information about data. A directory entry maps a filename to an inode number. The name is not stored as an inode field.","e":"stat ./notes.txt","s":"https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/inode.7.html","l":"Beginner","k":["filesystem-specific","notes.txt","inode","timestamps","filename","metadata","stat"]},{"i":"q11","t":"Files","q":"How do hard links and symbolic links differ?","a":"A hard link is another directory entry for the same inode and normally cannot cross filesystems or point to directories. A symbolic link is its own file containing a target pathname. A symbolic link may point to a missing target.","e":"ln -s ./notes.txt ./notes-link","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/symlink.7.html","l":"Beginner","k":["notes-link","symbolic","pathname","notes.txt","hard","link","inode"]},{"i":"q12","t":"Files","q":"Why can a symbolic link become broken?","a":"A symbolic link stores a pathname. If the target is moved or removed, path resolution may no longer find it. Relative target names are resolved from the directory containing the link, not from whichever directory you later run a command in.","e":"readlink ./notes-link","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/symlink.7.html","l":"Beginner","k":["readlink","notes-link","whichever","symbolic","pathname","link","resolution"]},{"i":"q13","t":"Files","q":"How would you find log files safely?","a":"Search a specific directory with find and a quoted pattern. Start with read-only output. Do not add -delete or a deletion action until the result set, backups and retention rules have been reviewed.","e":"find ./logs -type f -name \"*.log\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/find.1.html","l":"Scenario","k":["read-only","find","quoted","retention","deletion","log","type"]},{"i":"q14","t":"Permissions","q":"What does chmod 640 mean for a regular file?","a":"Owner permissions are read and write (6), group has read (4), and others have none (0). Permission bits are not the only access controls: ACLs, security modules and parent-directory permissions can also matter.","e":"chmod 640 ./notes.txt","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["parent-directory","chmod","notes.txt","acls","none","matter","modules"]},{"i":"q15","t":"Permissions","q":"What does execute permission mean on a directory?","a":"Directory execute permission permits search/traversal. Read permission allows listing names. Modifying entries generally needs write and execute on the containing directory, with other controls such as the sticky bit also applying.","e":"ls -ld ./practice","s":"https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/inode.7.html","l":"Beginner","k":["search/traversal","practice","sticky","execute","permission","permits","modifying"]},{"i":"q16","t":"Permissions","q":"What are owner, group and other permissions?","a":"Traditional permission bits define access for the file owner, members of the file group and everyone else. Use ls -l or stat to inspect them. Extra controls such as ACLs can affect the final result.","e":"ls -l ./notes.txt","s":"https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/inode.7.html","l":"Beginner","k":["notes.txt","acls","define","everyone","owner","permission","final"]},{"i":"q17","t":"Permissions","q":"Why is chmod 777 a bad default fix?","a":"It grants read, write and execute to owner, group and others. That is broader than most tasks need and can expose or allow changes to data. Identify the needed user/group and grant only the required access.","e":"chmod 640 ./notes.txt","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html","l":"Scenario","k":["user/group","broader","chmod","notes.txt","expose","execute","identify"]},{"i":"q18","t":"Storage","q":"What is the difference between df and du?","a":"df reports usage and available space for a filesystem. du estimates space used by files in a directory tree. They can differ because of permissions, filesystem behavior and open deleted files, among other reasons.","e":"df -h .\ndu -sh ./logs","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/df.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["differ","behavior","reasons","estimates","logs","tree","filesystem"]},{"i":"q19","t":"Storage","q":"How can a filesystem be full when bytes remain?","a":"A filesystem can run out of allocatable inodes before exhausting data blocks, depending on its design. Inspect both block usage and inode usage. Many tiny files can contribute to inode exhaustion.","e":"df -h .\ndf -i .","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/df.1.html","l":"Scenario","k":["allocatable","exhausting","inodes","exhaustion","contribute","tiny","inode"]},{"i":"q20","t":"Processes","q":"How do you inspect running processes?","a":"ps gives a process snapshot. ps -ef selects all processes with a full listing in procps. Inspect PID, parent PID, owner and command; process namespaces and permissions affect what you can see.","e":"ps -ef","s":"https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ps.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["procps","namespaces","snapshot","processes","inspect","process","pid"]},{"i":"q21","t":"Processes","q":"Why use SIGTERM before SIGKILL?","a":"SIGTERM requests termination and can be handled so the program can clean up. SIGKILL cannot be caught, blocked or ignored. Verify the PID and impact first. Forced termination can leave incomplete work.","e":"kill -TERM 12345","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/kill.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["term","incomplete","sigterm","sigkill","kill","ignored","termination"]},{"i":"q22","t":"System","q":"Why is low free RAM not always a problem?","a":"Linux uses memory for caches. The available value estimates memory usable for new applications without swapping and includes reclaimable memory. Check workload behavior and pressure instead of treating the free column alone as an alarm.","e":"free -h","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/free.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["reclaimable","caches","swapping","usable","free","workload","alarm"]},{"i":"q23","t":"System","q":"Does uname -r show the distribution version?","a":"No. It prints the kernel release. The distribution release is a different concept. A distribution can update or customize its kernel independently from its release naming.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/uname.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["independently","uname","distribution","concept","customize","naming","release"]},{"i":"q24","t":"Services","q":"What is systemd?","a":"systemd is a system and service manager. When running as PID 1, it initializes userspace and manages units. Not every Linux distribution uses systemd, so its tools are not universally available.","e":"systemctl list-units --type=service","s":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.html","l":"Beginner","k":["initializes","userspace","universally","list-units","systemd","units","type"]},{"i":"q25","t":"Services","q":"How are active and enabled different?","a":"Active describes a unit running now. Enabled describes unit-file links used to arrange automatic activation. An enabled service can be inactive; an active service can be disabled. Static and other unit states also exist.","e":"systemctl is-active ssh.service\nsystemctl is-enabled ssh.service","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/systemctl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["unit-file","ssh.service","is-active","is-enabled","active","arrange","enabled"]},{"i":"q26","t":"Services","q":"A service failed. What should you check first?","a":"Inspect status, unit logs, configuration, dependencies and recent changes. Confirm the service name for the distribution. Avoid blind restarts on production; understand the error and the effect on users before changing state.","e":"systemctl status nginx.service\njournalctl -u nginx.service -n 50 --no-pager","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/systemctl.1.html","l":"Scenario","k":["nginx.service","no-pager","restarts","journalctl","blind","service","dependencies"]},{"i":"q27","t":"Boot","q":"How do you inspect logs from the current boot?","a":"On a systemd machine, journalctl -b selects the current boot. Journal access can require extra permissions and prior-boot history depends on storage settings. Logs can contain private information.","e":"journalctl -b --no-pager","s":"https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/journalctl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["prior-boot","no-pager","journal","journalctl","current","boot","systemd"]},{"i":"q28","t":"Networking","q":"How do you inspect network addresses and routes?","a":"Use ip address show for addresses and ip route show for routes. A configured address or default route does not prove DNS resolution or end-to-end connectivity. Inspect each layer separately.","e":"ip address show\nip route show","s":"https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["end-to-end","route","address","connectivity","separately","addresses","configured"]},{"i":"q29","t":"Networking","q":"How do you list listening TCP and UDP sockets?","a":"Use ss with TCP, UDP, listening and numeric-output selectors. A listening socket indicates local socket state, not whether a remote firewall or routing path permits access.","e":"ss -tuln","s":"https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["numeric-output","selectors","udp","tuln","socket","tcp","listening"]},{"i":"q30","t":"Networking","q":"Why verify an SSH host key?","a":"The host key helps authenticate the remote server. Check its fingerprint through a trusted channel before accepting an unexpected key. A changed key may be legitimate, but do not blindly remove warnings or disable verification.","e":"ssh user@server.example","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Scenario","k":["server.example","unexpected","fingerprint","blindly","disable","authenticate","legitimate"]},{"i":"q31","t":"Scheduling","q":"How do you view your scheduled cron jobs?","a":"crontab -l lists the current user table. Cron jobs run with an environment that can differ from an interactive shell. Use intentional paths and logging, and check the implementation documentation before scheduling.","e":"crontab -l","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/crontab.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["cron","intentional","crontab","jobs","differ","logging","scheduling"]},{"i":"q32","t":"Text","q":"How do fixed-string grep and regular-expression grep differ?","a":"grep normally interprets a regular expression. -F treats the pattern as a fixed string. Quote patterns to prevent shell expansion; -n shows matching line numbers.","e":"grep -n -F \"ERROR\" ./app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/grep.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["expression","string","regular","interprets","fixed","grep","error"]},{"i":"q33","t":"Text","q":"Does uniq remove every duplicate from unsorted data?","a":"No. It compares adjacent lines. Sort first if you want equal lines grouped, recognizing that sorting changes order. Decide whether preserving original order is important before choosing that pipeline.","e":"sort ./names.txt | uniq","s":"https://linuxcommand.org/lc3_man_pages/uniq1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["names.txt","uniq","recognizing","sort","adjacent","preserving","compares"]},{"i":"q34","t":"Files","q":"How do you inspect a file before changing it?","a":"Start with its path, owner, mode, type, size and timestamps. stat and ls -l provide metadata; read small text files with cat or head. Confirm you are in the intended directory before any write.","e":"pwd\nstat ./notes.txt","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/stat.1.html","l":"Scenario","k":["notes.txt","timestamps","intended","metadata","pwd","stat","provide"]},{"i":"q35","t":"Shell","q":"Why should you not paste environment or history output publicly?","a":"Environment variables and command history may contain passwords, tokens or private paths. Inspect only the specific value needed and review any diagnostic output before sharing it.","e":"printenv HOME","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Scenario","k":["tokens","printenv","history","environment","output","home","diagnostic"]},{"i":"qz000","t":"Basics","q":"Who created the Linux kernel?","a":"Linus Torvalds. Linus Torvalds announced the Linux kernel in 1991 as a student in Finland.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/process/license-rules.html","l":"Beginner","k":["linus","torvalds","finland","uname","announced","student","kernel"]},{"i":"qz001","t":"Basics","q":"Linux itself is technically a...","a":"Kernel. Linux names the kernel, the layer between programs and hardware. A distribution adds tools and apps; some distributions do not use GNU tools.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://www.gnu.org/gnu/gnu-history.html","l":"Beginner","k":["etc/os-release","cat","apps","layer","distribution","hardware","gnu"]},{"i":"qz002","t":"Basics","q":"In which year was the Linux kernel first released?","a":"1991. Torvalds posted the first public release, version 0.01, in September 1991.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/sed.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["release","torvalds","posted","uname","september","first","version"]},{"i":"qz003","t":"Basics","q":"The Linux kernel is licensed under...","a":"GNU GPL v2. Linux kernel source is distributed under GPL-2.0-only, with a syscall exception; redistribution of modified kernel code has license conditions. Think kernel license, not every Linux app.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://www.gnu.org/gnu/gnu-history.html","l":"Beginner","k":["gpl-2.0-only","license","redistribution","syscall","gpl","think","uname"]},{"i":"qz004","t":"Basics","q":"Who founded the GNU Project?","a":"Richard Stallman. Richard Stallman started GNU in 1983 to build a free Unix-like OS.","e":"free -h","s":"https://www.gnu.org/gnu/gnu-history.html","l":"Beginner","k":["unix-like","stallman","gnu","free","richard","build","started"]},{"i":"qz005","t":"Scheduling","q":"GNU is a recursive acronym meaning...","a":"GNU's Not Unix. GNU stands for 'GNU's Not Unix' - a hacker's recursive joke.","e":"crontab -l","s":"https://www.gnu.org/gnu/gnu-history.html","l":"Beginner","k":["hacker","recursive","joke","crontab","gnu","stands","unix"]},{"i":"qz006","t":"Basics","q":"Which of these is a Linux distribution?","a":"Ubuntu. Ubuntu is a Debian-based Linux distro; Solaris and FreeBSD are Unix-like but not Linux.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["debian-based","solaris","freebsd","unix-like","ubuntu","distro","etc/os-release"]},{"i":"qz007","t":"Basics","q":"The official Linux mascot is...","a":"Tux the penguin. Tux the penguin is the Linux mascot, chosen by Linus Torvalds.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/process/license-rules.html","l":"Beginner","k":["mascot","penguin","tux","linus","torvalds","chosen","uname"]},{"i":"qz008","t":"Basics","q":"Unix, which inspired Linux, was originally developed at...","a":"Bell Labs. Ken Thompson and Dennis Ritchie built Unix at AT&T's Bell Labs in 1969.","e":"which python3","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/process/license-rules.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ritchie","unix","thompson","dennis","ken","python3","bell"]},{"i":"qz009","t":"Basics","q":"Android is built on top of which kernel?","a":"Linux kernel. Android uses a modified Linux kernel under its own user space.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/process/license-rules.html","l":"Beginner","k":["android","uname","modified","kernel","space","own","user"]},{"i":"qz010","t":"Basics","q":"What is a shell in Linux?","a":"A command-line interpreter between user and kernel. The shell reads your commands and asks the kernel to run them.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["command-line","interpreter","uname","shell","asks","reads","kernel"]},{"i":"qz011","t":"Basics","q":"On many traditional Linux distributions, which shell is commonly the default interactive shell?","a":"bash. bash is common on Debian, Ubuntu and many server installations. Defaults vary by distro and account; check your own with getent passwd \"$USER\".","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["getent","ubuntu","distro","etc/os-release","vary","installations","passwd"]},{"i":"qz012","t":"Basics","q":"The main job of the Linux kernel is to...","a":"Manage hardware resources like CPU, memory and devices. The kernel schedules CPU work, manages memory and devices. It is not the same thing as a shell or desktop.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["uname","thing","desktop","manage","manages","kernel","resources"]},{"i":"qz013","t":"Basics","q":"Open source software means...","a":"Source code can be viewed, modified and shared. Open source licenses grant rights to read, modify and redistribute code.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/process/license-rules.html","l":"Beginner","k":["licenses","redistribute","source","open","etc/os-release","modified","modify"]},{"i":"qz014","t":"Basics","q":"Which of these is NOT a Linux distribution?","a":"Apache. Apache is a web server; Debian, Fedora and Arch are distros.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["fedora","arch","distros","etc/os-release","apache","cat","debian"]},{"i":"qz015","t":"Basics","q":"Ubuntu is based on which distribution?","a":"Debian. Ubuntu is built from Debian's unstable/testing branches.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["unstable/testing","ubuntu","branches","etc/os-release","built","cat","debian"]},{"i":"qz016","t":"Basics","q":"Which distribution is built from Red Hat Enterprise Linux sources?","a":"AlmaLinux. AlmaLinux is built from RHEL sources for binary compatibility. It is not necessarily a bit-for-bit or 1:1 rebuild of every RHEL package.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/process/license-rules.html","l":"Beginner","k":["bit-for-bit","almalinux","built","sources","rebuild","necessarily","etc/os-release"]},{"i":"qz017","t":"Basics","q":"Arch Linux follows which release model?","a":"Rolling release. Arch is rolling: you install once and keep updating forever.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["release","arch","updating","rolling","forever","python3","once"]},{"i":"qz018","t":"Basics","q":"The apt package manager belongs to which distro family?","a":"Debian/Ubuntu. apt manages .deb packages on Debian, Ubuntu and their derivatives.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["derivatives","debian/ubuntu","deb","ubuntu","etc/os-release","apt","manages"]},{"i":"qz019","t":"Basics","q":"Which command shows the running kernel version?","a":"uname -r. uname -r prints the kernel release, e.g. 5.15.0-91-generic.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/process/license-rules.html","l":"Beginner","k":["e.g","uname","generic","kernel","release","prints"]},{"i":"qz020","t":"Basics","q":"A Linux distribution bundles the kernel with...","a":"GNU tools, package manager and applications. Distro = Linux kernel + GNU utilities + package management + apps.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://www.gnu.org/gnu/gnu-history.html","l":"Beginner","k":["utilities","uname","distro","kernel","apps","management","manager"]},{"i":"qz021","t":"Basics","q":"Linus Torvalds said he was inspired by which teaching OS?","a":"MINIX. Andrew Tanenbaum's MINIX inspired Torvalds to write his own kernel.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/process/license-rules.html","l":"Beginner","k":["inspired","tanenbaum","minix","torvalds","andrew","his","uname"]},{"i":"qz022","t":"Basics","q":"According to recent TOP500 lists, which OS family dominates the top 500 supercomputers?","a":"Linux. Linux dominates TOP500 systems. This is a changing ranking, so check the latest list rather than memorizing a permanent 100% claim.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/process/license-rules.html","l":"Beginner","k":["dominates","top500","memorizing","uname","ranking","latest","permanent"]},{"i":"qz023","t":"Basics","q":"Which of these is a Linux desktop environment?","a":"GNOME. GNOME (like KDE Plasma or XFCE) is a full desktop environment.","e":"printenv HOME","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/process/license-rules.html","l":"Beginner","k":["desktop","printenv","kde","xfce","environment","plasma","gnome"]},{"i":"qz024","t":"Basics","q":"The GPL's 'copyleft' rule means...","a":"Derivative works must stay under the same license. GPL copyleft applies when you distribute covered derivative code: recipients get the same GPL rights. Private changes need not be published just because you made them.","e":"less /usr/share/common-licenses/GPL-2","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/process/license-rules.html","l":"Beginner","k":["usr/share/common-licenses/gpl-2","copyleft","gpl","license","derivative","distribute","less"]},{"i":"qz025","t":"Basics","q":"Which command prints your current directory?","a":"pwd. pwd = print working directory.","e":"pwd","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["print","pwd","directory","working"]},{"i":"qz026","t":"Basics","q":"How do you jump straight to your home directory?","a":"cd ~. cd with no argument or cd ~ lands in your home directory.","e":"cd ~","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["lands","home","argument","directory"]},{"i":"qz027","t":"Basics","q":"The top of the Linux filesystem tree is...","a":"/. Everything hangs off the single root directory '/'.","e":"pwd","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["hangs","pwd","single","everything","root","directory"]},{"i":"qz028","t":"Basics","q":"Which ls option also shows hidden files?","a":"ls -a. -a (all) includes dotfiles like .bashrc.","e":"ls -a","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ls.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["dotfiles","bashrc","all"]},{"i":"qz029","t":"Basics","q":"Which option gives a long listing with permissions and owner?","a":"ls -l. ls -l shows type, permissions, owner, size and mtime.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["mtime","owner","app.log","permissions","stat","size","type"]},{"i":"qz030","t":"Basics","q":"How do you show file sizes in human-readable form (K, M, G)?","a":"ls -lh. -h makes sizes human readable; combine with -l.","e":"file report","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/rm.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["readable","human","sizes","combine","report","makes"]},{"i":"qz031","t":"Basics","q":"Which command moves you one directory up?","a":"cd ... '..' always means the parent directory.","e":"pwd","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["pwd","directory","means","parent"]},{"i":"qz032","t":"Basics","q":"Which of these paths is absolute as written (before shell expansion)?","a":"/etc/ssh/sshd_config. An absolute path starts with /. ~/data is expanded to a home-directory path by some shells, but the literal path does not start with /.","e":"pwd","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["home-directory","etc/ssh/sshd_config","absolute","literal","pwd","path","expanded"]},{"i":"qz033","t":"Basics","q":"Hidden files in Linux start with...","a":"A dot (.). Files like .profile are hidden from plain ls.","e":"ls -a","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["hidden","profile","dot","plain"]},{"i":"qz034","t":"Basics","q":"System-wide configuration files live in...","a":"/etc. /etc holds configs like /etc/passwd and /etc/fstab.","e":"passwd trainee","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["etc/fstab","etc/passwd","trainee","passwd","configs","holds"]},{"i":"qz035","t":"Basics","q":"Logs and other constantly changing data belong in...","a":"/var. /var = variable data: logs, caches, spool.","e":"echo \"$HOME\"","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["spool","caches","home","echo","variable","logs","var"]},{"i":"qz036","t":"Basics","q":"Regular users' home directories are usually under...","a":"/home. Users get /home/username; root alone gets /root.","e":"cd ~","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["home/username","home","users","get","alone","root"]},{"i":"qz037","t":"Basics","q":"Files in /tmp are intended for...","a":"Temporary data that may be deleted by cleanup policy. /tmp is temporary scratch space. Cleanup timing depends on the system; do not depend on its contents surviving a reboot.","e":"sudo reboot","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["scratch","tmp","surviving","cleanup","reboot","depend","temporary"]},{"i":"qz038","t":"Basics","q":"According to the Filesystem Hierarchy Standard, which path is for essential system-administration binaries?","a":"/sbin. /sbin historically held essential admin commands. On merged-/usr systems it may be a symlink to /usr/sbin.","e":"ln -s /real/target linkname","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["merged-/usr","usr/sbin","essential","real/target","linkname","historically","sbin"]},{"i":"qz039","t":"Basics","q":"/proc is...","a":"A virtual filesystem exposing kernel and process info. /proc is generated on the fly by the kernel; try cat /proc/cpuinfo.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/proc.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["proc/cpuinfo","proc","exposing","info","try","generated","fly"]},{"i":"qz040","t":"Basics","q":"Device files like /dev/sda are found in...","a":"/dev. /dev holds special files representing hardware devices.","e":"file report","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["dev","special","report","representing","holds","hardware","devices"]},{"i":"qz041","t":"Basics","q":"According to the Filesystem Hierarchy Standard, where do shareable, read-only system programs and data primarily belong?","a":"/usr. /usr contains many binaries, libraries and shareable data. Local administrator-installed software often goes under /usr/local instead.","e":"file report","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["administrator-installed","shareable","usr/local","binaries","report","usr","goes"]},{"i":"qz042","t":"Basics","q":"What does 'cd -' do?","a":"Jumps back to the previous directory. cd - toggles to the last directory you were in.","e":"pwd","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["toggles","pwd","jumps","were","previous","back","last"]},{"i":"qz043","t":"Basics","q":"In paths, '~' expands to...","a":"The current user's home directory. At the start of a shell word, ~ normally expands to the current user’s home directory. This is shell expansion, not a literal filesystem path.","e":"cd ~","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["expands","literal","path","expansion","word","filesystem","current"]},{"i":"qz044","t":"Basics","q":"Linux filenames are generally...","a":"Case-sensitive (File.txt and file.txt differ). ext4 and friends treat File.txt and file.txt as two files.","e":"ls File.txt file.txt","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/diff.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["case-sensitive","file.txt","differ","ext4","friends","two","treat"]},{"i":"qz045","t":"Basics","q":"Which command finds files by name under a directory tree?","a":"find /path -name pattern. find walks the tree; -name matches the filename.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/find.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["tree","filename","walks","find","pwd","name","pattern"]},{"i":"qz046","t":"Basics","q":"In the traditional Unix saying, many resources are represented as a...","a":"File. The Unix idea is to expose many resources through file-like interfaces, for example devices under /dev. It is a useful model, not a literal claim about every object.","e":"source ~/.bashrc","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["file-like","source","bashrc","expose","literal","resources","unix"]},{"i":"qz047","t":"Basics","q":"Which command shows how much space a directory uses?","a":"du -sh dir. du -sh summarizes disk usage; df is for whole filesystems.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/df.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["dir","summarizes","pwd","filesystems","whole","usage","disk"]},{"i":"qz048","t":"Basics","q":"The path separator on Linux is...","a":"/ (forward slash). Linux uses / ; backslash is an escape character in the shell.","e":"echo $SHELL","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["backslash","slash","escape","character","echo","shell","forward"]},{"i":"qz049","t":"Basics","q":"A single dot '.' in a path refers to...","a":"The current directory. . = here, .. = one level up.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["pwd","level","current","one","directory"]},{"i":"qz050","t":"Files","q":"Which command creates a new directory?","a":"mkdir. mkdir projects creates the directory 'projects'.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/mkdir.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["mkdir","creates","projects","pwd","directory"]},{"i":"qz051","t":"Files","q":"What does mkdir -p /a/b/c do?","a":"Creates parent directories as needed. -p makes the whole chain and never errors if it exists.","e":"mkdir -p projects/app","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/mkdir.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["projects/app","mkdir","chain","whole","makes","parent","directories"]},{"i":"qz052","t":"Files","q":"On a nonexistent path, touch normally creates what?","a":"Create an empty file or update its timestamp. touch creates an empty file if it does not exist. For an existing file, it changes timestamps instead of emptying it.","e":"sudo apt update","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/touch.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["touch","create","timestamp","update","creates","exist","apt"]},{"i":"qz053","t":"Files","q":"Copy file1.txt to backup.txt with...","a":"cp file1.txt backup.txt. cp leaves the original in place; mv would rename.","e":"file report","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/cp.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["file1.txt","backup.txt","rename","leaves","report","original","place"]},{"i":"qz054","t":"Files","q":"Copying a whole directory tree requires...","a":"cp -r. -r (recursive) descends into subdirectories.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/cp.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["descends","subdirectories","recursive","pwd"]},{"i":"qz055","t":"Files","q":"Which command both moves and renames files?","a":"mv. mv old new renames; mv file dir/ moves.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/mv.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["renames","moves","python3","dir","old","new"]},{"i":"qz056","t":"Files","q":"Delete a file with...","a":"rm file. rm removes the file immediately - no trash bin.","e":"file report","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/rm.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["trash","removes","bin","immediately","report"]},{"i":"qz057","t":"Files","q":"rmdir works only when the directory is...","a":"Empty. rmdir refuses to remove non-empty directories; use rm -r for those.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/rm.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["non-empty","rmdir","refuses","pwd","empty","remove","directories"]},{"i":"qz058","t":"Files","q":"rm -r is used to...","a":"Delete directories and their contents recursively. rm -r deletes the directory and everything inside it.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/sed.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["recursively","deletes","pwd","delete","contents","inside","everything"]},{"i":"qz059","t":"Files","q":"What makes rm -rf dangerous on a wrong path?","a":"It force-deletes everything without asking, no undo. -r descends into directories; -f suppresses many prompts. GNU rm protects the literal / by default, not every dangerous typo.","e":"pwd","s":"https://www.gnu.org/gnu/gnu-history.html","l":"Beginner","k":["force-deletes","dangerous","suppresses","undo","descends","typo","prompts"]},{"i":"qz060","t":"Files","q":"Create a symbolic (soft) link with...","a":"ln -s /real/target linkname. ln -s makes a symlink; plain ln makes a hard link.","e":"ln file.txt file.hard","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["real/target","file.hard","hard","linkname","file.txt","symlink","link"]},{"i":"qz061","t":"Files","q":"For a regular file on the same filesystem, why can a hard link still access its data after another name is removed?","a":"It shares the same inode, so it survives target deletion. Both names point to one inode. Removing one directory entry does not remove file data while another hard link remains.","e":"ln file.txt file.hard","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["hard","file.hard","survives","file.txt","remove","deletion","link"]},{"i":"qz062","t":"Files","q":"Which command identifies a file's real type regardless of extension?","a":"file. file inspects magic bytes, e.g. 'report' could be a PDF without .pdf.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["e.g","pdf","python3","magic","inspects","bytes","report"]},{"i":"qz063","t":"Files","q":"Which tool looks up file paths using a periodically updated database (when installed)?","a":"locate. locate/plocate uses a file-name index updated by updatedb. It can be stale and is not installed everywhere.","e":"locate httpd.conf","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/cat.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["locate/plocate","file-name","httpd.conf","locate","updatedb","stale","updated"]},{"i":"qz064","t":"Files","q":"With GNU find, which command matches files whose size rounds up to more than 100 units of 1 MiB under /home?","a":"find /home -size +100M. GNU find -size +100M uses 1 MiB units rounded up; it is not exactly the same as a strict >100 MiB byte comparison. Add -type f to exclude directories.","e":"which python3","s":"https://www.gnu.org/gnu/gnu-history.html","l":"Beginner","k":["mib","exclude","strict","size","rounded","gnu","byte"]},{"i":"qz065","t":"Files","q":"Which command looks up a command on PATH and prints an executable path when found?","a":"which. which searches PATH for an executable; e.g. which python3 may print /usr/bin/python3. Shell aliases/functions need separate inspection with type.","e":"myfunc() { echo hi; }","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["usr/bin/python3","aliases/functions","myfunc","executable","e.g","inspection","print"]},{"i":"qz066","t":"Files","q":"Rename notes.txt to todo.txt using...","a":"mv notes.txt todo.txt. mv handles renames within the same directory.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/mv.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["todo.txt","notes.txt","renames","handles","pwd","directory"]},{"i":"qz067","t":"Files","q":"Which cp option preserves permissions and timestamps like an archive copy?","a":"cp -a. -a (archive) keeps mode, ownership, timestamps and recurses.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["recurses","timestamps","archive","app.log","stat","ownership","mode"]},{"i":"qz068","t":"Files","q":"Show a one-line total size of /var/log with...","a":"du -sh /var/log. -s summarizes, -h humanizes.","e":"journalctl -f","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["humanizes","var/log","summarizes","journalctl"]},{"i":"qz069","t":"Files","q":"Which command counts newline characters in a file?","a":"wc -l file. wc -l counts newline characters, usually called lines. A final line without a newline is not added to this count.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/wc.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["newline","characters","counts","python3","line","final","called"]},{"i":"qz070","t":"Files","q":"Which tool shows line-by-line differences between two text files?","a":"diff. diff compares lines and prints changes; e.g. diff -u old.txt new.txt uses unified format. cmp checks bytes instead.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/diff.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["new.txt","cmp","old.txt","unified","compares","e.g","diff"]},{"i":"qz071","t":"Files","q":"Create directories logs, data and backup in one shot with...","a":"mkdir logs data backup. mkdir accepts multiple names in one command.","e":"mkdir -p projects/app","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/mkdir.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["projects/app","mkdir","backup","one","accepts","multiple","logs"]},{"i":"qz072","t":"Files","q":"Running 'cp report.txt ~/docs/' when docs exists will...","a":"Place report.txt inside ~/docs with the same name. When the destination is a directory, cp copies into it keeping the name.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ping.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["report.txt","docs","pwd","place","inside","copies","destination"]},{"i":"qz073","t":"Files","q":"When mv moves a regular file across filesystems, what does it usually do?","a":"Copies it then deletes the original. rename cannot cross filesystems, so GNU mv typically copies data and removes the original after a successful copy. This is not an atomic rename.","e":"file report","s":"https://www.gnu.org/gnu/gnu-history.html","l":"Beginner","k":["atomic","cross","rename","typically","deletes","cannot","removes"]},{"i":"qz074","t":"Files","q":"Which command shows full metadata of a file - inode, size, permissions, timestamps?","a":"stat. stat reports inode number, links, access/modify/change times and more.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["access/modify/change","times","inode","app.log","stat","number","links"]},{"i":"qz075","t":"Text","q":"Print an entire file to the terminal with...","a":"cat file. cat dumps the whole file at once - best for short files.","e":"file report","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/cat.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["dumps","short","report","best","once","whole","cat"]},{"i":"qz076","t":"Text","q":"The best command to page through a long file interactively is...","a":"less. less scrolls forward and backward without loading everything at once.","e":"file report","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["scrolls","backward","report","once","less","forward","loading"]},{"i":"qz077","t":"Text","q":"Show the first 10 lines of a file with...","a":"head file. head defaults to the first 10 lines.","e":"file report","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/head.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["lines","report","first","head","defaults"]},{"i":"qz078","t":"Text","q":"Show the last 10 lines of a file with...","a":"tail file. tail defaults to the last 10 lines - perfect for recent log entries.","e":"last","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tail.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["last","perfect","lines","recent","tail","entries","defaults"]},{"i":"qz079","t":"Text","q":"Watch a growing log file live with...","a":"tail -f app.log. tail -f follows the file and prints new lines as they arrive.","e":"watch -n 2 df -h","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tail.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["watch","arrive","tail","app.log","follows","prints","lines"]},{"i":"qz080","t":"Text","q":"Print the first 20 lines with...","a":"head -n 20 file. head -n 20 prints at most 20 lines from the start. On GNU head, older -20 syntax may also work, so it is not a useful wrong option.","e":"file report","s":"https://www.gnu.org/gnu/gnu-history.html","l":"Beginner","k":["lines","report","syntax","wrong","older","head","option"]},{"i":"qz081","t":"Text","q":"Count lines, words and bytes with...","a":"wc file. wc = word count; wc -l is the classic line counter.","e":"file report","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/wc.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["word","count","counter","line","report","classic"]},{"i":"qz082","t":"Text","q":"Search for the word 'error' inside a file with...","a":"grep error app.log. grep prints lines matching the pattern.","e":"file report","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["error","report","grep","app.log","pattern","matching","prints"]},{"i":"qz083","t":"Text","q":"grep -i does what?","a":"Case-insensitive search. -i matches ERROR, error, Error alike.","e":"grep -n error app.log","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["case-insensitive","alike","grep","error","app.log","search","matches"]},{"i":"qz084","t":"Text","q":"Search a pattern in every file under a directory with...","a":"grep -r pattern dir/. -r recurses through subdirectories.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["recurses","subdirectories","dir","pattern","pwd","grep"]},{"i":"qz085","t":"Text","q":"grep -v pattern shows...","a":"Lines that do NOT match. -v inverts: it prints the non-matching lines.","e":"grep -n error app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/grep.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["non-matching","inverts","grep","error","app.log","match","prints"]},{"i":"qz086","t":"Text","q":"Which grep option prints line numbers?","a":"-n. grep -n error app.log prefixes each hit with its line number.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/grep.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["prefixes","number","hit","grep","python3","line","app.log"]},{"i":"qz087","t":"Text","q":"The pipe symbol | does what?","a":"Sends one command's output into another's input. ps aux | grep nginx feeds ps output into grep.","e":"grep -n error app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/grep.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["grep","feeds","error","app.log","aux","input","nginx"]},{"i":"qz088","t":"Text","q":"Redirect output to a file, overwriting it, with...","a":"command > file. > truncates and writes; >> appends.","e":"command > out.txt 2>&1","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/cat.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["out.txt","truncates","appends","writes"]},{"i":"qz089","t":"Text","q":"Append output to an existing file with...","a":"command >> file. >> keeps old content and adds to the end.","e":"file report","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/grep.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["end","report","old","content","keeps","adds"]},{"i":"qz090","t":"Text","q":"Redirect only error messages (stderr) with...","a":"command 2> errors.txt. File descriptor 2 is stderr; 1 is stdout.","e":"command > out.txt 2>&1","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["stderr","out.txt","errors.txt","descriptor","stdout"]},{"i":"qz091","t":"Text","q":"In a POSIX-style shell, which form redirects both stdout and stderr to all.txt?","a":"command > all.txt 2>&1. The shell opens all.txt for stdout, then 2>&1 sends stderr to that same destination. Order matters: command 2>&1 > all.txt behaves differently.","e":"command > out.txt 2>&1","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/rm.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["all.txt","stderr","out.txt","behaves","differently","stdout","shell"]},{"i":"qz092","t":"Text","q":"Inside less, how do you search for text?","a":"Type / then the text. /pattern searches forward, ?pattern searches backward, n repeats.","e":"less /var/log/syslog","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["less","backward","var/log/syslog","text","repeats","pattern","forward"]},{"i":"qz093","t":"Text","q":"How do you quit less?","a":"Press q. q quits; Ctrl+C only interrupts operations.","e":"less /var/log/syslog","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["less","interrupts","var/log/syslog","ctrl","press","quits","operations"]},{"i":"qz094","t":"Text","q":"cat -n does what?","a":"Numbers all output lines. cat -n prefixes each line with its number.","e":"cat /etc/hosts","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/cat.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["etc/hosts","prefixes","cat","output","numbers","lines","line"]},{"i":"qz095","t":"Text","q":"Which sort option compares lines by numeric value rather than lexically?","a":"sort -n. sort -n puts 9 before 10 by comparing leading numbers. Plain sort compares text and may put 10 first.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/sort.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["compares","sort","puts","python3","leading","plain","text"]},{"i":"qz096","t":"Text","q":"uniq -c is usually combined with sort because...","a":"uniq only collapses adjacent duplicate lines. sort file | uniq -c counts how often each distinct line appears.","e":"file report","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/sort.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["collapses","duplicate","uniq","adjacent","sort","distinct","report"]},{"i":"qz097","t":"Text","q":"For a simple comma-delimited file with no quoted commas, how can you print field 2?","a":"cut -d, -f2 file. cut -d, -f2 uses comma as delimiter. Real CSV allows quoted commas and needs a CSV-aware parser.","e":"echo \"$HOME\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["csv-aware","commas","parser","csv","delimiter","quoted","home"]},{"i":"qz098","t":"Text","q":"Print just the first whitespace-separated column with...","a":"awk '{print $1}' file. awk splits on whitespace by default; $1 is the first field.","e":"file report","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/awk.1p.html","l":"Beginner","k":["whitespace","awk","print","report","first","splits","field"]},{"i":"qz099","t":"Text","q":"Add the line 'backup done' to the end of run.log without opening an editor...","a":"echo 'backup done' >> run.log. echo plus >> appends the line instantly.","e":"echo \"$NAME\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/journalctl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["run.log","done","appends","instantly","backup","line","echo"]},{"i":"qz100","t":"Permissions","q":"In rwx, the letters stand for...","a":"Read, Write, Execute. r=read, w=write, x=execute (or enter, for directories).","e":"cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["cut","etc/passwd","execute","enter","directories","write","read"]},{"i":"qz101","t":"Permissions","q":"Permission 755 means...","a":"rwxr-xr-x. 7=rwx for owner, 5=r-x for group and others.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/group.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["rwxr-xr-x","r-x","rwx","app.log","stat","owner","others"]},{"i":"qz102","t":"Permissions","q":"chmod 644 file.txt gives...","a":"rw-r--r--. 6=rw for owner, 4=r for group and others - typical for web files.","e":"chmod 644 app.conf","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["rw-r--r","app.conf","chmod","typical","web","owner","others"]},{"i":"qz103","t":"Permissions","q":"Which command adds execute permission to script.sh without replacing its existing read/write bits?","a":"chmod +x script.sh. chmod +x adds execute bits subject to umask; chmod 111 replaces other permissions. If you need only the owner, use chmod u+x.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html","l":"Scenario","k":["umask","replaces","execute","script.sh","bits","chmod","adds"]},{"i":"qz104","t":"Permissions","q":"Change owner and group of a file with...","a":"chown user:group file. chown sets owner; user:group sets both at once.","e":"sudo chown app:app app.conf","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chown.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["app.conf","chown","app","owner","group","once","sudo"]},{"i":"qz105","t":"Permissions","q":"User account information (name, UID, shell) is stored in...","a":"/etc/passwd. /etc/passwd is world-readable; no password hashes live there.","e":"passwd trainee","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["world-readable","hashes","etc/passwd","trainee","passwd","live","password"]},{"i":"qz106","t":"Permissions","q":"On a typical Linux system with shadow passwords, where are local password hashes kept?","a":"/etc/shadow. /etc/shadow stores local password hashes on systems using shadow passwords. Access is restricted; centralized logins may not store hashes locally.","e":"ls /etc","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["shadow","etc/shadow","hashes","centralized","locally","password","local"]},{"i":"qz107","t":"Permissions","q":"Group definitions live in...","a":"/etc/group. /etc/group lists group name, GID and members.","e":"ls /etc","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["etc/group","gid","members","group","lists","name"]},{"i":"qz108","t":"Permissions","q":"Which command prints your current username?","a":"whoami. whoami answers 'who am I' with your effective username.","e":"whoami","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["whoami","username","effective","answers"]},{"i":"qz109","t":"Permissions","q":"Show your UID, GID and all groups with...","a":"id. id prints uid, gid and every group you belong to.","e":"groups trainee","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/group.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["gid","uid","belong","trainee","groups","prints","every"]},{"i":"qz110","t":"Permissions","q":"On a configured sudo system, which command normally runs command as root?","a":"sudo command. sudo command runs as root by default when your sudo policy allows it. sudo -u otheruser selects another account.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/sudo.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["otheruser","python3","root","runs","sudo","policy","selects"]},{"i":"qz111","t":"Permissions","q":"Switch to another user account entirely with...","a":"su - username. su - opens a login shell as that user (their password needed).","e":"echo $SHELL","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["echo","shell","user","username","login","password","opens"]},{"i":"qz112","t":"Permissions","q":"Create a new user account with...","a":"useradd akash. useradd is the low-level tool; adduser is the friendlier Debian wrapper.","e":"sudo useradd -m trainee","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["low-level","useradd","wrapper","adduser","friendlier","trainee","akash"]},{"i":"qz113","t":"Permissions","q":"Set or change a user's password with...","a":"passwd username. passwd akash prompts for the new password interactively.","e":"passwd trainee","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/passwd.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["interactively","prompts","trainee","akash","passwd","password","username"]},{"i":"qz114","t":"Permissions","q":"Remove a user account with...","a":"userdel username. userdel deletes the account; -r also removes the home directory.","e":"cd ~","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["userdel","account","deletes","removes","username","home","directory"]},{"i":"qz115","t":"Permissions","q":"Which numeric UID has superuser privileges on Linux?","a":"0. UID 0 has superuser identity. The account named root normally has UID 0, but account names alone do not grant it.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["superuser","uid","python3","identity","grant","account","alone"]},{"i":"qz116","t":"Permissions","q":"For ordinary users other than root, permission 700 on a directory grants which access?","a":"Only the owner can enter, read or modify it. 700 = rwx for owner and --- for group/others. The owner can list, enter and modify the directory, subject to other filesystem constraints; root can bypass ordinary mode checks.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/group.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["group/others","bypass","ordinary","rwx","root","app.log","constraints"]},{"i":"qz117","t":"Permissions","q":"Ignoring root and ACLs, which class has the write bit in -rw-r--r--?","a":"Only the owner. The owner triplet rw- includes write. Group and other triplets r-- do not; root and ACLs can change effective access.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/getfacl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["triplet","acls","python3","write","effective","root","change"]},{"i":"qz118","t":"Permissions","q":"In ls -l output, a first character of 'd' means...","a":"Directory. d=directory, -=regular file, l=symlink.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/symlink.7.html","l":"Beginner","k":["symlink","pwd","regular","directory"]},{"i":"qz119","t":"Permissions","q":"A first character of 'l' in ls -l means...","a":"Symbolic link. l marks a symlink, shown as link -> target.","e":"ln -s /real/target linkname","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["real/target","linkname","symbolic","target","link","symlink","shown"]},{"i":"qz120","t":"Permissions","q":"Given a regular file created with default mode 666 and umask 022, which mode results?","a":"644. Creation applies mode & ~umask: 0666 & ~0022 = 0644. A program may request a more restrictive starting mode.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["umask","restrictive","creation","mode","python3","applies","request"]},{"i":"qz121","t":"Permissions","q":"Which action normally requires root?","a":"Changing another user's file ownership with chown. Only root can chown files to arbitrary users.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chown.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["chown","arbitrary","python3","root","ownership","changing","user"]},{"i":"qz122","t":"Permissions","q":"/etc/sudoers should be edited with...","a":"visudo. visudo syntax-checks before saving, so a typo can't lock you out.","e":"sudo visudo","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["visudo","syntax-checks","typo","sudo","lock","saving"]},{"i":"qz123","t":"Permissions","q":"Which command lists the groups you belong to?","a":"groups. groups prints your group memberships; id shows them with IDs.","e":"groups trainee","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/group.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["trainee","groups","memberships","ids","prints","shows"]},{"i":"qz124","t":"Permissions","q":"Add user akash to the sudo group on Debian/Ubuntu with...","a":"usermod -aG sudo akash. -aG appends a supplementary group; forgetting -a replaces all groups.","e":"sudo usermod -aG developers trainee","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["developers","supplementary","forgetting","usermod","akash","replaces","trainee"]},{"i":"qz125","t":"Processes","q":"Which command shows a snapshot of processes with user, CPU and memory columns?","a":"ps aux. ps aux shows a snapshot with USER, %CPU, %MEM and more. top is for a refreshing view.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ps.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["mem","snapshot","refreshing","cpu","aux","user","shows"]},{"i":"qz126","t":"Processes","q":"Which tool gives a live, refreshing view of processes?","a":"top. top (or htop) updates continuously; ps is a snapshot.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ps.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["continuously","htop","snapshot","aux","updates","top"]},{"i":"qz127","t":"Processes","q":"PID stands for...","a":"Process ID. Every process gets a unique numeric PID.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["numeric","pid","aux","unique","gets","every","process"]},{"i":"qz128","t":"Processes","q":"PPID is...","a":"The parent process's PID. Each process (except PID 1) was started by a parent, recorded as PPID.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ppid","pid","aux","except","was","recorded","parent"]},{"i":"qz129","t":"Processes","q":"On a Linux system using systemd as its init system, which program runs as PID 1?","a":"systemd. The kernel starts systemd as PID 1 on systemd-based systems. Other init systems or containers may use a different PID 1.","e":"systemctl list-units --type=service","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["systemd-based","init","list-units","systemd","pid","type","systemctl"]},{"i":"qz130","t":"Processes","q":"The default signal sent by plain 'kill PID' is...","a":"SIGTERM (15). SIGTERM politely asks the process to shut down and clean up.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/kill.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["politely","sigterm","shut","aux","clean","asks","process"]},{"i":"qz131","t":"Processes","q":"Which signal does kill -9 PID send?","a":"Force-kills the process; it cannot be caught or ignored. kill -9 sends SIGKILL; a program cannot catch or ignore it. An uninterruptible kernel wait may delay its exit.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/kill.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["force-kills","uninterruptible","sigkill","ignored","kill","ignore","cannot"]},{"i":"qz132","t":"Processes","q":"Kill processes by name instead of PID with...","a":"pkill nginx. pkill matches process names; kill needs a numeric PID.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/kill.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["pkill","numeric","kill","pid","aux","process","nginx"]},{"i":"qz133","t":"Processes","q":"On a typical Linux system, which command signals every process whose executable name is exactly nginx?","a":"killall nginx. killall nginx matches processes by executable name. pkill -x nginx also does this, so the offered pkill option must not be a second correct answer.","e":"killall nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/kill.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["killall","executable","pkill","second","offered","nginx","answer"]},{"i":"qz134","t":"Processes","q":"On Linux, the user-visible nice values normally range from...","a":"-20 (highest priority) to 19 (lowest). Linux nice values run from -20 (higher scheduling priority) to 19 (lower). CPU time still depends on scheduler and other processes.","e":"crontab -l","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/nice.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["nice","scheduler","values","crontab","higher","scheduling","highest"]},{"i":"qz135","t":"Processes","q":"Change the priority of an already-running process with...","a":"renice. renice -n 5 -p 1234 adjusts PID 1234 on the fly.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/nice.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["renice","adjusts","fly","aux","pid"]},{"i":"qz136","t":"Processes","q":"Run a command in the background by ending it with...","a":"&. 'longjob &' starts it in the background of your shell.","e":"echo $SHELL","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["longjob","background","echo","shell","starts"]},{"i":"qz137","t":"Processes","q":"List this shell's background jobs with...","a":"jobs. jobs shows job numbers like [1] Running for the current shell.","e":"echo $SHELL","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["jobs","shell","echo","numbers","running","current","shows"]},{"i":"qz138","t":"Processes","q":"Bring job 1 back to the foreground with...","a":"fg %1. fg %1 resumes job 1 attached to your terminal.","e":"rm -i old.txt","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ps.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["old.txt","resumes","job","attached","terminal"]},{"i":"qz139","t":"Processes","q":"Resume a stopped job in the background with...","a":"bg. bg %1 continues a Ctrl+Z'd job without the terminal.","e":"top","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ps.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["top","job","ctrl","continues","terminal"]},{"i":"qz140","t":"Processes","q":"Ctrl+C sends which signal?","a":"SIGINT. SIGINT interrupts; programs may catch it to clean up.","e":"kill -l","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/signal.7.html","l":"Beginner","k":["sigint","interrupts","kill","catch","clean","programs"]},{"i":"qz141","t":"Processes","q":"Ctrl+Z does what to the foreground process?","a":"Suspends (stops) it and returns your prompt. Ctrl+Z sends SIGTSTP; resume with fg or bg.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ps.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["sigtstp","suspends","ctrl","prompt","resume","aux","returns"]},{"i":"qz142","t":"Processes","q":"Which option helps a shell-launched process ignore SIGHUP after logout?","a":"nohup command &. nohup ignores SIGHUP on logout. The final & backgrounds the command, while a service manager is better for long-running services.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["long-running","sighup","logout","nohup","ignores","better","aux"]},{"i":"qz143","t":"Processes","q":"In ps aux, the 'a' option means...","a":"Show processes of all users with a terminal. a lifts the 'only your processes' restriction (for tty processes).","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ps.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["restriction","tty","aux","lifts","terminal","processes","all"]},{"i":"qz144","t":"Processes","q":"A zombie process is...","a":"A dead child whose exit status the parent has not collected. Zombies hold only an exit-code entry; killing them does nothing.","e":"echo $?","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/kill.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["exit-code","zombies","dead","nothing","echo","child","collected"]},{"i":"qz145","t":"Processes","q":"What generally adopts an orphaned process when its parent exits on modern Linux?","a":"PID 1 or a designated subreaper. Orphans are reparented to the nearest living child subreaper, if one exists, otherwise PID 1. It is not always directly PID 1.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["subreaper","reparented","orphans","designated","nearest","otherwise","aux"]},{"i":"qz146","t":"Processes","q":"The three load averages displayed by uptime normally summarize which time windows?","a":"1, 5 and 15 minutes. The windows are 1, 5 and 15 minutes. On Linux the load includes runnable and uninterruptible tasks, not just CPU use.","e":"uptime","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ps.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["uptime","runnable","windows","uninterruptible","load","minutes","tasks"]},{"i":"qz147","t":"Processes","q":"Check RAM and swap usage in human-readable form with...","a":"free -h. free -h shows total, used, free memory plus swap.","e":"free -h","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/free.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["swap","free","total","plus","memory","shows"]},{"i":"qz148","t":"Processes","q":"List all signal names and numbers with...","a":"kill -l. kill -l prints the table: 1 HUP, 2 INT, 9 KILL, 15 TERM...","e":"kill -l","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/kill.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["hup","kill","int","term","table","prints"]},{"i":"qz149","t":"Processes","q":"Live details about process 1234 (memory maps, fds, status) are under...","a":"/proc/1234. The kernel exposes every process as a directory under /proc/<pid>.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/proc.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["proc/1234","proc","exposes","pwd","process","pid","every"]},{"i":"qz150","t":"Packages","q":"The package manager on Debian and Ubuntu is...","a":"apt. apt installs .deb packages on Debian-family systems.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["debian-family","deb","etc/os-release","cat","apt","packages","installs"]},{"i":"qz151","t":"Packages","q":"Refresh the list of available packages with...","a":"sudo apt update. update downloads package indexes; it does not upgrade anything yet.","e":"sudo apt update","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["update","package","apt","upgrade","downloads","anything","indexes"]},{"i":"qz152","t":"Packages","q":"On Debian/Ubuntu, which command upgrades installed packages without normally removing installed packages?","a":"sudo apt upgrade. apt upgrade installs available upgrades but normally avoids removing installed packages; full-upgrade may remove them. Run apt update first to refresh the index.","e":"sudo apt update","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["full-upgrade","upgrade","upgrades","refresh","update","removing","packages"]},{"i":"qz153","t":"Packages","q":"Install the nginx package with...","a":"sudo apt install nginx. apt install resolves dependencies and installs them automatically.","e":"sudo -u appuser whoami","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["appuser","whoami","resolves","install","dependencies","nginx","apt"]},{"i":"qz154","t":"Packages","q":"Remove a package but keep its config files with...","a":"sudo apt remove pkg. remove deletes binaries; configs in /etc stay for a later reinstall.","e":"file report","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["reinstall","binaries","pkg","remove","deletes","report","later"]},{"i":"qz155","t":"Packages","q":"Remove a package AND its configuration files with...","a":"sudo apt purge pkg. purge = remove + config cleanup.","e":"file report","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["config","purge","pkg","cleanup","remove","report","apt"]},{"i":"qz156","t":"Packages","q":"Search available packages for 'editor' with...","a":"apt search editor. apt search scans names and descriptions of all known packages.","e":"bash -x script.sh","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["editor","script.sh","search","packages","scans","descriptions","bash"]},{"i":"qz157","t":"Packages","q":"Show version, size and description of a package with...","a":"apt show pkg. apt show displays the package's full metadata.","e":"bash -x script.sh","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["pkg","script.sh","displays","metadata","bash","package","apt"]},{"i":"qz158","t":"Packages","q":"Install a downloaded .deb file with...","a":"sudo dpkg -i file.deb. dpkg -i installs a local .deb without resolving dependencies by itself. apt install ./file.deb is often easier when dependencies matter.","e":"file report","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["file.deb","deb","dpkg","resolving","easier","install","report"]},{"i":"qz159","t":"Packages","q":"Which command lists installed Debian packages and their status?","a":"dpkg -l. dpkg -l shows package status and versions; it may also show removed packages with residual configs, so check the status column for ii.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["residual","packages","dpkg","etc/os-release","package","status","configs"]},{"i":"qz160","t":"Packages","q":"Which current command-line package manager is standard on modern Fedora and RHEL 8+?","a":"dnf. dnf is standard in modern Fedora/RHEL. The older yum command may remain as a compatibility entry point.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html/managing_software_with_the_dnf_tool/assembly_yum-commands-list_managing-software-with-the-dnf-tool","l":"Beginner","k":["fedora/rhel","yum","standard","modern","dnf","etc/os-release","compatibility"]},{"i":"qz161","t":"Packages","q":"Install httpd on a Fedora/RHEL system with...","a":"sudo dnf install httpd. dnf install handles repos and dependencies like apt does.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["httpd","repos","dnf","handles","etc/os-release","install","dependencies"]},{"i":"qz162","t":"Packages","q":"Which command installs a local RPM file with rpm (without automatic dependency resolution)?","a":"sudo rpm -ivh file.rpm. rpm -ivh installs with verbose output and progress. dnf install ./file.rpm is generally preferable when dependencies must be resolved.","e":"which python3","s":"https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html/managing_software_with_the_dnf_tool/assembly_yum-commands-list_managing-software-with-the-dnf-tool","l":"Beginner","k":["file.rpm","preferable","verbose","ivh","rpm","dnf","python3"]},{"i":"qz163","t":"Packages","q":"List every installed RPM package with...","a":"rpm -qa. -q query, -a all: rpm -qa | grep nginx.","e":"grep -n error app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/rpm.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["rpm","grep","error","all","app.log","query","nginx"]},{"i":"qz164","t":"Packages","q":"Find which installed package owns /usr/bin/curl on Debian with...","a":"dpkg -S /usr/bin/curl. dpkg -S searches by file path; rpm -qf is the RHEL equivalent.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["usr/bin/curl","dpkg","etc/os-release","rhel","equivalent","cat","rpm"]},{"i":"qz165","t":"Packages","q":"Arch Linux uses which package manager?","a":"pacman. pacman is Arch's fast, simple package manager.","e":"sudo pacman -Syu","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["pacman","syu","arch","manager","simple","package","fast"]},{"i":"qz166","t":"Packages","q":"Fully sync and upgrade an Arch system with...","a":"sudo pacman -Syu. -Syu syncs databases and upgrades everything - the Arch ritual.","e":"sudo pacman -Syu","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["pacman","syu","ritual","arch","upgrades","syncs","sudo"]},{"i":"qz167","t":"System","q":"Which company develops and maintains the snap packaging system?","a":"Canonical. Canonical develops snapd and the Snap Store. Remember Canonical -> Ubuntu -> snap.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["develops","snap","snapd","canonical","ubuntu","remember","etc/os-release"]},{"i":"qz168","t":"System","q":"By default, Flatpak applications are designed to run...","a":"Sandboxed, isolated from most of the system. Flatpak apps use sandbox permissions; access can be granted or overridden, so do not assume complete isolation.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["flatpak","overridden","isolation","sandboxed","isolated","app.log","stat"]},{"i":"qz169","t":"Packages","q":"A package dependency is...","a":"Another package or library the software needs to run. apt/dnf resolve dependencies automatically; dpkg alone does not.","e":"sudo dpkg -i file.deb","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["apt/dnf","file.deb","dpkg","library","package","dependencies","software"]},{"i":"qz170","t":"Packages","q":"A repository (repo) in package management is...","a":"A server hosting packages and their metadata. Repos are what apt update downloads its package lists from.","e":"cat /etc/apt/sources.list","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["repos","etc/apt/sources.list","metadata","package","apt","downloads","cat"]},{"i":"qz171","t":"Packages","q":"Remove packages that were installed as dependencies but are no longer needed...","a":"sudo apt autoremove. autoremove clears orphaned dependencies; clean clears the download cache.","e":"clear","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["clear","autoremove","orphaned","dependencies","download","cache","apt"]},{"i":"qz172","t":"Packages","q":"Where can APT repository definitions be placed on Debian/Ubuntu?","a":"/etc/apt/sources.list and /etc/apt/sources.list.d/. APT reads /etc/apt/sources.list and files under /etc/apt/sources.list.d/; these may be .list or modern .sources files.","e":"cat /etc/apt/sources.list","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["etc/apt/sources.list.d","etc/apt/sources.list","sources","apt","list","cat","modern"]},{"i":"qz173","t":"Packages","q":"Fix broken/unmet dependencies after a failed install with...","a":"sudo apt --fix-broken install. apt -f install pulls in or removes whatever resolves the breakage.","e":"sudo -u appuser whoami","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["fix-broken","appuser","breakage","whoami","whatever","resolves","removes"]},{"i":"qz174","t":"Packages","q":"Why prefer the package manager over compiling from source for most installs?","a":"It handles dependencies, updates and clean removal. Package managers track files so upgrades and uninstalls stay clean.","e":"sudo apt update","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ls.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["uninstalls","upgrades","update","removal","handles","package","apt"]},{"i":"qz175","t":"Networking","q":"Show all IP addresses on the machine with...","a":"ip addr. ip a lists every interface with its addresses.","e":"ip addr show","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-address.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["addr","addresses","show","interface","every","lists"]},{"i":"qz176","t":"Networking","q":"For managing network interface addresses, the ip command is the modern replacement for...","a":"ifconfig. ip addr replaces many ifconfig uses. ip route also replaces older route uses; the question asks specifically about addresses.","e":"ifconfig -a","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-route.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ifconfig","addr","replaces","addresses","specifically","question","older"]},{"i":"qz177","t":"Networking","q":"Show the routing table with...","a":"ip route. ip route shows the default gateway and per-network routes.","e":"ip route","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-route.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["per-network","route","routes","gateway","default","shows"]},{"i":"qz178","t":"Networking","q":"The default gateway is...","a":"The router your machine sends outside traffic to. Packets for unknown networks go to the default gateway.","e":"ip route","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["gateway","route","default","outside","unknown","router","packets"]},{"i":"qz179","t":"Networking","q":"Test basic connectivity to 8.8.8.8 with...","a":"ping 8.8.8.8. ping sends ICMP echo requests and measures round-trip time.","e":"ping -c 4 8.8.8.8","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["round-trip","icmp","ping","measures","requests","sends","time"]},{"i":"qz180","t":"Networking","q":"DNS does what?","a":"Resolves names like google.com to IP addresses. DNS is the internet's phone book.","e":"cat /etc/resolv.conf","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-address.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["etc/resolv.conf","google.com","phone","book","dns","resolves","internet"]},{"i":"qz181","t":"Networking","q":"Which file traditionally lists resolver nameservers, possibly as a local stub?","a":"/etc/resolv.conf. /etc/resolv.conf lists resolver nameservers, though systemd-resolved or NetworkManager often manages it and may point to a local stub.","e":"systemctl list-units --type=service","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["nameservers","stub","systemd-resolved","resolver","etc/resolv.conf","list-units","networkmanager"]},{"i":"qz182","t":"Networking","q":"Static hostname-to-IP mappings live in...","a":"/etc/hosts. /etc/hosts holds local hostname mappings. The lookup order with DNS comes from /etc/nsswitch.conf.","e":"hostnamectl","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["etc/nsswitch.conf","hostname","mappings","etc/hosts","hostnamectl","dns","comes"]},{"i":"qz183","t":"Networking","q":"Which modern command lists listening TCP and UDP sockets numerically?","a":"ss -tuln. ss -tuln: -t TCP, -u UDP, -l listening, -n numeric. UDP has no LISTEN state in the TCP sense, but the option selects bound sockets.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["numeric","udp","sockets","tcp","listening","tuln","sense"]},{"i":"qz184","t":"Networking","q":"Which command writes the body of https://example.com to stdout by default?","a":"curl https://example.com. curl URL prints the response body to stdout by default; use -o file or -O to save it. wget defaults to saving a file.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/curl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["body","stdout","wget","https","example.com","curl","python3"]},{"i":"qz185","t":"Networking","q":"Download a file to disk non-interactively with...","a":"wget URL. wget saves the file locally; curl -O does the same.","e":"file report","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/curl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["wget","locally","saves","curl","report","url"]},{"i":"qz186","t":"Networking","q":"Securely log into a remote Linux machine with...","a":"ssh user@host. SSH encrypts the whole session; telnet sends plaintext.","e":"host example.com","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["plaintext","telnet","example.com","encrypts","host","ssh","session"]},{"i":"qz187","t":"Networking","q":"SSH listens on which port by default?","a":"22. Port 22 for SSH; 21 FTP, 23 telnet.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["telnet","python3","ssh","port","ftp"]},{"i":"qz188","t":"Networking","q":"HTTPS uses which port?","a":"443. 443 is HTTPS; 80 is plain HTTP.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["https","python3","plain"]},{"i":"qz189","t":"Networking","q":"Which is the standard port number for DNS service?","a":"53. DNS commonly uses port 53 over UDP or TCP. DNS-over-TLS and DNS-over-HTTPS use other ports/protocols.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://bind9.readthedocs.io/en/stable/manpages.html","l":"Beginner","k":["dns-over-tls","dns-over-https","ports/protocols","udp","dns","port","status"]},{"i":"qz190","t":"Networking","q":"Trace the path packets take to a host with...","a":"traceroute host. traceroute (or tracepath/mtr) shows each hop.","e":"traceroute example.com","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["tracepath/mtr","traceroute","example.com","host","hop","shows"]},{"i":"qz191","t":"Networking","q":"Show or set the machine's name with...","a":"hostname. hostname prints it; hostnamectl sets it persistently.","e":"hostnamectl","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["persistently","hostname","hostnamectl","sets","prints"]},{"i":"qz192","t":"Networking","q":"127.0.0.1 is...","a":"The loopback address (this machine itself). localhost always points back at your own machine.","e":"ping 127.0.0.1","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["localhost","loopback","ping","itself","back","machine","points"]},{"i":"qz193","t":"Networking","q":"Which option is an RFC 1918 private IPv4 address block?","a":"192.168.0.0/16. RFC 1918 defines 192.168.0.0/16 as private. The 100.64.0.0/10 block is shared carrier-grade address space, a different category.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["carrier-grade","rfc","defines","private","category","address","block"]},{"i":"qz194","t":"Networking","q":"Which dotted-decimal subnet mask corresponds to an IPv4 /24 prefix?","a":"255.255.255.0. /24 has 24 leading 1 bits, giving 255.255.255.0. A /24 has 256 total IPv4 addresses; not every network has exactly 254 usable hosts.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ipv4","usable","python3","exactly","total","leading","bits"]},{"i":"qz195","t":"Networking","q":"Manage NetworkManager connections from the CLI with...","a":"nmcli. nmcli device status / nmcli connection show are daily tools on RHEL-family.","e":"nmcli device status","s":"https://networkmanager.dev/docs/api/latest/nmcli.html","l":"Beginner","k":["rhel-family","nmcli","connection","device","status","daily","tools"]},{"i":"qz196","t":"Networking","q":"The machine's hostname is stored persistently in...","a":"/etc/hostname. /etc/hostname is read at boot; hostnamectl writes it.","e":"hostnamectl","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["etc/hostname","hostnamectl","writes","boot","read"]},{"i":"qz197","t":"Networking","q":"Query DNS records in detail with...","a":"dig example.com. dig (or nslookup/host) queries A, MX, TXT and other records.","e":"nslookup example.com","s":"https://bind9.readthedocs.io/en/stable/manpages.html","l":"Beginner","k":["nslookup/host","nslookup","txt","records","example.com","dig","queries"]},{"i":"qz198","t":"Networking","q":"On Ubuntu installations where it is installed, which tool is a simple firewall frontend?","a":"ufw. ufw stands for Uncomplicated Firewall. It is a frontend, not the underlying Linux firewall engine.","e":"sudo firewall-cmd --list-all","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["frontend","uncomplicated","list-all","ufw","firewall-cmd","firewall","underlying"]},{"i":"qz199","t":"Networking","q":"Copy a file securely to another host over SSH with...","a":"scp file user@host:/path. scp (or rsync over ssh) copies through the encrypted SSH channel.","e":"rsync -av /data/ user@host:/backup/","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["scp","rsync","encrypted","host","backup","ssh","channel"]},{"i":"qz200","t":"Storage","q":"Show free space on all mounted filesystems, human-readable...","a":"df -h. df = disk free, per filesystem; -h for G/M units.","e":"sudo mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt/data","s":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.html","l":"Beginner","k":["g/m","dev/sdb1","mnt/data","mount","free","filesystem","sudo"]},{"i":"qz201","t":"Storage","q":"Show the total size of /var/log with...","a":"du -sh /var/log. du -sh summarizes usage; df shows the whole filesystem.","e":"file report","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["var/log","summarizes","report","filesystem","whole","usage","shows"]},{"i":"qz202","t":"Storage","q":"List block devices (disks and partitions) as a tree with...","a":"lsblk. lsblk shows sda -> sda1/sda2 hierarchy with sizes and mountpoints.","e":"lsblk -f","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["sda1/sda2","sda","lsblk","mountpoints","hierarchy","sizes","shows"]},{"i":"qz203","t":"Storage","q":"See the partition table of /dev/sda with...","a":"sudo fdisk -l /dev/sda. fdisk -l (or parted -l) prints partition layout.","e":"sudo parted -l","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/sudo.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["dev/sda","parted","partition","fdisk","layout","sudo","prints"]},{"i":"qz204","t":"Storage","q":"Create an ext4 filesystem on /dev/sdb1 with...","a":"sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/sdb1. mkfs formats - it destroys existing data on the partition.","e":"file report","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/sudo.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["dev/sdb1","mkfs.ext4","mkfs","destroys","partition","report","formats"]},{"i":"qz205","t":"Storage","q":"Attach /dev/sdb1 at /mnt/data with...","a":"sudo mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt/data. mount grafts the filesystem into the directory tree.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/sudo.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["grafts","dev/sdb1","mnt/data","pwd","tree","filesystem","sudo"]},{"i":"qz206","t":"Storage","q":"Detach a mounted filesystem with...","a":"umount /mnt/data. It's umount (no first 'n'). It fails while files are open.","e":"sudo umount /mnt/data","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/mount.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["umount","mnt/data","sudo","fails","open","first"]},{"i":"qz207","t":"Storage","q":"Filesystems mounted automatically at boot are listed in...","a":"/etc/fstab. fstab = filesystem table: device, mountpoint, type, options.","e":"sudo mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt/data","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["etc/fstab","mountpoint","dev/sdb1","mnt/data","fstab","mount","filesystem"]},{"i":"qz208","t":"Storage","q":"Find a partition's UUID with...","a":"blkid. blkid prints UUIDs and filesystem types for all block devices.","e":"sudo blkid","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/find.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["blkid","uuids","types","sudo","filesystem","devices","block"]},{"i":"qz209","t":"Storage","q":"Why mount by UUID in fstab instead of /dev/sdb1?","a":"Device letters can change between boots. /dev/sdX naming depends on detection order; UUIDs are stable.","e":"sudo mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt/data","s":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.html","l":"Beginner","k":["dev/sdx","dev/sdb1","mnt/data","uuids","mount","letters","detection"]},{"i":"qz210","t":"Storage","q":"Which description best fits swap space on Linux?","a":"Storage used for swapped memory pages when the kernel chooses to move them out of RAM. Swap holds memory pages moved out of RAM by the kernel, which can happen before RAM is completely full.","e":"bash -x script.sh","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/swapon.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["swapped","script.sh","chooses","swap","completely","happen","bash"]},{"i":"qz211","t":"Storage","q":"Enable a swap partition with...","a":"swapon /dev/sdb2. mkswap formats it first, then swapon activates it.","e":"sudo swapon /dev/sdb2","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/swapon.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["dev/sdb2","swapon","mkswap","activates","formats","sudo","first"]},{"i":"qz212","t":"Storage","q":"What is a typical benefit of Linux Logical Volume Manager (LVM)?","a":"Flexible volumes you can resize and span across disks. LVM pools physical storage into volume groups and gives flexible logical volumes. Filesystem support and free space determine whether online growth or shrinking works.","e":"groups trainee","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/group.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["span","resize","lvm","volume","volumes","shrinking","logical"]},{"i":"qz213","t":"Storage","q":"The LVM stacking order is...","a":"Physical volumes -> volume group -> logical volumes. Disks become PVs, PVs pool into a VG, LVs are carved from the VG.","e":"sudo lvs","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/group.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["lvs","pvs","carved","volumes","become","pool","volume"]},{"i":"qz214","t":"Storage","q":"Initialize /dev/sdb as an LVM physical volume with...","a":"pvcreate /dev/sdb. pvcreate labels the disk as LVM physical volume.","e":"sudo pvcreate /dev/sdb","s":"https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html-single/configuring_and_managing_logical_volumes/index","l":"Beginner","k":["dev/sdb","pvcreate","lvm","volume","physical","labels","sudo"]},{"i":"qz215","t":"Storage","q":"Create volume group 'data' from /dev/sdb with...","a":"vgcreate data /dev/sdb. vgcreate names the pool and assigns PVs to it.","e":"sudo vgcreate data /dev/sdb","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/group.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["dev/sdb","vgcreate","pvs","assigns","pool","sudo","names"]},{"i":"qz216","t":"Storage","q":"Create a 20G logical volume 'web' in VG 'data' with...","a":"lvcreate -L 20G -n web data. lvcreate -L size -n name vgname, then mkfs the new /dev/data/web.","e":"sudo lvcreate -L 20G -n web data","s":"https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html-single/configuring_and_managing_logical_volumes/index","l":"Beginner","k":["lvcreate","dev/data/web","vgname","web","mkfs","sudo","size"]},{"i":"qz217","t":"Storage","q":"On an ext4 filesystem with enough free space in VG data, which command sequence grows web LV by 10G and then grows its filesystem?","a":"lvextend -L +10G /dev/data/web && resize2fs /dev/data/web. lvextend grows the logical volume; resize2fs grows ext4 afterward. XFS uses xfs_growfs instead. Verify the device and backups before changing storage.","e":"sudo resize2fs /dev/data/web","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/free.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["resize2fs","dev/data/web","xfs_growfs","grows","lvextend","afterward","ext4"]},{"i":"qz218","t":"Storage","q":"Which pair is a common default for Ubuntu/Debian and RHEL Linux installations respectively?","a":"ext4 and XFS. Ubuntu/Debian commonly default to ext4; RHEL commonly uses XFS. They are not necessarily the two most common on every Linux system.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ubuntu/debian","ext4","rhel","xfs","necessarily","etc/os-release","default"]},{"i":"qz219","t":"Storage","q":"An inode stores...","a":"File metadata (permissions, blocks, times) - not the filename. Names live in directory entries pointing at inode numbers.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["inode","filename","metadata","app.log","stat","times","entries"]},{"i":"qz220","t":"Storage","q":"Two hard links to the same file share...","a":"The same inode. ls -li shows identical inode numbers for hard-linked names.","e":"ln file.txt file.hard","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/inode.7.html","l":"Beginner","k":["hard-linked","identical","file.hard","file.txt","inode","numbers","names"]},{"i":"qz221","t":"Storage","q":"Which tool checks and can repair an unmounted filesystem using its appropriate filesystem-specific checker?","a":"fsck (on an unmounted filesystem). fsck dispatches to filesystem-specific checkers; use the right tool for the filesystem and do not repair a mounted writable filesystem. XFS repair is handled separately by xfs_repair.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/mount.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["filesystem-specific","unmounted","dispatches","checkers","writable","repair","xfs_repair"]},{"i":"qz222","t":"Storage","q":"Which command copies raw bytes from /dev/sda to disk.img (assuming enough destination space)?","a":"dd if=/dev/sda of=disk.img bs=4M status=progress. dd copies raw data from if= to of=. It can read an entire disk image; check source, destination and permissions carefully to avoid data loss.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["dev/sda","disk.img","raw","copies","destination","carefully","entire"]},{"i":"qz223","t":"Storage","q":"In a healthy two-disk RAID 1 mirror, what happens if one disk fails?","a":"Mirroring - same data on two disks. RAID 1 mirrors data. A healthy two-disk mirror generally keeps running after one disk fails, but RAID is no backup against deletion or corruption.","e":"cat /proc/mdstat","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/lsblk.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["two-disk","healthy","mirror","proc/mdstat","two","raid","corruption"]},{"i":"qz224","t":"Storage","q":"Which option can interactively explore directory disk usage when ncdu is installed?","a":"ncdu /. ncdu lets you navigate disk usage interactively. du piped to sort is a noninteractive alternative; they should not share one answer option.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/du.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["noninteractive","ncdu","interactively","piped","option","usage","navigate"]},{"i":"qz225","t":"Shell","q":"Which first line selects /bin/bash when you execute a script directly on a system where bash is there?","a":"#!/bin/bash. #!/bin/bash is a shebang that selects that interpreter. Scripts do not universally require it; bash script.sh works without an executable bit.","e":"head -1 script.sh","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["bin/bash","shebang","universally","executable","head","script.sh","interpreter"]},{"i":"qz226","t":"Shell","q":"Which command adds execute permission to backup.sh without running it?","a":"chmod +x backup.sh. chmod +x adds the execute bit, subject to umask. Running via bash backup.sh does not require the execute bit.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["backup.sh","umask","execute","chmod","running","adds","app.log"]},{"i":"qz227","t":"Shell","q":"Assuming script.sh is executable with a valid interpreter, how do you run it from the current directory?","a":"./script.sh. ./ explicitly names the current directory. Most shells do not search . via PATH by default.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["explicitly","script.sh","current","pwd","directory","search","default"]},{"i":"qz228","t":"Shell","q":"Correct variable assignment in bash...","a":"NAME=akash. No spaces around = ; the $ appears only when reading.","e":"echo \"$HOME\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["akash","home","echo","appears","spaces","reading","name"]},{"i":"qz229","t":"Shell","q":"Print the value of variable NAME with...","a":"echo \"$NAME\". $NAME expands the variable; quotes preserve spaces.","e":"echo \"$HOME\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["variable","preserve","home","echo","quotes","name","expands"]},{"i":"qz230","t":"Shell","q":"Store a command's output in a variable with...","a":"NOW=$(date). $(command) is command substitution; backticks are the old form.","e":"echo \"$HOME\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["substitution","backticks","home","date","echo","old","form"]},{"i":"qz231","t":"Shell","q":"Read keyboard input into a variable with...","a":"read name. read name pauses and stores the typed line in $name.","e":"echo \"$HOME\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["pauses","typed","home","echo","read","stores","line"]},{"i":"qz232","t":"Shell","q":"Inside a script, the first argument is...","a":"$1. $1, $2... are positional parameters; $0 is the script name.","e":"echo \"$1\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["positional","script","echo","parameters","name"]},{"i":"qz233","t":"Shell","q":"$# contains...","a":"The number of arguments passed. $# counts positional parameters - handy for usage checks.","e":"echo \"$1\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/sed.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["handy","positional","passed","echo","arguments","parameters","counts"]},{"i":"qz234","t":"Shell","q":"In bash, what does quoted \"$@\" represent?","a":"All arguments as separate words. Quote \"$@\" to keep each argument separate, including names with spaces; without quotes bash splits arguments further.","e":"echo \"$1\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["quote","further","bash","echo","splits","arguments","quotes"]},{"i":"qz235","t":"Shell","q":"Immediately after a command completes in bash, what does $? contain?","a":"The exit status of the last command. $? is the exit status of the most recently completed command; reading it after another command gives that newer status.","e":"echo $?","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/stat.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["newer","recently","completed","echo","exit","last","gives"]},{"i":"qz236","t":"Shell","q":"A command that succeeds exits with code...","a":"0. Zero = success is the Unix convention.","e":"echo backup.sh | at 23:00","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["backup.sh","convention","zero","success","echo","unix"]},{"i":"qz237","t":"Shell","q":"A correct if-statement skeleton is...","a":"if [ \"$a\" = \"1\" ]; then ... fi. if ...; then ...; fi - and mind the spaces inside [ ].","e":"stat app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/stat.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["stat","mind","app.log","spaces","inside"]},{"i":"qz238","t":"Shell","q":"Compare two strings for equality with...","a":"[ \"$a\" = \"$b\" ]. = compares strings; -eq is only for integers.","e":"cat f | tr a-z A-Z","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["a-z","strings","compares","integers","cat"]},{"i":"qz239","t":"Shell","q":"Which test checks that a regular file exists?","a":"[ -f /path ]. -f file exists and is regular; -d tests directories; -x tests executable.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["regular","executable","exists","python3","directories","path","tests"]},{"i":"qz240","t":"Shell","q":"A for loop over servers looks like...","a":"for s in web1 web2; do echo $s; done. for name in list; do ...; done - the list can also come from $(...).","e":"echo \"$NAME\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["web1","web2","echo","name","come","done","list"]},{"i":"qz241","t":"Shell","q":"A while loop needs which keywords?","a":"while ...; do ...; done. while condition; do body; done - e.g. while read -r line.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["e.g","python3","body","condition","done","line","read"]},{"i":"qz242","t":"Shell","q":"Best structure for many pattern branches (start|stop|restart)...","a":"case $1 in ... esac. case matches patterns like start) ... ;; stop) ... ;; esac.","e":"top","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["esac","top","stop","case","start","patterns","matches"]},{"i":"qz243","t":"Shell","q":"Define a function in bash with...","a":"backup() { tar czf /tmp/b.tgz /data; }. name() { commands; } then call it by name.","e":"myfunc() { echo hi; }","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["tmp/b.tgz","myfunc","czf","tar","echo","call","backup"]},{"i":"qz244","t":"Shell","q":"Comments in bash start with...","a":"#. Everything after # on a line is ignored (except the shebang).","e":"head -1 script.sh","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["shebang","head","script.sh","ignored","except","everything","line"]},{"i":"qz245","t":"Shell","q":"In bash, what does set -e usually do for an unhandled failing simple command?","a":"Exit the shell, with exceptions in conditions and command chains. set -e exits on many unhandled failures, but not in several if/while tests, !, &&/|| chains and other contexts. Test error handling explicitly.","e":"set -euo pipefail","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["if/while","unhandled","euo","pipefail","set","explicitly","failures"]},{"i":"qz246","t":"Shell","q":"The classic way to process a file line by line is...","a":"while read -r line; do ...; done < file. while read handles spaces safely; for over $(cat) splits on whitespace.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["safely","whitespace","handles","line","aux","splits","done"]},{"i":"qz247","t":"Shell","q":"echo \"$HOME\" vs echo '$HOME' differ because...","a":"Double quotes expand variables; single quotes keep text literal. Single quotes protect everything; double quotes allow $ expansion.","e":"echo \"$HOME\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/diff.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["literal","home","echo","double","expansion","quotes","protect"]},{"i":"qz248","t":"Shell","q":"PATH contains...","a":"Directories the shell searches for commands. When you type ls, the shell checks each PATH directory in order.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["pwd","path","order","searches","directories","type","shell"]},{"i":"qz249","t":"Shell","q":"Debug a script by printing each command before running it with...","a":"bash -x script.sh. -x (xtrace) traces execution - the first debugging tool to reach for.","e":"bash -x script.sh","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["xtrace","traces","debugging","script.sh","execution","bash","reach"]},{"i":"qz250","t":"Services","q":"On a systemd-based Linux system, which program normally runs as PID 1?","a":"systemd. On a systemd-based installation, systemd is PID 1 and manages units. Other Linux init systems exist.","e":"systemctl list-units --type=service","s":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.html","l":"Beginner","k":["systemd-based","list-units","init","systemd","pid","units","type"]},{"i":"qz251","t":"Boot","q":"BIOS/UEFI's role at boot is to...","a":"Initialize hardware and hand over to the bootloader. Firmware finds a bootable device and loads the bootloader from it.","e":"find /var -name \"*.log\"","s":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.html","l":"Beginner","k":["initialize","bootable","var","firmware","bootloader","find","hand"]},{"i":"qz252","t":"Boot","q":"Which widely used bootloader commonly loads Linux kernels on PCs?","a":"GRUB. GRUB is widely used, but systemd-boot and other bootloaders are also valid choices. The question asks for a common one.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.html","l":"Beginner","k":["systemd-boot","widely","bootloaders","grub","choices","uname","valid"]},{"i":"qz253","t":"Boot","q":"The correct boot order is...","a":"Firmware -> bootloader -> kernel -> systemd. UEFI loads GRUB, GRUB loads the kernel, the kernel starts systemd.","e":"systemctl list-units --type=service","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["uefi","list-units","firmware","bootloader","grub","type","systemctl"]},{"i":"qz254","t":"Services","q":"Start the nginx service immediately with...","a":"sudo systemctl start nginx. start acts now; enable only affects future boots.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["future","nginx","acts","status","systemctl","start","affects"]},{"i":"qz255","t":"Boot","q":"Make nginx start automatically at boot with...","a":"sudo systemctl enable nginx. enable creates the symlink systemd checks at boot.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["symlink","nginx","boot","status","systemctl","systemd","enable"]},{"i":"qz256","t":"Services","q":"Enable AND start a service in one command...","a":"sudo systemctl enable --now nginx. --now combines enable + start.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["enable","combines","status","systemctl","start","nginx","now"]},{"i":"qz257","t":"Services","q":"Which command shows a systemd service state and some recent journal lines by default?","a":"systemctl status nginx. systemctl status nginx shows current state and recent journal entries when available; journalctl -u nginx gives a fuller log history.","e":"journalctl -u nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/systemctl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["fuller","journal","recent","journalctl","state","nginx","history"]},{"i":"qz258","t":"Services","q":"Which command lists loaded service units and their current state (inactive units need --all)?","a":"systemctl list-units --type=service. systemctl list-units --type=service lists loaded service units; --all includes inactive loaded units. To list installed unit files, use list-unit-files.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/systemctl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["list-unit-files","inactive","list-units","loaded","all","units","lists"]},{"i":"qz259","t":"Boot","q":"Check if nginx is enabled at boot without starting anything...","a":"systemctl is-enabled nginx. is-enabled reports enabled/disabled; is-active reports running state.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["enabled/disabled","is-enabled","is-active","nginx","status","systemctl","running"]},{"i":"qz260","t":"Services","q":"Where should administrator-managed systemd system unit files and overrides usually go?","a":"/etc/systemd/system/. /etc/systemd/system is the administrator location. Vendor units commonly live in /usr/lib/systemd/system or /lib/systemd/system.","e":"systemctl list-units --type=service","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["etc/systemd/system","usr/lib/systemd/system","lib/systemd/system","administrator","list-units","vendor","units"]},{"i":"qz261","t":"Services","q":"A unit file's three classic sections are...","a":"[Unit] [Service] [Install]. Unit=description/dependencies, Service=how to run, Install=boot wiring.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.html","l":"Beginner","k":["description/dependencies","wiring","unit","status","systemctl","nginx","boot"]},{"i":"qz262","t":"Services","q":"In a service unit, what does WantedBy=multi-user.target typically specify for systemctl enable?","a":"Start this service when the system boots to multi-user mode. WantedBy tells enable where to install the wants symlink. Enabling the unit makes it start when that target is activated, subject to other unit settings.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["multi-user","wantedby","activated","enable","target","enabling","symlink"]},{"i":"qz263","t":"Services","q":"After changing a systemd unit file on disk, which command makes the manager reload unit definitions?","a":"sudo systemctl daemon-reload. daemon-reload makes systemd re-read unit definitions; restart the affected service separately if the running process must pick up changes.","e":"sudo systemctl daemon-reload","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["re-read","definitions","daemon-reload","separately","makes","restart","systemd"]},{"i":"qz264","t":"Services","q":"View logs collected by systemd with...","a":"journalctl. journalctl queries the systemd journal - the modern log store.","e":"journalctl -u nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/journalctl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["journal","journalctl","systemd","queries","nginx","log","store"]},{"i":"qz265","t":"Services","q":"Logs for just the nginx unit with...","a":"journalctl -u nginx. -u filters the journal by unit name.","e":"journalctl -u nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/journalctl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["journal","journalctl","nginx","unit","filters","name"]},{"i":"qz266","t":"Services","q":"Follow new journal entries live (like tail -f) with...","a":"journalctl -f. -f streams entries as they arrive.","e":"journalctl -u nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/journalctl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["entries","journalctl","arrive","streams","nginx"]},{"i":"qz267","t":"Boot","q":"See logs from the current boot only with...","a":"journalctl -b. -b limits to this boot; -b -1 shows the previous boot.","e":"journalctl -u nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/journalctl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["journalctl","boot","previous","nginx","limits","shows"]},{"i":"qz268","t":"Services","q":"In systemd, runlevels were replaced by...","a":"Targets. Targets like multi-user.target group units like runlevels did.","e":"systemctl get-default","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["multi-user.target","runlevels","get-default","systemctl","targets","units","group"]},{"i":"qz269","t":"Services","q":"multi-user.target is roughly equivalent to old runlevel...","a":"3. Runlevel 3 = full multi-user without GUI; 5 added graphics.","e":"systemctl get-default","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["multi-user","runlevel","get-default","gui","graphics","systemctl","full"]},{"i":"qz270","t":"Services","q":"graphical.target corresponds to runlevel...","a":"5. Runlevel 5 = multi-user with a graphical login.","e":"systemctl get-default","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["graphical","multi-user","runlevel","get-default","systemctl","login"]},{"i":"qz271","t":"Services","q":"Shut the system down immediately with...","a":"sudo shutdown now. shutdown now (or poweroff) stops services cleanly and powers off.","e":"sudo shutdown now","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/sudo.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["shutdown","poweroff","cleanly","now","powers","stops","sudo"]},{"i":"qz272","t":"Boot","q":"Reboot the machine with...","a":"sudo reboot. reboot = shutdown -r; both restart the system cleanly.","e":"sudo shutdown now","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["shutdown","cleanly","now","reboot","restart","sudo"]},{"i":"qz273","t":"Boot","q":"See which target the system boots into by default with...","a":"systemctl get-default. get-default shows the symlinked default target.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["get-default","target","symlinked","default","status","systemctl","nginx"]},{"i":"qz274","t":"Services","q":"Normally, what does systemctl mask nginx prevent that disable does not?","a":"A masked unit cannot be started at all, even manually. Masking links the unit to /dev/null, preventing ordinary activation, including manual start, until unmasked. Running instances may continue until stopped.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["dev/null","unmasked","manually","instances","even","continue","systemctl"]},{"i":"qz275","t":"Security","q":"With SSH key auth, the PUBLIC key goes...","a":"Into ~/.ssh/authorized_keys on the server. The server keeps your public key; you guard the private key.","e":"cat ~/.ssh/authorized_keys","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ssh/authorized_keys","key","public","guard","cat","private","keeps"]},{"i":"qz276","t":"Security","q":"Generate an SSH key pair with...","a":"ssh-keygen. ssh-keygen -t ed25519 is the modern default choice.","e":"ssh-keygen -t ed25519","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh-keygen.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ed25519","ssh-keygen","choice","modern","default"]},{"i":"qz277","t":"Security","q":"Install your public key on a remote host in one step with...","a":"ssh-copy-id user@host. ssh-copy-id appends your public key to the server's authorized_keys.","e":"cat ~/.ssh/authorized_keys","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh-copy-id.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["authorized_keys","ssh-copy-id","ssh/authorized_keys","key","public","appends","host"]},{"i":"qz278","t":"Security","q":"Disable direct root login over SSH by setting...","a":"PermitRootLogin no in /etc/ssh/sshd_config. Edit sshd_config, then reload sshd; keep a sudo-capable user ready.","e":"sudo -u appuser whoami","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["sudo-capable","etc/ssh/sshd_config","appuser","sshd_config","whoami","permitrootlogin","reload"]},{"i":"qz279","t":"Security","q":"System and application logs traditionally live in...","a":"/var/log. /var/log/syslog (or journal), auth.log, nginx/ and more.","e":"more file.txt","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["auth.log","var/log","var/log/syslog","file.txt","nginx","journal"]},{"i":"qz280","t":"Security","q":"On Debian/Ubuntu, where are SSH authentication events often recorded, if traditional auth logging is enabled?","a":"/var/log/auth.log (or the journal). /var/log/auth.log is common on Debian-family systems; RHEL often uses /var/log/secure. Journal-based systems may use journalctl -u ssh or sshd.","e":"journalctl -u nginx","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["journal-based","var/log/auth.log","var/log/secure","debian-family","journal","journalctl","sshd"]},{"i":"qz281","t":"Security","q":"Show recent login history with...","a":"last. last reads wtmp: who logged in, when, from where.","e":"history 20","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/journalctl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["history","wtmp","logged","last","reads"]},{"i":"qz282","t":"Security","q":"See who is logged in right now with...","a":"who. who (or w) lists current sessions with terminals and origins.","e":"who","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ls.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["origins","sessions","terminals","current","lists"]},{"i":"qz283","t":"Scheduling","q":"Edit your personal cron schedule with...","a":"crontab -e. crontab -e opens your user crontab and installs it on save.","e":"crontab -l","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/crontab.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["crontab","save","opens","installs","user"]},{"i":"qz284","t":"Scheduling","q":"The five cron time fields are...","a":"minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week. m h dom mon dow - then the command.","e":"crontab -l","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/crontab.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["day-of-month","day-of-week","dom","dow","crontab","mon","month"]},{"i":"qz285","t":"Scheduling","q":"Cron entry '*/5 * * * *' runs...","a":"Every 5 minutes. */5 in the minute field = every five minutes.","e":"crontab -l","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/crontab.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["crontab","minutes","five","minute","field","every"]},{"i":"qz286","t":"Scheduling","q":"Cron entry '0 2 * * *' runs...","a":"Daily at 2:00 AM. minute 0, hour 2, every day - the classic backup slot.","e":"crontab -l","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/crontab.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["crontab","slot","minute","daily","day","classic","hour"]},{"i":"qz287","t":"Scheduling","q":"The cron shortcut for once a day is...","a":"@daily. @daily (= 0 0 * * *), @hourly, @weekly, @reboot also exist.","e":"sudo reboot","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/crontab.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["hourly","reboot","weekly","daily","sudo","exist"]},{"i":"qz288","t":"Scheduling","q":"System-wide cron drop-in directories include...","a":"/etc/cron.d and /etc/cron.daily. Packages drop jobs into /etc/cron.d, cron.daily, cron.weekly...","e":"jobs -l","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["etc/cron.daily","etc/cron.d","drop","cron.daily","cron.weekly","jobs","packages"]},{"i":"qz289","t":"Files","q":"Create a gzipped tar archive of /etc with...","a":"tar -czf etc.tar.gz /etc. c=create, z=gzip, f=file.","e":"tar -tf backup.tar","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["etc.tar.gz","gzip","tar","backup.tar","czf","create"]},{"i":"qz290","t":"Files","q":"Extract a .tar.gz archive with...","a":"tar -xzf archive.tar.gz. x=extract, z=gzip, f=file; add -C to choose the target dir.","e":"tar -tf backup.tar","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["tar","backup.tar","extract","xzf","archive.tar.gz","gzip","choose"]},{"i":"qz291","t":"Files","q":"List an archive's contents without extracting with...","a":"tar -tzf archive.tar.gz. t=list - always peek before extracting unknown archives.","e":"tar -tf backup.tar","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["peek","backup.tar","extracting","archive.tar.gz","tzf","tar","archives"]},{"i":"qz292","t":"Files","q":"Compress a single file with...","a":"gzip file. gzip file creates file.gz and removes the original.","e":"file report","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/bookworm/gzip/gzip.1.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["file.gz","gzip","removes","report","original","creates"]},{"i":"qz293","t":"Security","q":"Efficiently sync a directory to a remote host with...","a":"rsync -av /data/ user@host:/backup/. rsync transfers only what changed and preserves permissions with -a.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/rsync.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["preserves","rsync","app.log","transfers","stat","host","backup"]},{"i":"qz294","t":"Security","q":"Which common permission mode keeps an SSH private key readable and writable only by its owner?","a":"600. 600 makes the private key readable/writable by its owner only. 400 (owner read only) can also be safe; public-read mode 644 is not.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["readable/writable","public-read","private","key","mode","owner","app.log"]},{"i":"qz295","t":"Security","q":"fail2ban protects servers by...","a":"Banning IPs after repeated failed logins. fail2ban watches logs and firewall-bans brute-forcers.","e":"sudo firewall-cmd --list-all","s":"https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html/configuring_firewalls_and_packet_filters/using-and-configuring-firewalld_firewall-packet-filters","l":"Beginner","k":["firewall-bans","brute-forcers","banning","fail2ban","list-all","firewall-cmd","ips"]},{"i":"qz296","t":"Security","q":"The principle of least privilege means...","a":"Give users and services only the access they need. Minimal rights limit the damage when something breaks or is attacked.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["damage","something","attacked","minimal","breaks","give","status"]},{"i":"qz297","t":"Security","q":"Which routine action helps reduce exposure to already-fixed server vulnerabilities?","a":"Applying security updates regularly. Apply security updates regularly: patched software closes known flaws. Updates alone are not a guarantee of security.","e":"sudo apt update","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["flaws","closes","update","regularly","known","apt","applying"]},{"i":"qz298","t":"Security","q":"SELinux and AppArmor are...","a":"Mandatory access control systems confining what processes may do. MAC systems confine even root-owned daemons to their policy.","e":"aa-status","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["root-owned","aa-status","mandatory","confining","confine","daemons","mac"]},{"i":"qz299","t":"Security","q":"scp to a server whose SSH runs on port 2222 uses...","a":"scp -P 2222 file user@host:/path. scp uses capital -P for port (ssh uses lowercase -p).","e":"file report","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["lowercase","scp","port","capital","ssh","report","uses"]},{"i":"iv000","t":"Basics","q":"Who created Linux, and when?","a":"Linus Torvalds, a Finnish student, announced the first Linux kernel (version 0.01) in September 1991. He still leads kernel development today.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/process/license-rules.html","l":"Beginner","k":["finnish","linus","torvalds","uname","student","announced","september"]},{"i":"iv001","t":"Basics","q":"What is the difference between Linux and a Linux distribution?","a":"Linux is only the kernel - the core that manages CPU, memory, devices and processes. A distribution (Ubuntu, Fedora, Debian) bundles the kernel with GNU tools, libraries, a package manager and apps to make a complete, usable OS.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://www.gnu.org/gnu/gnu-history.html","l":"Beginner","k":["bundles","fedora","usable","distribution","ubuntu","etc/os-release","manages"]},{"i":"iv002","t":"Basics","q":"Which license is the Linux kernel released under?","a":"GNU GPL version 2 (GPL-2.0-only), with a syscall exception so normal programs that just use Linux system calls do not have to be GPL themselves.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://www.gnu.org/gnu/gnu-history.html","l":"Beginner","k":["gpl-2.0-only","syscall","gpl","themselves","uname","exception","version"]},{"i":"iv003","t":"Basics","q":"What does GNU stand for, and why does it matter to Linux?","a":"GNU stands for \"GNU's Not Unix\". Richard Stallman started the GNU Project in 1983 to build a free Unix-like OS. GNU supplied the tools (gcc, bash, coreutils) that, combined with the Linux kernel, make a full system - hence the name GNU/Linux.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://www.gnu.org/gnu/gnu-history.html","l":"Beginner","k":["gnu/linux","coreutils","unix-like","stallman","gnu","gcc","uname"]},{"i":"iv004","t":"Basics","q":"What is the kernel's main job?","a":"It manages the hardware for every program: CPU scheduling, memory management, device drivers, filesystems and networking. Programs request these services through system calls instead of touching hardware directly.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/touch.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["scheduling","status","systemctl","manages","drivers","touching","nginx"]},{"i":"iv005","t":"Basics","q":"What is the difference between Unix and Linux?","a":"Unix is the original proprietary OS family started at Bell Labs in 1969. Linux is a free, open-source Unix-like kernel written from scratch; it follows Unix design and POSIX conventions but contains no Unix source code.","e":"source ~/.bashrc","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["open-source","proprietary","unix-like","posix","scratch","bashrc","unix"]},{"i":"iv006","t":"Basics","q":"What is a shell?","a":"A command-line interpreter between you and the OS. It reads commands, starts programs and provides scripting features like variables, loops and pipes. Bash is the most common Linux shell.","e":"echo \"$HOME\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["command-line","pipes","interpreter","home","echo","loops","features"]},{"i":"iv007","t":"Basics","q":"Name some popular Linux distributions and their families.","a":"Debian family: Debian, Ubuntu, Linux Mint (they use apt and .deb packages). Red Hat family: RHEL, CentOS Stream, Fedora (they use dnf and .rpm packages). Others: Arch, openSUSE, Alpine.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["opensuse","alpine","centos","fedora","arch","dnf","deb"]},{"i":"iv008","t":"Basics","q":"What is the difference between kernel space and user space?","a":"Kernel space is where the kernel runs with full hardware access. User space is where normal programs run with restricted access; for privileged work like file or network access they must ask the kernel through a system call.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["privileged","space","uname","kernel","restricted","user","normal"]},{"i":"iv009","t":"Basics","q":"Why is Linux so popular for servers?","a":"It is free and open source, stable for years of uptime, secure by design (users, permissions, fast patches), runs on almost any hardware, and is easy to automate with shells and tools. Most cloud and web servers run Linux.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["automate","uptime","almost","years","easy","etc/os-release","fast"]},{"i":"iv010","t":"Basics","q":"How is Windows different from Linux?","a":"Windows is a commercial operating system made by Microsoft. Linux is free and open source, and it is more secure, stable, and widely used for servers.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/free.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["windows","commercial","etc/os-release","made","microsoft","widely","stable"]},{"i":"iv011","t":"Basics","q":"What is one feature Linux has but Windows does not?","a":"Linux is fully open source, so anyone can see and modify its code. This gives complete control and customization that Windows does not allow.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/process/license-rules.html","l":"Beginner","k":["customization","windows","anyone","etc/os-release","fully","modify","complete"]},{"i":"iv012","t":"Basics","q":"What is Linux?","a":"Linux is a free, open-source operating system kernel based on Unix. Combined with system tools in a distribution, it powers most servers, Android phones, and cloud systems.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/free.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["open-source","android","phones","etc/os-release","powers","cloud","operating"]},{"i":"iv013","t":"Basics","q":"What is an Operating System (OS)?","a":"An Operating System is software that manages the computer's hardware and software resources. It acts as a bridge between the user and the hardware.","e":"source ~/.bashrc","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/process/license-rules.html","l":"Beginner","k":["operating","bashrc","source","computer","acts","bridge","manages"]},{"i":"iv014","t":"Basics","q":"What is Bash?","a":"Bash (Bourne Again Shell) is the default command-line shell in most Linux distributions. It is used to run commands and to write shell scripts.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/sed.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["bourne","command-line","etc/os-release","again","bash","cat","distributions"]},{"i":"iv015","t":"Basics","q":"What is the difference between CLI and GUI?","a":"CLI (Command Line Interface) lets you work by typing commands. GUI (Graphical User Interface) lets you work with windows, icons, and menus.","e":"diff -u old.conf new.conf","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ping.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["cli","gui","diff","graphical","old.conf","new.conf","typing"]},{"i":"iv016","t":"Basics","q":"What is the difference between the Kernel and the Shell?","a":"The Kernel talks directly to the hardware and manages system resources. The Shell is the interface through which the user gives commands to the Kernel.","e":"source ~/.bashrc","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/diff.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["bashrc","source","talks","shell","manages","kernel","directly"]},{"i":"iv017","t":"Basics","q":"What is the difference between RHEL and Ubuntu?","a":"RHEL uses RPM packages with dnf/yum and is enterprise-focused with paid support. Ubuntu uses DEB packages with apt and is community-focused and free.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["dnf/yum","enterprise-focused","community-focused","ubuntu","rhel","deb","paid"]},{"i":"iv018","t":"Basics","q":"What is the difference between CentOS and RHEL?","a":"RHEL is a commercial distribution with official Red Hat support. CentOS was its free community rebuild; today CentOS Stream sits just ahead of RHEL development.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.html","l":"Beginner","k":["centos","hat","rebuild","rhel","official","community","sits"]},{"i":"iv019","t":"Basics","q":"Which Linux distribution do you like, and why?","a":"Sample answer: RHEL, because it is stable, secure, and widely used in enterprise environments with long-term support.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/sed.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["long-term","enterprise","etc/os-release","rhel","sample","widely","stable"]},{"i":"iv020","t":"Basics","q":"Have you explored the Linux kernel?","a":"Sample answer: Yes. I understand that the kernel manages hardware, memory, processes, and device drivers, and I use commands like uname and dmesg to see kernel information.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/process/license-rules.html","l":"Beginner","k":["dmesg","uname","yes","sample","manages","kernel","answer"]},{"i":"iv021","t":"Basics","q":"What is the difference between an absolute and a relative path?","a":"An absolute path starts from the root / (for example /home/akash/file.txt) and means the same thing from anywhere. A relative path is resolved from your current directory (docs/file.txt) and changes meaning as you cd around.","e":"pwd","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["home/akash/file.txt","docs/file.txt","absolute","relative","anywhere","thing","pwd"]},{"i":"iv022","t":"Basics","q":"What do . and .. mean in a directory listing?","a":". refers to the current directory and .. to the parent directory. They are real directory entries, so commands like cd .. move you up one level and ./script runs a script in the current directory.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["refers","move","pwd","level","directory","entries","real"]},{"i":"iv023","t":"Basics","q":"How do you see your current directory and jump to your home directory?","a":"pwd prints the current directory. cd with no argument (or cd ~) takes you to your home directory, which is stored in the $HOME variable.","e":"cd ~","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["current","home","takes","argument","directory","variable","pwd"]},{"i":"iv024","t":"Basics","q":"What is the difference between ls, ls -l and ls -a?","a":"ls lists names only. ls -l shows the long format: permissions, link count, owner, group, size and modification time. ls -a also shows hidden files - names starting with a dot, including . and ..","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["long","hidden","app.log","modification","stat","dot","format"]},{"i":"iv025","t":"Basics","q":"What is the root directory and what lives directly under it?","a":"/ is the top of the single filesystem tree. Standard children: /etc (configuration), /home (user data), /var (logs and changing data), /usr (programs and libraries), /bin and /sbin (essential commands), /tmp (temporary files), /dev (device files), /proc and /sys (kernel information).","e":"pwd","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["sbin","sys","tmp","proc","children","essential","bin"]},{"i":"iv026","t":"Basics","q":"What are /proc and /sys?","a":"Virtual filesystems the kernel exposes as files. /proc shows process and system information (for example /proc/cpuinfo); /sys exposes devices and kernel objects for tools like udev. Nothing there is a real disk file.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/proc.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["proc/cpuinfo","udev","sys","proc","exposes","aux","nothing"]},{"i":"iv027","t":"Basics","q":"How is cd - useful?","a":"cd - jumps back to the previous directory you were in and prints it. It is the fastest way to toggle between two directories.","e":"pwd","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["fastest","toggle","pwd","jumps","were","previous","way"]},{"i":"iv028","t":"Basics","q":"What is a hidden file in Linux?","a":"Any file whose name begins with a dot, like .bashrc. It is not secret or protected - it is simply not shown by default; ls -a reveals it. Dotfiles in your home directory store per-user configuration.","e":"cd ~","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ls.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["per-user","dotfiles","reveals","begins","bashrc","simply","shown"]},{"i":"iv029","t":"Basics","q":"What does the tilde (~) expand to?","a":"Your home directory: ~/docs means /home/akash/docs. The form ~name expands to that user's home directory, for example ~root is /root.","e":"cd ~","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["home/akash/docs","docs","form","expands","means","root","home"]},{"i":"iv030","t":"Basics","q":"What is the difference between cp a b and mv a b?","a":"cp copies, so the original stays in place. mv renames or moves, so the original path no longer exists. Across filesystems mv actually copies and then deletes the original.","e":"file report","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["renames","actually","deletes","report","longer","original","stays"]},{"i":"iv031","t":"Basics","q":"What is the purpose of /etc, /var, /home, /tmp, /opt, /usr, /boot, /dev, /proc and /sys?","a":"/etc stores configuration files. /var stores changing data like logs. /home stores users' personal files. /tmp stores temporary files. /opt stores optional third-party software. /usr stores commands, libraries, and applications. /boot stores bootloader and kernel files. /dev contains device files. /proc shows process and kernel information. /sys shows hardware and kernel object information.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["third-party","opt","usr","sys","tmp","dev","proc"]},{"i":"iv032","t":"Basics","q":"What is the difference between a file and a directory?","a":"A file stores data. A directory is a container that stores files and other directories.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/diff.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["pwd","directory","container","stores","directories"]},{"i":"iv033","t":"Files","q":"How do you create an empty file or update a file's timestamp?","a":"touch filename. It creates the file if it is missing; if it exists, it updates the access and modification times to now.","e":"sudo apt update","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["update","times","filename","apt","modification","touch","updates"]},{"i":"iv034","t":"Files","q":"What is the difference between a hard link and a symbolic (soft) link?","a":"A hard link is another name for the same inode - the same data - so it survives deletion of the other names, but it cannot cross filesystems or point to a directory. A symlink is a small file containing a path: it can cross filesystems and point to directories, but it breaks if the target is deleted.","e":"ln file.txt file.hard","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["hard","file.hard","survives","file.txt","deletion","symlink","link"]},{"i":"iv035","t":"Files","q":"Which commands show disk usage?","a":"df -h shows free and used space per mounted filesystem. du -sh * shows the size of each item in the current directory in human-readable form. ncdu (if installed) gives an interactive browser.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/free.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["human-readable","ncdu","browser","item","interactive","pwd","form"]},{"i":"iv036","t":"Files","q":"How do you create a directory together with its parents in one step?","a":"mkdir -p /a/b/c creates any missing parent directories and does not complain if some already exist.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/mkdir.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["a/b/c","complain","mkdir","parent","pwd","missing","directories"]},{"i":"iv037","t":"Files","q":"What does rm -r do, and why is rm -rf dangerous?","a":"rm -r deletes directories and everything inside them, recursively. Adding -f forces the deletion without prompts and ignores missing files, so a single typo can silently destroy the wrong directory tree or even the whole system.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/rm.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["silently","recursively","typo","destroy","prompts","ignores","deletion"]},{"i":"iv038","t":"Files","q":"How do you copy a whole directory tree?","a":"cp -r source dest copies recursively. cp -a (archive mode) also preserves permissions, ownership and timestamps, which is what you want for backups.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["dest","recursively","preserves","timestamps","app.log","stat","ownership"]},{"i":"iv039","t":"Files","q":"How do you find a file by name under a directory?","a":"find /path -name 'file.txt'. Useful variants: -iname for case-insensitive matching, -type f or -type d, and -mtime -7 for files changed in the last 7 days.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/find.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["case-insensitive","iname","variants","mtime","file.txt","find","pwd"]},{"i":"iv040","t":"Files","q":"What is the difference between find and locate?","a":"find searches the live filesystem, so it is slower but always current. locate searches a prebuilt database (refreshed by updatedb), so it is instant but can miss very new files or still list deleted ones.","e":"locate httpd.conf","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["locate","httpd.conf","slower","prebuilt","updatedb","refreshed","instant"]},{"i":"iv041","t":"Files","q":"What does the file command do?","a":"file name inspects the file's actual contents (magic bytes) and reports its real type - text, ELF binary, image, archive - no matter what extension the name has.","e":"tar -tf backup.tar","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["elf","backup.tar","tar","extension","magic","inspects","binary"]},{"i":"iv042","t":"Files","q":"How do you rename many files at once?","a":"The usual approach is a shell loop: for f in *.txt; do mv \"$f\" \"${f%.txt}.bak\"; done. Some distros also ship a rename command. Plain mv has no batch renaming syntax.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["rename","bak","batch","txt","renaming","approach","usual"]},{"i":"iv043","t":"Files","q":"What is the Linux equivalent of a Windows .exe file?","a":"Linux does not depend on extensions. Any file with execute permission can run as a program, and shell scripts (.sh) are the common equivalent for tools and installers.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["installers","equivalent","execute","extensions","app.log","depend","stat"]},{"i":"iv044","t":"Files","q":"How do you compress and extract files?","a":"Use tar: tar -czvf archive.tar.gz folder/ creates a compressed archive, and tar -xzvf archive.tar.gz extracts it. For .zip files, use zip and unzip.","e":"tar -tf backup.tar","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["czvf","xzvf","unzip","backup.tar","archive.tar.gz","tar","compressed"]},{"i":"iv045","t":"Text","q":"What is the difference between cat, less, head and tail?","a":"cat dumps whole files to stdout - fine for small files or joining files. less pages through big files interactively with search. head shows the first lines (10 by default), tail the last; tail -f follows a growing file live, the classic way to watch logs.","e":"watch -n 2 df -h","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["watch","interactively","tail","stdout","less","dumps","head"]},{"i":"iv046","t":"Text","q":"How do you search for text inside files?","a":"grep 'pattern' file. Common flags: -r recursive into directories, -i case-insensitive, -n show line numbers, -v invert (show non-matching lines), -c just count matches.","e":"file report","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["non-matching","case-insensitive","invert","recursive","flags","report","grep"]},{"i":"iv047","t":"Text","q":"What is a pipe?","a":"The | operator sends one command's standard output into the next command's standard input, for example ps aux | grep nginx. Chaining small tools this way is the core Unix philosophy.","e":"grep -n error app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/grep.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["operator","philosophy","grep","error","app.log","chaining","way"]},{"i":"iv048","t":"Text","q":"How do you count lines, words and bytes in a file?","a":"wc file prints lines, words and bytes; wc -l gives only the line count. A typical use: grep ERROR app.log | wc -l to count error lines.","e":"file report","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/grep.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["bytes","words","count","lines","line","report","grep"]},{"i":"iv049","t":"Text","q":"What does sort do, and how do you get unique lines?","a":"sort orders lines alphabetically; -n sorts numerically, -r reverses, -k selects a column. uniq removes only ADJACENT duplicate lines, so the standard idiom is sort file | uniq, or simply sort -u.","e":"file report","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/sort.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["alphabetically","reverses","idiom","numerically","duplicate","uniq","adjacent"]},{"i":"iv050","t":"Text","q":"How do you see the differences between two files?","a":"diff file1 file2 shows the changed lines; diff -u produces the unified format used in patches. For side-by-side comparison use diff -y or vimdiff.","e":"file report","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/sed.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["side-by-side","produces","vimdiff","file1","file2","unified","diff"]},{"i":"iv051","t":"Text","q":"What are cut, awk and sed used for?","a":"cut extracts columns by a delimiter: cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd. awk splits lines into fields and processes them: awk '{print $1}'. sed edits streams, most often search-and-replace: sed 's/old/new/g' file (add -i to edit the file in place).","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["search-and-replace","s/old/new/g","awk","sed","cut","delimiter","etc/passwd"]},{"i":"iv052","t":"Text","q":"How do you watch a log file in real time?","a":"tail -f /var/log/syslog, or journalctl -f on systemd systems. New lines appear as they are written; Ctrl+C stops following.","e":"journalctl -u nginx","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["var/log/syslog","ctrl","journalctl","written","systemd","appear","nginx"]},{"i":"iv053","t":"Text","q":"How do you find which files under /etc contain a string?","a":"grep -r 'string' /etc. Add -l to list only the matching filenames, or -n to include line numbers with each match.","e":"which python3","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["string","filenames","python3","grep","numbers","matching","line"]},{"i":"iv054","t":"Permissions","q":"What do the fields in an ls -l permission string like -rwxr-xr-- mean?","a":"The first character is the type (- file, d directory, l symlink). Then come three triplets: owner, group, others. In each triplet r = read (4), w = write (2), x = execute (1). So rwxr-xr-- is owner rwx, group r-x, others r-- - octal 754.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/group.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["rwxr-xr","r-x","triplets","rwx","octal","execute","symlink"]},{"i":"iv055","t":"Permissions","q":"How do you change permissions with chmod?","a":"Symbolic form: chmod u+x file, chmod g-w file. Octal form: chmod 755 file gives the owner rwx and everyone else r-x. On directories, x means 'allowed to enter'.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["g-w","r-x","octal","symbolic","rwx","chmod","everyone"]},{"i":"iv056","t":"Permissions","q":"What is the difference between chmod, chown and chgrp?","a":"chmod changes the permission bits. chown changes the owner, and optionally the group too: chown user:group file. chgrp changes only the group. You need to be root or the file's owner.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html","l":"Scenario","k":["chgrp","chown","optionally","chmod","too","app.log","stat"]},{"i":"iv057","t":"Permissions","q":"Where are user accounts and groups stored?","a":"Account information lives in /etc/passwd; password hashes live in /etc/shadow, readable only by root. Groups are defined in /etc/group, with /etc/gshadow for group passwords and admins.","e":"passwd trainee","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["etc/gshadow","etc/shadow","etc/group","readable","hashes","etc/passwd","trainee"]},{"i":"iv058","t":"Permissions","q":"How do you add a user and give them sudo rights?","a":"Create the account with adduser name (Debian/Ubuntu) or useradd name plus passwd name. Then add them to the admin group: usermod -aG sudo name on Debian/Ubuntu, or the wheel group on RHEL/Fedora.","e":"sudo useradd -m trainee","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["useradd","rhel/fedora","debian/ubuntu","adduser","usermod","trainee","add"]},{"i":"iv059","t":"Permissions","q":"What is the root user, and what is sudo?","a":"root (UID 0) is the superuser that bypasses permission checks. sudo lets permitted users run single commands as root, with every use logged - much safer than working as root all the time.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/sudo.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["bypasses","superuser","safer","uid","permitted","root","app.log"]},{"i":"iv060","t":"Permissions","q":"What does the id command show?","a":"Your numeric uid, your primary gid, and every group you belong to - for example uid=1000(akash) gid=1000(akash) groups=1000(akash),27(sudo).","e":"groups trainee","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/group.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["belong","gid","uid","trainee","numeric","akash","primary"]},{"i":"iv061","t":"Permissions","q":"What is the difference between su and sudo?","a":"su switches to another account (root by default) and asks for the TARGET account's password. sudo runs one command as root, asks for YOUR password, checks the sudoers policy, and logs the action.","e":"systemctl get-default","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["sudoers","get-default","default","systemctl","action","switches","sudo"]},{"i":"iv062","t":"Permissions","q":"How do you lock or delete a user account?","a":"Lock the password with usermod -L name (or passwd -l name). Delete the account with userdel name; add -r to also remove the home directory and mail spool.","e":"cd ~","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/passwd.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["userdel","spool","delete","lock","usermod","account","mail"]},{"i":"iv063","t":"Permissions","q":"What are the SUID, SGID and sticky bits?","a":"Special permission bits: SUID (octal 4xxx) runs a program with the file owner's rights - passwd is the classic example. SGID (2xxx) does the same with the group, and on a directory makes new files inherit the directory's group. The sticky bit (1xxx) on a shared directory like /tmp means only a file's owner can delete it.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["suid","sgid","sticky","xxx","inherit","octal","tmp"]},{"i":"iv064","t":"Permissions","q":"What is the difference between useradd and adduser?","a":"useradd is the low-level command with basic options. adduser is a friendlier script on Debian/Ubuntu that creates the home directory and applies defaults automatically.","e":"cd ~","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["low-level","useradd","adduser","debian/ubuntu","friendlier","basic","applies"]},{"i":"iv065","t":"Permissions","q":"How do you add a user to a group?","a":"Use usermod -aG groupname username. The -a option appends the group; without it, the user's other group memberships would be replaced.","e":"sudo usermod -aG developers trainee","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/group.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["developers","groupname","usermod","trainee","appends","memberships","user"]},{"i":"iv066","t":"Permissions","q":"How do you change a user's password?","a":"Use passwd username and enter the new password. A user changes their own password with plain passwd; root can change anyone's.","e":"passwd trainee","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/passwd.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["trainee","change","passwd","password","anyone","user","enter"]},{"i":"iv067","t":"Permissions","q":"What are primary and secondary groups?","a":"A primary group is the user's default group, which new files they create belong to. Secondary groups give the user extra permissions to shared resources.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/group.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["secondary","primary","belong","app.log","stat","give","groups"]},{"i":"iv068","t":"Permissions","q":"What is the difference between a user and a group?","a":"A user is an individual account used to log in to the system. A group is a collection of users that makes managing permissions easier.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/group.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["collection","app.log","easier","stat","individual","group","log"]},{"i":"iv069","t":"Permissions","q":"Why do we use groups in Linux?","a":"Groups let you manage permissions for many users at the same time. This makes administration easier and more secure.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/group.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["administration","app.log","easier","stat","groups","manage","secure"]},{"i":"iv070","t":"Permissions","q":"How do you give a file only read and write permission?","a":"Use chmod 600 filename. This gives the owner read and write (rw-) and removes all permissions for group and others.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["filename","chmod","write","removes","app.log","stat","read"]},{"i":"iv071","t":"Processes","q":"How do you see which processes are running?","a":"ps aux for a one-time snapshot of all processes; top or htop for a live, sorted view; pgrep name to find the PID of a process by name.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/pgrep.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["one-time","pgrep","htop","snapshot","processes","aux","process"]},{"i":"iv072","t":"Processes","q":"What are PID and PPID?","a":"PID is the process ID the kernel assigns to each process; PPID is its parent's PID. PID 1 - systemd on most distros - is the first process at boot and the ancestor of everything else.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ancestor","ppid","assigns","distros","pid","aux","systemd"]},{"i":"iv073","t":"Processes","q":"How do you stop a process?","a":"kill PID sends SIGTERM (15), a polite request to exit that allows cleanup. kill -9 PID sends SIGKILL, which the process cannot catch or ignore - a last resort, because it gets no chance to clean up. pkill and killall work by name instead of PID.","e":"killall nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/kill.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["killall","polite","pkill","sigterm","cleanup","sigkill","kill"]},{"i":"iv074","t":"Processes","q":"What is the difference between a process and a thread?","a":"A process has its own private memory space; threads are tasks inside a process that share its memory, so they are cheaper to create but can interfere with each other's data.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["cheaper","interfere","threads","aux","process","create","share"]},{"i":"iv075","t":"Processes","q":"What is a daemon?","a":"A background service process that runs detached from any terminal - for example sshd or cron. On modern distros daemons are normally started and supervised by systemd as service units.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["supervised","detached","daemons","cron","sshd","distros","aux"]},{"i":"iv076","t":"Processes","q":"How do you run a command in the background and bring it back?","a":"Append & to start it in the background. jobs lists the shell's jobs, fg %1 brings one to the foreground, Ctrl+Z suspends a foreground job, and bg %1 resumes it in the background. To survive logout use nohup, tmux, or a systemd service.","e":"systemctl list-units --type=service","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["tmux","suspends","resumes","logout","nohup","foreground","background"]},{"i":"iv077","t":"Processes","q":"What is a zombie process?","a":"A process that has finished but whose parent has not yet collected its exit status with wait(). It keeps only a process-table entry and no memory. The real fix is to signal or restart the misbehaving parent; orphaned zombies are reaped by PID 1.","e":"echo $?","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["process-table","misbehaving","reaped","zombies","orphaned","finished","restart"]},{"i":"iv078","t":"Processes","q":"How do you find which process is using a file or a port?","a":"lsof /path/to/file shows processes with that file open; lsof -i :80 shows what holds port 80. ss -tulpen maps listening sockets to processes (run as root to see other users' processes).","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["path/to/file","tulpen","lsof","port","aux","sockets","holds"]},{"i":"iv079","t":"Processes","q":"What do nice and renice do?","a":"They adjust CPU scheduling priority. Nice values run from -20 (highest priority, root only) to 19 (lowest). nice -n 10 command starts a program at lower priority; renice changes the priority of an already-running process.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["already-running","renice","nice","adjust","scheduling","priority","highest"]},{"i":"iv080","t":"Processes","q":"What is the difference between SIGTERM and SIGKILL?","a":"SIGTERM (15) asks the process to shut down and can be caught or ignored, so the process can clean up first. SIGKILL (9) is enforced by the kernel directly: the process dies immediately with no cleanup.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/kill.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["sigterm","sigkill","enforced","cleanup","ignored","dies","shut"]},{"i":"iv081","t":"Processes","q":"What is a process?","a":"A process is a running instance of a program. Every process gets a unique Process ID (PID) from the kernel.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["aux","process","instance","pid","unique","gets","program"]},{"i":"iv082","t":"Processes","q":"What is the difference between a process and a service?","a":"A process is any running program. A service is a background process that provides system or application functionality, like sshd or Apache.","e":"myfunc() { echo hi; }","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["myfunc","sshd","echo","process","functionality","service","apache"]},{"i":"iv083","t":"Processes","q":"What is an orphan process?","a":"An orphan is a running process whose parent has already terminated. It is automatically adopted by PID 1 (init or systemd).","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["terminated","orphan","init","adopted","aux","process","systemd"]},{"i":"iv084","t":"Processes","q":"What is the difference between foreground and background processes?","a":"A foreground process runs in the terminal and accepts user input. A background process runs independently without blocking the terminal.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["foreground","background","independently","aux","process","accepts","terminal"]},{"i":"iv085","t":"Processes","q":"What are parent and child processes?","a":"A parent process is one that creates another process. The new process is called the child, and it runs independently but is initially managed by its parent.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["child","independently","parent","initially","aux","process","called"]},{"i":"iv086","t":"Packages","q":"What is the difference between apt, apt-get and dpkg?","a":"dpkg installs individual .deb files but does not resolve dependencies. apt and apt-get work with repositories and handle dependencies automatically; apt is the friendlier modern command (apt install, apt update), apt-get is the older script-stable interface.","e":"sudo apt update","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["apt-get","script-stable","friendlier","update","dpkg","repositories","handle"]},{"i":"iv087","t":"Packages","q":"What do apt update and apt upgrade actually do?","a":"apt update only downloads the latest package INDEX from the repositories - it installs nothing. apt upgrade then installs the newer versions of your installed packages. The correct order is update first, then upgrade.","e":"sudo apt update","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["upgrade","newer","update","repositories","apt","latest","nothing"]},{"i":"iv088","t":"Packages","q":"How do you find, install and remove a package on Debian/Ubuntu?","a":"apt search keyword to find it, apt show name for details, sudo apt install name to install it, and sudo apt remove name to uninstall (apt purge also deletes its configuration files).","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["uninstall","purge","remove","keyword","find","deletes","etc/os-release"]},{"i":"iv089","t":"Packages","q":"What are the equivalents on RHEL/Fedora systems?","a":"dnf, the modern replacement for yum: dnf search, dnf install, dnf remove, dnf update. These manage .rpm packages; rpm -ivh installs a single downloaded package without resolving dependencies.","e":"sudo apt update","s":"https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html/managing_software_with_the_dnf_tool/assembly_yum-commands-list_managing-software-with-the-dnf-tool","l":"Beginner","k":["ivh","yum","update","dnf","replacement","resolving","apt"]},{"i":"iv090","t":"Packages","q":"What is a package repository?","a":"A server that hosts packages plus signed metadata about them. Your system fetches from the repositories listed in /etc/apt/sources.list and /etc/apt/sources.list.d (Debian/Ubuntu) or /etc/yum.repos.d (RHEL/Fedora).","e":"cat /etc/apt/sources.list","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["etc/yum.repos.d","etc/apt/sources.list.d","rhel/fedora","fetches","etc/apt/sources.list","debian/ubuntu","repositories"]},{"i":"iv091","t":"Packages","q":"What is a dependency?","a":"Another package a program needs in order to run - libraries, interpreters, helper tools. apt and dnf install dependencies automatically; dpkg and rpm alone do not, which is the main reason the higher-level tools exist.","e":"which python3","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["higher-level","helper","interpreters","dnf","dpkg","python3","main"]},{"i":"iv092","t":"Packages","q":"How do you list installed packages and find which package owns a file?","a":"On Debian/Ubuntu: dpkg -l lists installed packages and dpkg -S /path/to/file names the owning package. On RHEL/Fedora: rpm -qa lists them and rpm -qf /path/to/file names the owner.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["path/to/file","rhel/fedora","debian/ubuntu","packages","dpkg","etc/os-release","package"]},{"i":"iv093","t":"Packages","q":"What are snap and flatpak?","a":"Distribution-independent package formats that bundle their dependencies and run sandboxed. Snaps come from Canonical's store; flatpaks usually come from Flathub. They trade extra disk space for portability across distros.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["distribution-independent","bundle","flathub","portability","canonical","flatpaks","snaps"]},{"i":"iv094","t":"System","q":"How do you compile software from source, in short?","a":"The classic flow is ./configure (checks your system and generates a Makefile), make (compiles), then sudo make install (copies files, usually under /usr/local). You need a compiler and the relevant -dev libraries installed first.","e":"source ~/.bashrc","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Scenario","k":["usr/local","makefile","compiles","compiler","generates","source","bashrc"]},{"i":"iv095","t":"Packages","q":"How do you fix 'Unable to locate package' on Ubuntu?","a":"Run sudo apt update first - the local package index is missing or stale. If it still fails, either the package name is wrong (check with apt search) or the repository that carries it (universe, multiverse, or a PPA) is not enabled.","e":"cat /etc/apt/sources.list","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["multiverse","etc/apt/sources.list","ppa","repository","carries","stale","universe"]},{"i":"iv096","t":"Packages","q":"What is RPM?","a":"RPM (Red Hat Package Manager) is the package format used by RHEL-based distributions. The rpm command installs and manages individual .rpm package files.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/rpm.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["rhel-based","hat","rpm","etc/os-release","red","manages","individual"]},{"i":"iv097","t":"Packages","q":"What is the difference between RPM and YUM/DNF?","a":"rpm installs individual package files but does not resolve dependencies. YUM and DNF work with repositories and install dependencies automatically.","e":"file report","s":"https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html/managing_software_with_the_dnf_tool/assembly_yum-commands-list_managing-software-with-the-dnf-tool","l":"Beginner","k":["yum","dnf","rpm","repositories","report","dependencies","individual"]},{"i":"iv098","t":"System","q":"What is the difference between MySQL and MariaDB?","a":"MariaDB is a community-driven fork of MySQL. It is fully open source and offers extra features and performance improvements.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.html","l":"Beginner","k":["community-driven","mariadb","fork","mysql","offers","improvements","etc/os-release"]},{"i":"iv099","t":"Networking","q":"How do you check your IP address and routing table?","a":"ip addr shows interfaces and their addresses; ip route shows the routing table, including the default gateway. The older equivalents are ifconfig and route -n.","e":"ifconfig -a","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-route.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ifconfig","addr","table","routing","equivalents","gateway","older"]},{"i":"iv100","t":"Networking","q":"How do you test connectivity step by step?","a":"Ping your gateway first, then an external IP like 8.8.8.8, then a name like google.com. If the IP works but the name fails, the problem is DNS; if even the gateway fails, the problem is the local link or Wi-Fi.","e":"ip route","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["wi-fi","google.com","route","external","problem","dns","gateway"]},{"i":"iv101","t":"Networking","q":"What is DNS and where is it configured?","a":"DNS resolves hostnames to IP addresses. Clients query the resolvers listed in /etc/resolv.conf (often managed by systemd-resolved or NetworkManager today). The static /etc/hosts file is consulted before DNS.","e":"hostnamectl","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["systemd-resolved","hostnames","etc/hosts","etc/resolv.conf","hostnamectl","resolvers","networkmanager"]},{"i":"iv102","t":"Networking","q":"How do you see which ports are listening on a machine?","a":"ss -tuln lists listening TCP and UDP sockets numerically; add -p (as root) to see the process behind each one. The older equivalent is netstat -tulpn.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["netstat","tulpn","numerically","udp","see","listening","tuln"]},{"i":"iv103","t":"Networking","q":"What is the difference between TCP and UDP?","a":"TCP is connection-oriented: ordered, acknowledged and retransmitted delivery - used by the web and SSH. UDP is connectionless: no delivery guarantee or ordering, but very low overhead - used by DNS queries, DHCP and voice/video.","e":"less /var/log/syslog","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["connection-oriented","voice/video","acknowledged","retransmitted","connectionless","overhead","udp"]},{"i":"iv104","t":"Networking","q":"How do you test whether a remote port is open?","a":"nc -zv host port does a direct check; telnet host port also works. For a web service, curl -Iv https://host tests the whole stack including DNS and TLS.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://bind9.readthedocs.io/en/stable/manpages.html","l":"Beginner","k":["telnet","tls","curl","port","direct","dns","status"]},{"i":"iv105","t":"Networking","q":"What does the /etc/hosts file do?","a":"It maps names to IP addresses locally and is checked before DNS. Each line is an IP followed by one or more names, for example 127.0.0.1 localhost. It is handy for overrides and for blocking names by pointing them at 127.0.0.1.","e":"file report","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["overrides","handy","localhost","locally","report","dns","followed"]},{"i":"iv106","t":"Networking","q":"What is a default gateway?","a":"The router your machine forwards traffic to when the destination is outside the local network. It appears as 'default via ...' in the output of ip route.","e":"ip route","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-route.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["route","default","outside","router","appears","machine","forwards"]},{"i":"iv107","t":"Networking","q":"How do you download a file from the command line?","a":"curl -O URL saves it under the remote name; wget URL does the same. curl is also the standard tool for testing HTTP APIs: curl -s https://api.example.com.","e":"file report","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/curl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["api.example.com","wget","saves","curl","report","url","apis"]},{"i":"iv108","t":"Networking","q":"What is the difference between ssh and scp?","a":"ssh opens an encrypted remote shell: ssh user@host. scp copies files over the same encrypted channel: scp file user@host:/path. sftp is the interactive file-transfer variant, and rsync over ssh is better for large or repeated syncs.","e":"rsync -av /data/ user@host:/backup/","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["file-transfer","sftp","scp","variant","rsync","syncs","encrypted"]},{"i":"iv109","t":"Networking","q":"What is an IP address?","a":"An IP address is a unique address assigned to a device on a network. It allows devices to find and communicate with each other.","e":"find /var -name \"*.log\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-address.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["var","address","find","communicate","name","unique","log"]},{"i":"iv110","t":"Networking","q":"What is the difference between IPv4 and IPv6?","a":"IPv4 is a 32-bit address (like 192.168.1.10) and is the most common. IPv6 is a 128-bit address created to solve the IPv4 address shortage.","e":"diff -u old.conf new.conf","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["shortage","ipv6","ipv4","diff","old.conf","new.conf","solve"]},{"i":"iv111","t":"Networking","q":"What is a subnet mask?","a":"A subnet mask separates the network part from the host part of an IP address. It tells whether two devices are in the same network.","e":"host example.com","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-address.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["subnet","separates","mask","example.com","host","part","tells"]},{"i":"iv112","t":"Networking","q":"What is DHCP?","a":"DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol) automatically assigns IP addresses and other network settings to devices. It removes manual configuration.","e":"host example.com","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-address.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["dhcp","example.com","assigns","dynamic","removes","host","protocol"]},{"i":"iv113","t":"Networking","q":"What is NAT?","a":"NAT (Network Address Translation) converts private IP addresses into a public IP address. It lets many devices share one public IP address.","e":"echo backup.sh | at 23:00","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-address.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["nat","backup.sh","translation","converts","echo","share","public"]},{"i":"iv114","t":"Networking","q":"What is the difference between a switch and a router?","a":"A switch connects devices within the same network. A router connects different networks and forwards data between them.","e":"diff -u old.conf new.conf","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/diff.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["router","diff","old.conf","new.conf","switch","connects","forwards"]},{"i":"iv115","t":"Networking","q":"What is a MAC address?","a":"A MAC address is a unique physical address assigned to a network card (NIC). It is used for communication inside the local network.","e":"sort users.txt | uniq -c","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["users.txt","nic","uniq","mac","sort","address","card"]},{"i":"iv116","t":"Networking","q":"What is ping?","a":"Ping is a network utility that checks connectivity between two devices. It also measures the response time.","e":"ping -c 4 8.8.8.8","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ping.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ping","connectivity","utility","measures","response","two","devices"]},{"i":"iv117","t":"Networking","q":"What is traceroute?","a":"Traceroute shows the path packets take to reach a destination. It helps find where network delays or failures happen.","e":"traceroute example.com","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/find.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["traceroute","example.com","failures","take","delays","happen","packets"]},{"i":"iv118","t":"Networking","q":"What are nslookup and dig?","a":"Both are DNS lookup tools. nslookup quickly resolves a name to an IP address; dig gives detailed DNS query results and is preferred for troubleshooting.","e":"nslookup example.com","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-address.8.html","l":"Scenario","k":["nslookup","lookup","dig","example.com","troubleshooting","quickly","preferred"]},{"i":"iv119","t":"Networking","q":"What is the difference between /etc/resolv.conf and /etc/hosts?","a":"/etc/resolv.conf stores the DNS server addresses the system queries. /etc/hosts maps names to IP addresses locally and is checked before DNS.","e":"diff -u old.conf new.conf","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["etc/hosts","etc/resolv.conf","diff","old.conf","new.conf","locally","dns"]},{"i":"iv120","t":"Networking","q":"What are the 7 layers of the OSI model?","a":"1. Physical, 2. Data Link, 3. Network, 4. Transport, 5. Session, 6. Presentation, 7. Application.","e":"ss -tuln","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/cat.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["tuln","presentation","transport","session","physical","link","application"]},{"i":"iv121","t":"Networking","q":"What are the common port numbers?","a":"22 - SSH, 21 - FTP, 53 - DNS, 80 - HTTP, 443 - HTTPS, 143 - IMAP, 3389 - RDP, 161 - SNMP, 2049 - NFS.","e":"ss -tuln","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["imap","rdp","snmp","tuln","nfs","ftp","dns"]},{"i":"iv122","t":"Networking","q":"What is a port number?","a":"A port number identifies a specific service or application on a device. It directs network traffic to the correct application.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/cat.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["identifies","number","port","directs","status","systemctl","nginx"]},{"i":"iv123","t":"Networking","q":"What is RIP?","a":"RIP is a distance-vector routing protocol. It chooses the best route based on hop count.","e":"sed -i.bak 's/old/new/g' file.txt","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["i.bak","rip","distance-vector","s/old/new/g","sed","file.txt","chooses"]},{"i":"iv124","t":"Networking","q":"What are OSPF and IGRP?","a":"OSPF is a link-state routing protocol. IGRP is Cisco's distance-vector routing protocol.","e":"stat app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/stat.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["link-state","ospf","igrp","distance-vector","cisco","routing","app.log"]},{"i":"iv125","t":"Networking","q":"What is the main DNS configuration file?","a":"On RHEL-based systems running BIND, the main DNS server configuration file is /etc/named.conf.","e":"file report","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["etc/named.conf","rhel-based","main","bind","dns","configuration","report"]},{"i":"iv126","t":"Networking","q":"Why do we need subnetting?","a":"Subnetting divides a network into smaller networks. It improves IP address management, performance, and security.","e":"rm -i old.txt","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-address.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["subnetting","old.txt","improves","divides","smaller","performance","management"]},{"i":"iv127","t":"Networking","q":"What is a VLAN?","a":"A VLAN divides one physical network into multiple logical networks. Devices in different VLANs cannot communicate without a router.","e":"diff -u old.conf new.conf","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/diff.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["vlan","old.conf","new.conf","divides","diff","cannot","multiple"]},{"i":"iv128","t":"Networking","q":"What are the classes of IP addresses?","a":"The five classes are A, B, C, D, and E. Classes A, B, and C are used for host addressing, D is for multicast, and E is experimental.","e":"host example.com","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-address.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["classes","multicast","example.com","experimental","host","five","addressing"]},{"i":"iv129","t":"Networking","q":"What is the network ID of 72.59.120.10/25?","a":"A /25 mask is 255.255.255.128, so the network ID is 72.59.120.0. Usable host addresses run from 72.59.120.1 to 72.59.120.126.","e":"host example.com","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["example.com","usable","host","mask","network","addresses","run"]},{"i":"iv130","t":"Networking","q":"What is the difference between private and public IP addresses?","a":"A private IP is used inside a local network and is not reachable from the internet. A public IP is assigned by the ISP and is used to communicate on the internet.","e":"diff -u old.conf new.conf","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-address.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["diff","old.conf","new.conf","public","reachable","private","isp"]},{"i":"iv131","t":"Networking","q":"What is the difference between HTTP and HTTPS?","a":"HTTP transfers data without encryption. HTTPS encrypts data using SSL/TLS, so the communication is secure.","e":"diff -u old.conf new.conf","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/diff.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ssl/tls","diff","encryption","old.conf","new.conf","http","encrypts"]},{"i":"iv132","t":"Networking","q":"What is Active Directory?","a":"Active Directory is Microsoft's directory service. It manages users, computers, groups, and permissions in a Windows network.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/group.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["active","computers","app.log","microsoft","stat","manages","directory"]},{"i":"iv133","t":"Networking","q":"How do you configure a switch?","a":"Assign a hostname and a management IP, configure VLANs, and enable the required ports. Always save the configuration after changes.","e":"hostnamectl","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["configure","hostname","hostnamectl","vlans","assign","save","management"]},{"i":"iv134","t":"Networking","q":"What is a network? What are LAN, MAN and WAN?","a":"A network is a group of connected devices that communicate and share data or resources. LAN covers a small local area like an office, MAN covers a city-level area, and WAN covers a large geographical area.","e":"source ~/.bashrc","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/group.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["city-level","wan","geographical","lan","bashrc","man","source"]},{"i":"iv135","t":"Networking","q":"What is the TCP/IP model and how is it different from OSI?","a":"TCP/IP commonly uses four layers: Link, Internet, Transport and Application. OSI is a seven-layer reference model. OSI Physical plus Data Link maps to TCP/IP Link, OSI Network maps to Internet, OSI Transport maps to Transport, and the upper OSI layers map to Application.","e":"diff -u old.conf new.conf","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["tcp/ip","seven-layer","osi","model","diff","old.conf","new.conf"]},{"i":"iv136","t":"Networking","q":"What is ARP?","a":"ARP maps an IPv4 address to a MAC address on the local network. Linux uses it when it knows the next-hop IP and needs that device's MAC address. Check the neighbor cache with ip neigh.","e":"echo backup.sh | at 23:00","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["next-hop","arp","neigh","neighbor","backup.sh","ipv4","echo"]},{"i":"iv137","t":"Networking","q":"What is Ethernet and what is a frame?","a":"Ethernet is a common LAN technology. A frame is the Layer-2 data unit that carries fields such as source MAC, destination MAC and payload. Switches forward Ethernet frames using MAC addresses.","e":"source ~/.bashrc","s":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ethernet","layer-2","payload","frame","carries","bashrc","source"]},{"i":"iv138","t":"Networking","q":"How does a switch forward frames?","a":"A switch learns which source MAC address is connected to each port and stores that in its MAC address table. It forwards a frame to the port matching the destination MAC. If the destination is unknown, it may flood the frame to other ports.","e":"source ~/.bashrc","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["frame","bashrc","switch","source","flood","unknown","learns"]},{"i":"iv139","t":"Networking","q":"How does a router forward packets?","a":"A router checks the destination IP address, looks up the best matching route in its routing table, and forwards the packet to the selected next hop or interface. Routers work mainly at Layer 3.","e":"echo backup.sh | at 23:00","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-address.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["router","packet","backup.sh","mainly","route","routing","echo"]},{"i":"iv140","t":"Networking","q":"Explain the TCP 3-way handshake.","a":"The client sends SYN, the server replies with SYN-ACK, and the client sends ACK. After these three steps, TCP connection state is established and application data can be exchanged.","e":"stat app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/cp.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["syn-ack","exchanged","syn","ack","tcp","replies","app.log"]},{"i":"iv141","t":"Networking","q":"What is the difference between a static route and a default route?","a":"A static route is manually configured for a specific destination. A default route is used only when no more specific route matches. View both with ip route.","e":"stat app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-route.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["static","manually","configured","route","stat","default","app.log"]},{"i":"iv142","t":"Networking","q":"What are eth0, ens33, enp0s3 and lo?","a":"They are network interface names. eth0 is the traditional style, ens33 and enp0s3 are predictable names, and lo is the loopback interface. Check the real names on a system with ip a. The loopback interface commonly uses 127.0.0.1/8 for IPv4.","e":"ping 127.0.0.1","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["enp0s3","eth0","ens33","predictable","loopback","ipv4","ping"]},{"i":"iv143","t":"Networking","q":"How do you configure IP address, gateway and DNS on Linux?","a":"The exact method depends on the distribution and network manager. On NetworkManager systems, use nmcli or nmtui for persistent configuration. Temporary testing can use ip addr and ip route, but those changes do not survive reboot unless saved in the system's network configuration.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.html","l":"Beginner","k":["addr","nmtui","nmcli","persistent","survive","networkmanager","reboot"]},{"i":"iv144","t":"Networking","q":"What are nmcli and nmtui?","a":"NetworkManager manages network connections. nmcli is its command-line interface, and nmtui is its terminal menu interface. Examples are nmcli device status and nmcli connection show.","e":"nmcli device status","s":"https://networkmanager.dev/docs/api/latest/nmcli.html","l":"Beginner","k":["nmcli","nmtui","command-line","networkmanager","device","menu","status"]},{"i":"iv145","t":"Networking","q":"How does SSH work?","a":"SSH provides encrypted remote login and secure transport, normally on TCP port 22 unless configured otherwise. The client establishes an encrypted connection to the server and authenticates with a password or SSH keys. Key-based authentication is common in production.","e":"less /var/log/syslog","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Scenario","k":["key-based","authenticates","less","var/log/syslog","encrypted","configured","otherwise"]},{"i":"iv146","t":"Networking","q":"A Linux server has no network. What do you check first?","a":"Troubleshoot layer by layer: check link and IP with ip link and ip a, routes with ip route, then gateway reachability, public IP reachability, DNS, listening ports, firewall rules and logs. A typical sequence is ip a, ip route, ping gateway, ping 8.8.8.8, dig example.com, ss -tulnp, then firewall and logs.","e":"sudo firewall-cmd --list-all","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-route.8.html","l":"Scenario","k":["tulnp","list-all","firewall-cmd","troubleshoot","dig","reachability","firewall"]},{"i":"iv147","t":"Networking","q":"What is a proxy server? Forward proxy vs reverse proxy?","a":"A proxy is an intermediary between a client and a destination service. A forward proxy commonly represents clients and can provide filtering, logging or caching. A reverse proxy commonly represents backend servers and routes client requests to them.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/journalctl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["reverse","intermediary","backend","proxy","caching","forward","logging"]},{"i":"iv148","t":"Networking","q":"What is a VPN?","a":"A VPN creates an authenticated and encrypted tunnel over another network, often the Internet. It lets a remote user or another network reach private resources securely. Common types are remote-access VPN and site-to-site VPN.","e":"source ~/.bashrc","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["remote-access","site-to-site","vpn","tunnel","securely","authenticated","bashrc"]},{"i":"iv149","t":"Networking","q":"What is NIC bonding or teaming?","a":"Bonding or teaming combines multiple network interfaces into one logical connection for redundancy, load distribution or both. The exact benefit depends on the bonding mode and switch configuration. Common modes include active-backup and LACP (802.3ad) where supported.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/cp.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["active-backup","bonding","teaming","lacp","redundancy","combines","modes"]},{"i":"iv150","t":"Networking","q":"What is a Linux bridge?","a":"A Linux bridge connects Layer-2 segments and forwards Ethernet frames using MAC learning, similar to a switch. It is common in virtualization to connect virtual machine interfaces to a physical LAN.","e":"echo backup.sh | at 23:00","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["layer-2","bridge","virtualization","backup.sh","segments","similar","ethernet"]},{"i":"iv151","t":"Networking","q":"What are the basic IPv6 address types?","a":"Common types are global unicast, link-local (fe80::/10), unique local (fc00::/7), multicast (ff00::/8) and loopback (::1). IPv6 does not use broadcast; it uses multicast and neighbor discovery instead. 2001:db8::/32 is reserved for documentation examples.","e":"ping 127.0.0.1","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["link-local","unicast","ipv6","fe80","fc00","ff00","db8"]},{"i":"iv152","t":"Networking","q":"How do you troubleshoot DNS?","a":"First separate IP connectivity from name resolution. If ping 8.8.8.8 works but ping example.com fails, suspect DNS. Check /etc/resolv.conf or resolvectl status, then test directly with dig example.com or nslookup example.com and verify the DNS server is reachable.","e":"nslookup example.com","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Scenario","k":["nslookup","resolvectl","etc/resolv.conf","suspect","example.com","reachable","dig"]},{"i":"iv153","t":"Networking","q":"DHCP is not assigning an IP. What do you check?","a":"Check whether the interface is up, the DHCP client is running, the VLAN or switch path is correct, and the DHCP server is available. If the client and server are on different subnets, also check the DHCP relay. NetworkManager and DHCP client logs can show the failed DORA exchange.","e":"diff -u old.conf new.conf","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/cp.1.html","l":"Scenario","k":["relay","dora","dhcp","subnets","old.conf","new.conf","vlan"]},{"i":"iv154","t":"Networking","q":"How do you check networking logs in Linux?","a":"On systemd systems, use journalctl. For NetworkManager, use journalctl -u NetworkManager, and for current boot messages use journalctl -b. Example: journalctl -u NetworkManager --since '10 min ago'.","e":"journalctl -u nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/journalctl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["networkmanager","journalctl","min","ago","messages","systemd","nginx"]},{"i":"iv155","t":"Networking","q":"How do you troubleshoot a network service?","a":"Confirm the service is running, listening on the expected address and port, reachable locally and remotely, and allowed by firewall or SELinux. Useful checks are systemctl status ssh, ss -lntp | grep :22, curl or nc tests, firewall rules and service logs.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Scenario","k":["lntp","remotely","reachable","locally","selinux","curl","status"]},{"i":"iv156","t":"Networking","q":"A port is listening locally but not reachable remotely. Why?","a":"A local listening socket only means the service is bound on that machine. Remote access can still fail because of firewall rules, routing, cloud security groups, ACLs, SELinux or the service binding only to localhost. Compare ss output on the server with a remote nc or curl test.","e":"sudo firewall-cmd --list-all","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/getfacl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["list-all","localhost","firewall-cmd","remote","listening","firewall","selinux"]},{"i":"iv157","t":"Networking","q":"Ping works but SSH does not. What would you check?","a":"Ping only proves basic IP/ICMP reachability; SSH uses TCP. Check that sshd is running, TCP/22 is listening, firewall and routing allow TCP/22, SSH is bound to the right address, and authentication is valid. Use systemctl status ssh or sshd, ss -lntp | grep :22, firewall-cmd --list-all and ssh -v user@server.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ip/icmp","tcp/22","proves","list-all","lntp","firewall-cmd","reachability"]},{"i":"iv158","t":"Storage","q":"How do you check disk space and directory sizes?","a":"df -h shows used and free space per mounted filesystem. du -sh /path shows how much one directory uses, and du -sh * sizes everything in the current directory.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/free.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["sizes","space","pwd","directory","filesystem","everything","mounted"]},{"i":"iv159","t":"Storage","q":"What does mounting a filesystem mean?","a":"Attaching a filesystem - from a partition, disk or network share - onto a directory in the tree, for example mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt/data. Everything written under that directory then goes to the device.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/mount.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["dev/sdb1","mnt/data","attaching","partition","onto","mount","written"]},{"i":"iv160","t":"Storage","q":"What is /etc/fstab?","a":"The table of filesystems mounted automatically at boot: device (best given as UUID=...), mount point, filesystem type, options, and dump/fsck fields. A broken line can drop the boot into emergency mode, so always test edits with mount -a.","e":"sudo mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt/data","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["dump/fsck","dev/sdb1","mnt/data","uuid","given","mount","emergency"]},{"i":"iv161","t":"Storage","q":"ext4, XFS and Btrfs in one line each?","a":"ext4 is the mature journaling default on many distros. XFS is tuned for large files and parallel workloads and is the RHEL default. Btrfs adds copy-on-write, snapshots and built-in volume features.","e":"cat /etc/os-release","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/lsblk.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["copy-on-write","built-in","btrfs","ext4","tuned","snapshots","xfs"]},{"i":"iv162","t":"Storage","q":"What is swap?","a":"Disk space the kernel uses as overflow memory when RAM runs out, and for hibernation. swapon --show lists active swap; a new swap area is prepared with mkswap and activated with swapon.","e":"sudo swapon /dev/sdb2","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/swapon.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["dev/sdb2","swapon","overflow","hibernation","mkswap","swap","activated"]},{"i":"iv163","t":"Storage","q":"What is LVM and why use it?","a":"Logical Volume Manager inserts a flexible layer between disks and filesystems: physical volumes (disks) join a volume group, from which logical volumes are carved out. The benefit is resizing, snapshots and moving volumes without repartitioning.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/group.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["resizing","repartitioning","carved","volumes","snapshots","inserts","python3"]},{"i":"iv164","t":"Storage","q":"What does fsck do, and when does it run?","a":"fsck checks and repairs filesystem consistency, and it must run on an UNMOUNTED filesystem. At boot it runs automatically when a filesystem is marked dirty or has passed its mount-count limit.","e":"sudo mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt/data","s":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.html","l":"Beginner","k":["mount-count","unmounted","fsck","dev/sdb1","mnt/data","consistency","dirty"]},{"i":"iv165","t":"Storage","q":"The disk is full - how do you find what is filling it?","a":"df -h shows which filesystem is full, then du -xh --max-depth=1 / | sort -h finds the biggest directories; /var/log and package caches are common culprits. Files that are deleted but still open keep holding space - find them with lsof +L1.","e":"which python3","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["max-depth","culprits","var/log","caches","lsof","full","biggest"]},{"i":"iv166","t":"Storage","q":"What is an inode?","a":"The filesystem's record for a file: permissions, owner, timestamps and pointers to the data blocks - everything except the name, which lives in directory entries. Running out of inodes makes a disk report full even with blocks free; check with df -i.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["inodes","timestamps","pointers","app.log","except","stat","lives"]},{"i":"iv167","t":"Storage","q":"How do you partition and format a disk from the command line?","a":"Identify the disk with lsblk, partition it with fdisk /dev/sdX or parted, format the new partition with mkfs.ext4 /dev/sdX1, then mount it. Every step destroys data if you pick the wrong disk - double-check the device name first.","e":"sudo parted -l","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/mount.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["dev/sdx1","double-check","dev/sdx","parted","mkfs.ext4","partition","fdisk"]},{"i":"iv168","t":"Storage","q":"What is a partition?","a":"A partition is a logical section of a storage device. It lets one disk be divided into multiple independent areas.","e":"echo backup.sh | at 23:00","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/journalctl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["backup.sh","partition","section","independent","echo","areas","multiple"]},{"i":"iv169","t":"Storage","q":"What is the difference between MBR and GPT?","a":"MBR supports disks up to 2 TB and a maximum of four primary partitions. GPT supports very large disks and many more partitions, and it is the modern standard.","e":"more file.txt","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/diff.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["mbr","gpt","file.txt","partitions","maximum","four","primary"]},{"i":"iv170","t":"Storage","q":"What is a file system?","a":"A file system is the method an operating system uses to store, organize, and manage files on a storage device. Examples are ext4, XFS, and Btrfs.","e":"file report","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/lsblk.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["organize","btrfs","ext4","xfs","method","report","manage"]},{"i":"iv171","t":"Storage","q":"Which file system does RHEL 7 use by default?","a":"RHEL 7 uses XFS as its default file system.","e":"which python3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/lsblk.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["rhel","xfs","python3","default","uses"]},{"i":"iv172","t":"Storage","q":"How do you check free memory?","a":"Use free -h to see RAM and swap usage in human-readable form.","e":"free -h","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/free.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["human-readable","free","swap","form","ram","see","usage"]},{"i":"iv173","t":"Storage","q":"How do you mount and unmount a directory?","a":"Use mount to attach a filesystem to a directory: mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt/data. Use umount /mnt/data to detach it safely before removing the device.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/mount.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["umount","detach","dev/sdb1","mnt/data","safely","attach","mount"]},{"i":"iv174","t":"Storage","q":"How do you increase a disk partition?","a":"First grow the partition itself (with growpart or fdisk), then expand the filesystem: resize2fs for ext4 or xfs_growfs for XFS. With LVM, use lvextend and then the same filesystem resize step.","e":"sudo resize2fs /dev/data/web","s":"https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html-single/configuring_and_managing_logical_volumes/index","l":"Beginner","k":["resize2fs","resize","dev/data/web","growpart","xfs_growfs","lvextend","partition"]},{"i":"iv175","t":"Storage","q":"What are the common LVM commands?","a":"pvcreate prepares a disk as a physical volume, vgcreate builds a volume group, lvcreate carves out a logical volume, lvextend grows it, and lvreduce shrinks it.","e":"sudo pvcreate /dev/sdb","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/group.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["pvcreate","dev/sdb","lvreduce","prepares","vgcreate","lvcreate","carves"]},{"i":"iv176","t":"Shell","q":"What is the shebang line?","a":"The first line of a script, like #!/bin/bash, that tells the kernel which interpreter should execute the file. Without it, your current shell interprets the script instead.","e":"head -1 script.sh","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ls.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["bin/bash","interprets","head","script.sh","script","interpreter","execute"]},{"i":"iv177","t":"Shell","q":"How do you make a script executable and run it?","a":"chmod +x script.sh, then run it with an explicit path: ./script.sh. The current directory is deliberately not in PATH, so the ./ is required. bash script.sh works without the execute bit.","e":"pwd","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["deliberately","explicit","execute","chmod","script.sh","pwd","bash"]},{"i":"iv178","t":"Shell","q":"How do variables and command substitution work?","a":"name=value sets a variable (no spaces around =); $name or ${name} reads it. $(command) captures a command's output, as in today=$(date +%F). Quote expansions - \"$name\" - so values with spaces survive.","e":"echo \"$HOME\"","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["variable","captures","survive","home","date","echo","expansions"]},{"i":"iv179","t":"Shell","q":"How do you read command-line arguments in a script?","a":"$1, $2... are the positional parameters, $# is their count, $@ is all of them and $0 is the script's own name. Validate them early: [ $# -lt 1 ] && { echo \"usage: $0 FILE\"; exit 1; }.","e":"echo \"$1\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["validate","positional","script","echo","early","parameters","exit"]},{"i":"iv180","t":"Shell","q":"What is the exit status and what is $?","a":"Every command returns 0 on success and a non-zero code on failure; $? holds the last command's status. Writing if command; then tests it directly, and well-behaved scripts end with exit 0 on success.","e":"echo $?","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["non-zero","well-behaved","exit","end","success","echo","status"]},{"i":"iv181","t":"Shell","q":"Give one example each of if, for and while in bash.","a":"if [ -f /etc/passwd ]; then echo yes; fi --- for f in *.log; do echo \"$f\"; done --- while read -r line; do echo \"$line\"; done < file. The [ ] command runs tests: -f file exists, -z empty string, = string equality, -eq numeric equality.","e":"passwd trainee","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["equality","etc/passwd","trainee","numeric","passwd","yes","empty"]},{"i":"iv182","t":"Shell","q":"What does set -euo pipefail do?","a":"Strict mode: -e exits the script at the first failing command, -u treats unset variables as errors, and -o pipefail makes a pipeline fail if ANY stage fails, not just the last. It surfaces bugs early and is expected in serious scripts.","e":"echo \"$HOME\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["surfaces","unset","pipefail","strict","serious","fail","bugs"]},{"i":"iv183","t":"Shell","q":"How do you schedule a script to run regularly?","a":"With cron: crontab -e, then a line like 0 2 * * * /home/akash/backup.sh (minute hour day month weekday). systemd timers are the modern alternative when you need dependencies and journal logging.","e":"crontab -l","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Scenario","k":["home/akash/backup.sh","weekday","cron","timers","crontab","logging","alternative"]},{"i":"iv184","t":"Shell","q":"How do you debug a shell script?","a":"bash -x script.sh prints every command as it executes; you can wrap a suspect block in set -x / set +x. Echo key variables along the way and check failures with echo $?.","e":"echo \"$HOME\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["suspect","executes","failures","wrap","script.sh","home","echo"]},{"i":"iv185","t":"Shell","q":"What is a shell script?","a":"A shell script is a text file that contains a sequence of Linux commands. It is used to automate repetitive tasks.","e":"bash -x script.sh","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/sed.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["repetitive","automate","script","script.sh","sequence","bash","shell"]},{"i":"iv186","t":"Shell","q":"Why do we use shell scripting?","a":"Shell scripting saves time by automating manual work. It makes tasks repeatable and reduces human error.","e":"bash -x script.sh","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["automating","repeatable","reduces","script.sh","scripting","saves","bash"]},{"i":"iv187","t":"Boot","q":"What happens when a Linux machine boots?","a":"The firmware (BIOS/UEFI) runs the bootloader (GRUB); GRUB loads the kernel and initramfs; the kernel mounts the root filesystem and starts PID 1 (systemd); systemd then starts units until the default target - multi-user or graphical - is reached.","e":"systemctl list-units --type=service","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["bios/uefi","multi-user","initramfs","graphical","list-units","firmware","bootloader"]},{"i":"iv188","t":"Services","q":"What is systemd and what is a unit?","a":"systemd is PID 1, the init system that starts and supervises everything else. A unit is a resource it manages: .service (daemons), .socket, .timer, .mount, .path and .target (boot stages that group units).","e":"systemctl list-units --type=service","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["supervises","list-units","daemons","init","timer","unit","stages"]},{"i":"iv189","t":"Services","q":"systemctl basics: start, stop, enable?","a":"systemctl start/stop/restart name controls a service right now. systemctl enable name links it to start at boot (disable removes the link). systemctl enable --now name does both at once, and status name shows its state plus recent log lines.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["start/stop/restart","disable","enable","systemctl","removes","status","start"]},{"i":"iv190","t":"Services","q":"What is the difference between systemctl enable and systemctl start?","a":"start changes the running system only - the effect is gone after reboot. enable only creates the boot-time symlink and does not start anything now. systemctl enable --now combines both.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["boot-time","enable","combines","symlink","reboot","gone","systemctl"]},{"i":"iv191","t":"Services","q":"How do you read a service's logs?","a":"journalctl -u name shows one unit's logs; journalctl -f follows new entries live; journalctl -b limits to the current boot; journalctl -p err shows errors and worse. Traditional text logs still live under /var/log.","e":"journalctl -u nginx","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["err","var/log","journalctl","worse","traditional","nginx","logs"]},{"i":"iv192","t":"Services","q":"Where do unit files live, and how do you edit one safely?","a":"Vendor units live in /usr/lib/systemd/system (or /lib/systemd/system on Debian/Ubuntu); local and custom units live in /etc/systemd/system and override them. Edit with systemctl edit name, which creates a drop-in instead of touching vendor files, then run systemctl daemon-reload.","e":"sudo systemctl daemon-reload","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["drop-in","usr/lib/systemd/system","lib/systemd/system","etc/systemd/system","override","daemon-reload","debian/ubuntu"]},{"i":"iv193","t":"Services","q":"What is a target?","a":"A systemd boot stage that groups units - the successor of runlevels. multi-user.target is the text-mode server state and graphical.target adds the desktop. systemctl get-default and set-default control which one boots; systemctl isolate switches immediately.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["text-mode","set-default","multi-user.target","successor","graphical.target","get-default","runlevels"]},{"i":"iv194","t":"Services","q":"How do you create your own service?","a":"Write /etc/systemd/system/myservice.service with a [Unit] section (Description, After=network.target), a [Service] section (ExecStart=/path/to/command, optionally Restart=on-failure) and an [Install] section (WantedBy=multi-user.target). Then systemctl daemon-reload and systemctl enable --now myservice.","e":"sudo systemctl daemon-reload","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["etc/systemd/system/myservice.service","path/to/command","on-failure","multi-user.target","network.target","execstart","myservice"]},{"i":"iv195","t":"Boot","q":"How do you see why boot was slow or what failed?","a":"systemd-analyze prints the total boot time; systemd-analyze blame ranks units by startup time; systemctl --failed lists failed units; journalctl -b -p err shows this boot's errors.","e":"journalctl -u nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["startup","systemd-analyze","blame","err","failed","journalctl","boot"]},{"i":"iv196","t":"Scheduling","q":"What is the difference between a systemd timer and cron?","a":"A timer unit triggers a service on a schedule and adds dependencies, journal logging and catch-up of missed runs (Persistent=true). cron is simpler and available everywhere; timers integrate with the rest of systemd.","e":"crontab -l","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/crontab.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["catch-up","simpler","cron","integrate","timer","triggers","persistent"]},{"i":"iv197","t":"Services","q":"What is a service?","a":"A service is a program that runs in the background to provide specific functionality. Examples include the SSH, Apache, and MySQL services.","e":"myfunc() { echo hi; }","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["myfunc","echo","functionality","ssh","service","provide","apache"]},{"i":"iv198","t":"Services","q":"What is the difference between systemctl and service?","a":"systemctl manages services on systemd-based systems, which covers most modern distros. service is the older SysVinit command that still works for compatibility on many systems.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/systemctl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["systemd-based","sysvinit","distros","systemctl","covers","compatibility","status"]},{"i":"iv199","t":"Boot","q":"What is GRUB?","a":"GRUB (Grand Unified Bootloader) is the Linux bootloader. It loads the kernel into memory and starts the operating system.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["grub","unified","bootloader","grand","uname","operating","loads"]},{"i":"iv200","t":"Services","q":"What is journald?","a":"journald is the logging service of systemd. It collects and stores system and service logs, which you read with journalctl.","e":"journalctl -u nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/journalctl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["journald","logging","journalctl","systemd","nginx","collects","stores"]},{"i":"iv201","t":"Services","q":"How do you host a web server on Linux?","a":"Install a web server like Apache or Nginx with the package manager, place the website files in its web root (for example /var/www/html), then start and enable the service with systemctl.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["var/www/html","web","website","server","status","systemctl","nginx"]},{"i":"iv202","t":"Security","q":"What are the most important log locations?","a":"The systemd journal (read it with journalctl), /var/log/syslog or /var/log/messages for general logs, /var/log/auth.log or /var/log/secure for logins and sudo, and per-service files like /var/log/nginx/. Kernel messages appear in the journal and /var/log/kern.log.","e":"journalctl -u nginx","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["per-service","var/log/nginx","var/log/kern.log","var/log/messages","var/log/auth.log","var/log/secure","var/log/syslog"]},{"i":"iv203","t":"Security","q":"How do you see who is logged in and the login history?","a":"who or w shows current sessions. last lists past logins from the wtmp database, and lastb lists FAILED attempts from btmp. journalctl _COMM=sshd filters SSH events.","e":"journalctl -u nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["lastb","btmp","comm","wtmp","attempts","journalctl","sshd"]},{"i":"iv204","t":"Scheduling","q":"What is the cron time format?","a":"Five fields - minute, hour, day of month, month, day of week - followed by the command. A * means 'every'. So 30 3 * * 0 runs at 03:30 every Sunday. Shortcuts like @daily and @reboot also exist.","e":"sudo reboot","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/crontab.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["shortcuts","reboot","sunday","week","five","month","minute"]},{"i":"iv205","t":"Scheduling","q":"crontab -e versus /etc/crontab versus /etc/cron.d?","a":"crontab -e edits the current user's personal crontab, whose lines have no user field. /etc/crontab and files in /etc/cron.d are system-wide and include a user field between the schedule and the command. Scripts dropped in cron.daily, cron.weekly and cron.monthly run on those schedules.","e":"crontab -l","s":"https://refspecs.linuxfoundation.org/FHS%5F3.0/fhs-3.0.html","l":"Beginner","k":["etc/crontab","etc/cron.d","system-wide","cron.monthly","cron.daily","cron.weekly","crontab"]},{"i":"iv206","t":"Scheduling","q":"A cron job did not run - how do you debug it?","a":"Check the log with grep CRON /var/log/syslog or journalctl -u cron, confirm the cron service is running, and remember cron uses a minimal environment: use absolute paths, set PATH explicitly, and capture errors with >> /tmp/job.log 2>&1.","e":"printenv HOME","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["tmp/job.log","printenv","var/log/syslog","cron","explicitly","remember","home"]},{"i":"iv207","t":"Security","q":"What is a Linux firewall and which tools manage it?","a":"It filters network traffic by rules. The kernel framework is nftables (iptables is the legacy one); friendly front-ends are ufw on Ubuntu (ufw allow 22, ufw enable) and firewalld on RHEL (firewall-cmd).","e":"sudo firewall-cmd --state","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["front-ends","ufw","nftables","iptables","firewalld","firewall-cmd","legacy"]},{"i":"iv208","t":"Security","q":"How do you harden SSH on a server?","a":"Use key-based login and disable passwords (PasswordAuthentication no), disable direct root login (PermitRootLogin no), optionally restrict users with AllowUsers, keep the system patched, and add fail2ban to slow brute-force attacks.","e":"sudo fail2ban-client status","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["brute-force","key-based","fail2ban-client","passwordauthentication","allowusers","fail2ban","permitrootlogin"]},{"i":"iv209","t":"Security","q":"What are SSH keys and how do you set them up?","a":"A key pair: the private key stays on your machine (~/.ssh/id_ed25519) and the public key goes on the server (~/.ssh/authorized_keys). Create them with ssh-keygen -t ed25519 and install the public key with ssh-copy-id user@host - after that, logins need no password.","e":"cat ~/.ssh/authorized_keys","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh-copy-id.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ssh/id_ed25519","ssh-copy-id","ssh/authorized_keys","ssh-keygen","ed25519","key","pair"]},{"i":"iv210","t":"Security","q":"What are SELinux and AppArmor?","a":"Mandatory Access Control systems that confine even root-owned processes by policy. SELinux (RHEL family) works with labels - check getenforce and the audit log for denials. AppArmor (Ubuntu, SUSE) confines programs by path-based profiles - check aa-status.","e":"aa-status","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["path-based","apparmor","root-owned","aa-status","getenforce","suse","mandatory"]},{"i":"iv211","t":"Security","q":"How do you investigate high load or memory trouble?","a":"uptime shows the load average - compare it with the CPU count. top or htop shows per-process CPU and memory, free -h shows RAM and swap usage, vmstat 1 shows trends, and dmesg -T reveals kernel events such as OOM-killer kills.","e":"ps aux","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/kill.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["per-process","oom-killer","vmstat","uptime","reveals","htop","dmesg"]},{"i":"iv212","t":"Security","q":"What is the difference between SELinux and a firewall?","a":"SELinux controls access inside the operating system - files, processes, and resources. A firewall controls incoming and outgoing network traffic.","e":"sudo firewall-cmd --list-all","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["incoming","outgoing","list-all","firewall-cmd","firewall","selinux","operating"]},{"i":"iv213","t":"Security","q":"What is firewalld?","a":"firewalld is the default firewall management service on RHEL-based systems. It manages rules dynamically with zones, using the firewall-cmd command.","e":"sudo firewall-cmd --state","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/sed.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["rhel-based","dynamically","firewalld","zones","firewall-cmd","firewall","state"]},{"i":"iv214","t":"Security","q":"What is the difference between iptables and a firewall?","a":"A firewall is the security system that filters network traffic. iptables is the classic command-line tool for writing those rules; modern systems use nftables instead.","e":"sudo firewall-cmd --list-all","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/diff.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["iptables","command-line","list-all","nftables","firewall-cmd","firewall","classic"]},{"i":"iv215","t":"Security","q":"How do you add an iptables rule?","a":"Use iptables -A to append a rule, for example: iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT allows SSH traffic. Save the rules afterwards so they survive a reboot.","e":"sudo reboot","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["iptables","dport","rule","afterwards","reboot","survive","append"]},{"i":"iv216","t":"Scheduling","q":"What is Anacron?","a":"Anacron runs scheduled tasks that were missed because the system was powered off. It is meant for machines that do not run all the time, like laptops.","e":"crontab -l","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["anacron","crontab","laptops","meant","machines","were","powered"]},{"i":"iv217","t":"Scheduling","q":"What is the difference between Cron and Anacron?","a":"Cron runs jobs at exact times and assumes the machine is always on. Anacron catches up on missed jobs when the system comes back, working with day-level schedules.","e":"crontab -l","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/crontab.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["day-level","anacron","cron","crontab","comes","assumes","catches"]},{"i":"iv218","t":"Security","q":"Your Linux system has malware. What will you do?","a":"Disconnect the system from the network, identify and remove the infected files, patch and update the system, and restore from a clean backup if needed. Review logs to find how it entered.","e":"sudo apt update","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/find.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["disconnect","infected","update","apt","identify","entered","patch"]},{"i":"iv219","t":"Security","q":"The server is down. How will you troubleshoot?","a":"Check power and network first, then whether the server is reachable with ping. Check services, logs, disk space, CPU, and memory. Restart the failed service and verify the application works.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Scenario","k":["reachable","restart","server","status","systemctl","nginx","power"]},{"i":"iv220","t":"Security","q":"SSH is not working. How will you troubleshoot?","a":"Check network connectivity, confirm sshd is running with systemctl status sshd, verify the firewall allows port 22, and review the SSH logs.","e":"systemctl status nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Scenario","k":["connectivity","sshd","ssh","status","systemctl","nginx","confirm"]},{"i":"iv221","t":"Files","q":"A service is not starting. What will you check?","a":"Check systemctl status for the error, read its logs with journalctl -u, and verify the configuration file for mistakes.","e":"journalctl -u nginx","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["mistakes","journalctl","nginx","systemctl","verify","configuration","error"]},{"i":"iv222","t":"Security","q":"The server cannot access the internet. What will you verify?","a":"Check the IP address, gateway, and DNS settings, then test step by step: ping the gateway, an external IP, and a domain name.","e":"ip route","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-address.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["route","external","dns","domain","gateway","settings","step"]},{"i":"iv223","t":"Security","q":"Users cannot log in. What will you check?","a":"Check whether the account is locked or expired, the password, permissions, and the authentication logs. Also verify that SSH or login services are running.","e":"stat -c %a app.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["expired","app.log","stat","ssh","locked","authentication","login"]},{"i":"iv224","t":"Security","q":"A filesystem became read-only. What could be the reason?","a":"Usually filesystem corruption or disk errors - the kernel remounts it read-only to protect data. Check dmesg and disk health, then run fsck from a rescue environment.","e":"printenv HOME","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/mount.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["read-only","printenv","remounts","dmesg","corruption","fsck","home"]},{"i":"iv225","t":"Files","q":"The server does not boot after a restart. What will you do?","a":"Check the bootloader (GRUB), the kernel, and the boot logs. Boot into rescue mode if needed, identify the failed component, and repair it.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["bootloader","grub","component","uname","boot","rescue","identify"]},{"i":"iv226","t":"Security","q":"How do you take a backup in Linux?","a":"Use tar for archives and rsync to sync files to another disk or server. Keep backups in a separate, secure location and test that restores work.","e":"tar -tf backup.tar","s":"https://man.archlinux.org/man/pacman.8","l":"Beginner","k":["restores","backup.tar","tar","sync","rsync","archives","location"]},{"i":"adv-backup-0","t":"Backup","q":"What is the difference between backup and synchronization?","a":"A backup is a recoverable copy kept with a retention and restore plan. Synchronization applies changes between locations; rsync --delete can also propagate accidental deletion. Preview changes, keep retained copies and test restoring to a separate lab directory. These are different goals even if the same tool is used.","e":"rsync -an --itemize-changes ./lab-source/ ./lab-restore/","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/rsync.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["propagate","accidental","itemize-changes","lab-source","lab-restore","recoverable","restoring"]},{"i":"adv-backup-1","t":"Backup","q":"What is the difference between rsync and cp?","a":"cp copies files; rsync selects changes using a quick check of size and modification time by default, or checksums with --checksum. Its delta-transfer algorithm can send changed portions on remote transfers; local transfers commonly use whole-file mode. Choose preservation flags deliberately and use a dry run before destructive synchronization.","e":"rsync -an --itemize-changes ./lab-source/ ./lab-restore/","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/rsync.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["delta-transfer","whole-file","preservation","itemize-changes","lab-source","lab-restore","rsync"]},{"i":"adv-backup-2","t":"Backup","q":"Why is rsync --dry-run important?","a":"--dry-run shows what rsync would change without changing the destination. It is especially useful before using --delete, which can remove files from the destination. Always preview destructive sync jobs.","e":"rsync -an --itemize-changes ./lab-source/ ./lab-restore/","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/rsync.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["dry-run","sync","itemize-changes","lab-source","lab-restore","rsync","destructive"]},{"i":"adv-backup-3","t":"Backup","q":"How do you create, list and extract a tar backup?","a":"Create a compressed archive with tar -czf backup.tar.gz /path. List contents with tar -tzf backup.tar.gz. Extract into a restore directory with tar -xzf backup.tar.gz -C /restore/.","e":"tar -tzf backup.tar.gz","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["backup.tar.gz","tzf","tar","extract","xzf","czf","create"]},{"i":"adv-backup-4","t":"Backup","q":"Why should backups have checksums?","a":"sha256sum can detect a changed archive when compared with a trusted previously recorded digest. It does not prove who created the archive and does not replace testing a restore. Store the checksum separately from the archive when that fits the threat model.","e":"sha256sum backup.tar.gz","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/sha256sum.1.html","l":"Scenario","k":["sha256sum","backup.tar.gz","checksum","detect","separately","previously","digest"]},{"i":"adv-backup-5","t":"Backup","q":"What makes a backup plan production-ready?","a":"For a recoverable operational backup, define the data and consistency requirements, retained copies, recovery destination and restore test. rsync alone does not specify retention or application consistency. Treat the plan as an operational checklist, not a guarantee that any archive is production-ready.","e":"rsync -an --itemize-changes ./lab-source/ ./lab-restore/","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/rsync.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["production-ready","checklist","itemize-changes","lab-source","lab-restore","recoverable","restore"]},{"i":"adv-ubuntu-packages-0","t":"Packages","q":"What is the difference between apt and dpkg?","a":"apt works with repositories and resolves dependencies, so it is normally the right tool for installing and upgrading packages on Ubuntu/Debian. dpkg is the lower-level tool for installing or inspecting individual .deb files. Use apt first when possible.","e":"apt policy bash","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["lower-level","ubuntu/debian","upgrading","policy","dpkg","repositories","apt"]},{"i":"adv-ubuntu-packages-1","t":"Packages","q":"What is the difference between apt update and apt upgrade?","a":"apt update refreshes the package index from repositories. apt upgrade installs available upgrades for installed packages. Update tells apt what is available; upgrade applies the changes.","e":"apt policy bash","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["upgrade","refreshes","upgrades","policy","update","repositories","apt"]},{"i":"adv-ubuntu-packages-2","t":"Packages","q":"What is the difference between apt remove, purge and autoremove?","a":"remove uninstalls a package but can leave configuration files. purge removes the package and its configuration where applicable. autoremove removes dependencies that are no longer needed.","e":"apt policy bash","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["autoremove","purge","uninstalls","policy","remove","apt","bash"]},{"i":"adv-ubuntu-packages-3","t":"Packages","q":"How do you inspect a package before installing it?","a":"Use apt search to find packages, apt show for details, and apt policy to see installed and candidate versions plus repositories. These commands do not install anything.","e":"apt policy bash","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["policy","repositories","candidate","bash","apt","install","details"]},{"i":"adv-ubuntu-packages-4","t":"Packages","q":"How do you inspect installed .deb packages with dpkg?","a":"Use dpkg -l to list installed packages, dpkg -L package to list files from a package, and dpkg -S /path/file to find which package owns a file.","e":"apt policy bash","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/dpkg.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["path/file","policy","dpkg","packages","bash","package","apt"]},{"i":"adv-ubuntu-packages-5","t":"Packages","q":"Why is apt usually preferred over dpkg -i?","a":"dpkg -i installs a local .deb but does not resolve repository dependencies by itself. apt understands repositories and dependencies, so it is safer for normal package installation.","e":"apt policy bash","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/stable/apt/apt.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["repository","safer","policy","dpkg","repositories","apt","deb"]},{"i":"adv-advanced-storage-0","t":"Storage","q":"What are RAID, NFS, Samba and swap used for?","a":"RAID combines disks for redundancy or performance depending on level, but is not a backup. NFS and SMB provide network file access; Samba implements SMB services and clients. Swap stores memory pages outside ordinary RAM; it can be a disk device/file or compressed RAM such as zram. Assess sustained swap activity with memory and I/O evidence.","e":"cat /proc/mdstat","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/mdadm.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["device/file","sustained","samba","proc/mdstat","nfs","smb","zram"]},{"i":"adv-advanced-storage-1","t":"Storage","q":"What are common RAID levels?","a":"RAID 0 stripes data for performance without redundancy. RAID 1 mirrors disks. RAID 5 uses distributed parity and needs at least three disks. RAID 6 uses double parity, and RAID 10 combines mirroring and striping.","e":"cat /proc/mdstat","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/mdadm.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["parity","proc/mdstat","raid","redundancy","stripes","combines","mirrors"]},{"i":"adv-advanced-storage-2","t":"Storage","q":"How do you check Linux software RAID status?","a":"Check /proc/mdstat for a quick status and rebuild progress. Use mdadm --detail /dev/md0 for detailed information about the array and member disks.","e":"cat /proc/mdstat","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/mdadm.8.html","l":"Scenario","k":["dev/md0","mdadm","proc/mdstat","rebuild","array","progress","quick"]},{"i":"adv-advanced-storage-3","t":"Storage","q":"What is the difference between NFS and Samba?","a":"NFS is commonly used for Linux and Unix network file shares. Samba provides SMB/CIFS shares, commonly for Windows clients. Both share files over the network, but they use different protocols.","e":"smbclient -L server -U labuser","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/nfs.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["smb/cifs","smbclient","samba","nfs","labuser","server","protocols"]},{"i":"adv-advanced-storage-4","t":"Storage","q":"What is swap and when is it a warning sign?","a":"Swap provides backing for memory pages, usually on disk or a swap file, but compressed RAM swap such as zram is also possible. Swap allocation alone does not prove current pressure. Check available memory, swap-in/swap-out rates and latency before diagnosing thrashing.","e":"swapon --show","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/swapon.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["swap-in/swap-out","swapon","thrashing","latency","swap","zram","diagnosing"]},{"i":"adv-advanced-storage-5","t":"Storage","q":"How do you mount an NFS or SMB share?","a":"After creating the mount point and verifying authorization, an NFS example is sudo mount -t nfs server:/share /mnt/share. An SMB example is sudo mount -t cifs //server/share /mnt/share -o username=labuser; enter credentials at a prompt or use a protected credentials file, never a public command line. These contact a server and change mounts. Verify with findmnt /mnt/share; support packages and access rights are required.","e":"findmnt /mnt/share","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/mount.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["mnt/share","server/share","authorization","smb","share","cifs","findmnt"]},{"i":"adv-boot-recovery-0","t":"Boot","q":"Why can a bad /etc/fstab entry stop Linux from booting normally?","a":"A required fstab mount that is missing or invalid can fail the normal boot path on systemd systems, depending on options such as nofail. Check the UUID, filesystem and mount point with lsblk -f and blkid. Review with findmnt --verify before any approved mount -a; actual mounting changes state and may contact network servers.","e":"lsblk -f","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/fstab.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["nofail","fstab","lsblk","invalid","blkid","normal","findmnt"]},{"i":"adv-boot-recovery-1","t":"Boot","q":"What is the normal Linux boot sequence?","a":"A common Linux boot path is firmware (BIOS/UEFI), a bootloader such as GRUB, the kernel with an initramfs when used, PID 1 such as systemd, then services and login. GRUB and systemd are common, not universal. Locate the failing stage before changing boot configuration.","e":"systemd-analyze critical-chain","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/systemd-analyze.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["bios/uefi","critical-chain","systemd-analyze","initramfs","firmware","bootloader","grub"]},{"i":"adv-boot-recovery-2","t":"Boot","q":"How do you reset a forgotten root password on many RHEL-family systems?","a":"Interrupt GRUB, add rd.break to the kernel line, boot, remount /sysroot read-write with mount -o remount,rw /sysroot, chroot into /sysroot, run passwd, then touch /.autorelabel so SELinux relabels on reboot. Exit cleanly and reboot. Use this only on systems you are authorized to administer.","e":"man passwd","s":"https://www.redhat.com/en/blog/recover-root-passwd","l":"Beginner","k":["read-write","rd.break","sysroot","chroot","autorelabel","relabels","administer"]},{"i":"adv-boot-recovery-3","t":"Boot","q":"What is the difference between rescue and emergency mode?","a":"Rescue mode starts more of the system and usually mounts filesystems, while emergency mode starts a very minimal environment for severe recovery. Use emergency mode when normal rescue mode cannot mount or start required pieces.","e":"systemd-analyze critical-chain","s":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.html","l":"Beginner","k":["critical-chain","emergency","systemd-analyze","rescue","severe","pieces","mode"]},{"i":"adv-boot-recovery-4","t":"Boot","q":"How do you test fstab changes before rebooting?","a":"Review fstab with findmnt --verify --verbose first. On systemd systems run sudo systemctl daemon-reload after editing, then use sudo mount -a only after reviewing the effects. This may mount new filesystems and contact network servers; it is not a no-change syntax check or a guarantee that the next boot succeeds.","e":"findmnt --verify --verbose","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/findmnt.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["no-change","verbose","succeeds","daemon-reload","fstab","findmnt","verify"]},{"i":"adv-boot-recovery-5","t":"Boot","q":"When should you use fsck?","a":"fsck dispatches to filesystem-specific checkers. Plan offline checks or repair using the filesystem documentation and backups; do not repair an in-use filesystem. XFS uses xfs_repair, not a meaningful repair via fsck.xfs. Root-filesystem repair can require rescue media. Never assume one generic repair command is safe for every filesystem.","e":"man fsck","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/fsck.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["in-use","root-filesystem","fsck","filesystem-specific","meaningful","fsck.xfs","dispatches"]},{"i":"adv-performance-0","t":"Performance","q":"How do you troubleshoot a slow Linux server?","a":"Start with evidence instead of killing processes blindly. Check load and CPU with uptime/top, memory and swap with free -h, disk I/O with iostat or pidstat, then correlate with application logs. Identify the responsible process and follow the change procedure.","e":"iostat -xz 1 3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/iostat.1.html","l":"Scenario","k":["uptime/top","iostat","correlate","i/o","blindly","pidstat","swap"]},{"i":"adv-performance-1","t":"Performance","q":"What does load average mean?","a":"Linux load average reflects runnable tasks and tasks in uninterruptible sleep (often disk I/O), averaged over 1, 5 and 15 minutes. It is not CPU percentage. Compare with CPU capacity, CPU utilization and I/O evidence; high load can come from blocked work even when CPUs are idle.","e":"cat /proc/loadavg","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/proc_loadavg.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["proc/loadavg","average","averaged","utilization","idle","runnable","i/o"]},{"i":"adv-performance-2","t":"Performance","q":"How do you find a high-CPU process?","a":"Use top for a live view, or ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head for a quick list. Identify what the process is and why it is busy before stopping it.","e":"ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ps.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["cpu","head","sort","stopping","busy","aux","process"]},{"i":"adv-performance-3","t":"Performance","q":"How do you check memory pressure?","a":"Use free -h to see RAM and swap usage. If available memory is low and swap is heavily used, find the memory-heavy process with top or ps aux --sort=-%mem | head.","e":"free -h","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/free.1.html","l":"Scenario","k":["memory-heavy","heavily","mem","free","memory","swap","low"]},{"i":"adv-performance-4","t":"Performance","q":"How do you investigate disk I/O problems?","a":"Use iostat -xz 1 3 for device statistics and pidstat -d 1 3 for per-task I/O. The initial iostat report is usually since boot, unlike later interval reports. await includes queue and service time; high %util is not a universal saturation test for parallel SSDs or RAID. Correlate latency with workload and application logs.","e":"iostat -xz 1 3","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/iostat.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["per-task","iostat","interval","saturation","i/o","await","queue"]},{"i":"adv-performance-5","t":"Performance","q":"What do you check when a filesystem is full?","a":"Use df -h to find the full filesystem and df -i to check inode exhaustion. Then use du -xhd1 on the filesystem to find large directories before deleting anything.","e":"df -i","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/df.1.html","l":"Scenario","k":["xhd1","exhaustion","full","inode","deleting","filesystem","anything"]},{"i":"adv-logging-0","t":"Logs","q":"What is the difference between rsyslog and logrotate?","a":"rsyslog collects, filters and forwards log messages. logrotate rotates, compresses and retains old logs so they do not fill the filesystem. Use journalctl or the right /var/log file to investigate, and logrotate -d to preview rotation safely.","e":"logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.conf","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/logrotate.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["logrotate","rsyslog","etc/logrotate.conf","rotates","investigate","rotation","var/log"]},{"i":"adv-logging-1","t":"Logs","q":"Where are common Linux logs stored?","a":"Systemd systems use the journal, read with journalctl. Traditional logs often live under /var/log, such as /var/log/messages on RHEL-family systems and /var/log/syslog on Debian-family systems.","e":"journalctl -b","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/journalctl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["var/log/messages","rhel-family","debian-family","var/log","var/log/syslog","journal","journalctl"]},{"i":"adv-logging-2","t":"Logs","q":"How do you read logs for one service?","a":"Use journalctl -u service-name. Add -f to follow live logs or --since '1 hour ago' to limit the time range. Example: journalctl -u nginx -f.","e":"journalctl -u nginx --since \"1 hour ago\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/journalctl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["service-name","ago","hour","journalctl","range","nginx","logs"]},{"i":"adv-logging-3","t":"Logs","q":"How do you create a test log message?","a":"Use logger 'message'. It writes a test message through the system logging path, which helps verify rsyslog or journal configuration.","e":"logger -t linux-lab \"test event\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/logger.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["logger","linux-lab","message","event","rsyslog","logging","test"]},{"i":"adv-logging-4","t":"Logs","q":"How do you test logrotate safely?","a":"Use logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.conf for a dry run. It shows what would rotate without changing files. Force rotation only when you understand the impact.","e":"logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.conf","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/logrotate.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["rotate","logrotate","etc/logrotate.conf","rotation","force","impact","dry"]},{"i":"adv-logging-5","t":"Logs","q":"How can logs fill a disk and how do you prevent it?","a":"High-volume application or system logs can consume all space in /var or /var/log. logrotate retention, compression and correct application log levels help prevent it. Check space with df -h and large directories with du.","e":"logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.conf","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/logrotate.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["high-volume","logrotate","etc/logrotate.conf","consume","prevent","var/log","retention"]},{"i":"adv-web-servers-0","t":"Web servers","q":"How do you troubleshoot a 502 from Nginx or Apache?","a":"Validate the web server config, check the service, confirm the upstream application is listening, and read the error log. Useful checks are nginx -t or apachectl -t, systemctl status, ss -tuln, curl and the site error logs.","e":"nginx -t","s":"https://nginx.org/en/docs/beginners_guide.html","l":"Scenario","k":["validate","apachectl","upstream","tuln","curl","nginx","config"]},{"i":"adv-web-servers-1","t":"Web servers","q":"What is the difference between Apache and Nginx?","a":"Apache HTTP Server and Nginx can both serve content and proxy requests. Their configuration and module models differ; select based on the application requirements, measured workload and team support rather than assuming one is always faster.","e":"nginx -t","s":"https://nginx.org/en/docs/beginners_guide.html","l":"Beginner","k":["apache","differ","assuming","faster","serve","requirements","workload"]},{"i":"adv-web-servers-2","t":"Web servers","q":"Why should you test web server config before reload?","a":"Check syntax with nginx -t or apachectl -t before a reload. A successful syntax check does not prove upstream health or that a request will work. A failed Nginx reload normally keeps the old configuration running; a restart is more disruptive. Review logs and test the endpoint after an approved reload.","e":"nginx -t","s":"https://nginx.org/en/docs/switches.html","l":"Beginner","k":["reload","disruptive","endpoint","apachectl","upstream","restart","successful"]},{"i":"adv-web-servers-3","t":"Web servers","q":"What is a virtual host or server block?","a":"It is the configuration that maps a site name, port and document root or proxy target to a website or application. Apache calls it a virtual host; Nginx commonly calls it a server block.","e":"nginx -t","s":"https://nginx.org/en/docs/beginners_guide.html","l":"Beginner","k":["virtual","block","website","document","host","server","nginx"]},{"i":"adv-web-servers-4","t":"Web servers","q":"How do you check whether web ports are listening?","a":"Use ss -tuln on the server and look for ports 80 and 443. Then test locally with curl -I and remotely from another machine to separate service binding from firewall or routing problems.","e":"nginx -t","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Scenario","k":["remotely","locally","tuln","curl","ports","routing","problems"]},{"i":"adv-web-servers-5","t":"Web servers","q":"What do common 4xx and 5xx HTTP errors mean?","a":"4xx errors are client-side or request-related, such as 403 forbidden or 404 not found. 5xx errors are server-side or upstream-related, such as 500, 502 and 503. Check logs to find the exact cause.","e":"nginx -t","s":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html","l":"Beginner","k":["client-side","request-related","server-side","upstream-related","forbidden","found","cause"]},{"i":"adv-mysql-0","t":"Databases","q":"An application cannot connect to MySQL. What do you check?","a":"Check the MySQL service, listening address and port, credentials, database name, user privileges and database logs before changing application code. For backup and restore practice, use mysqldump and a separate test database. Never expose production passwords in commands or docs.","e":"mysql -u lab_user -p -e \"SHOW DATABASES;\"","s":"https://docs.oracle.com/cd/E17952_01/mysql-8.4-en/mysql.html","l":"Scenario","k":["mysql","lab_user","mysqldump","database","databases","credentials","expose"]},{"i":"adv-mysql-1","t":"Databases","q":"Why might MySQL be called mysql or mysqld in systemctl?","a":"The installed service name is package- and distribution-dependent. Do not assume mysql, mysqld or mariadb exists: inspect systemctl list-unit-files and the package documentation, then check the discovered unit. MariaDB is a related but separate database product.","e":"mysql -u lab_user -p -e \"SHOW DATABASES;\"","s":"https://docs.oracle.com/cd/E17952_01/mysql-8.4-en/mysql.html","l":"Beginner","k":["distribution-dependent","mysqld","list-unit-files","mysql","mariadb","lab_user","database"]},{"i":"adv-mysql-2","t":"Databases","q":"How should you create a MySQL application user?","a":"Create a user for the required host and grant only the privileges the application needs. Avoid using root for applications, and never place production passwords in shared docs or command history.","e":"mysql -u lab_user -p -e \"SHOW DATABASES;\"","s":"https://docs.oracle.com/cd/E17952_01/mysql-8.4-en/creating-accounts.html","l":"Beginner","k":["mysql","lab_user","databases","create","privileges","application","user"]},{"i":"adv-mysql-3","t":"Databases","q":"How do you list databases and tables?","a":"Connect with mysql -u user -p, then use SHOW DATABASES, USE database_name and SHOW TABLES. These commands help confirm that the expected database exists.","e":"mysql -u lab_user -p -e \"SHOW DATABASES;\"","s":"https://docs.oracle.com/cd/E17952_01/mysql-8.4-en/show-databases.html","l":"Beginner","k":["database_name","tables","database","databases","lab_user","mysql","expected"]},{"i":"adv-mysql-4","t":"Databases","q":"How do you back up and restore a MySQL database?","a":"A logical lab backup is mysqldump -u lab_user -p labdb > backup.sql. Redirection creates or overwrites backup.sql and dumps may contain private data. For InnoDB, --single-transaction can help consistency, but concurrent DDL and other storage engines need a separate plan. Restore only into an authorized separate test database with mysql -u lab_user -p lab_restore < backup.sql after inspecting the SQL. Required privileges and GTID options vary.","e":"mysql -u lab_user -p -e \"SHOW DATABASES;\"","s":"https://docs.oracle.com/cd/E17952_01/mysql-8.4-en/mysqldump.html","l":"Beginner","k":["sql","single-transaction","backup.sql","overwrites","innodb","concurrent","lab_restore"]},{"i":"adv-mysql-5","t":"Databases","q":"How do you check whether MySQL is responding?","a":"Check the installed unit, listener and error log. mysqladmin -u lab_user -p ping checks whether the server is alive; it may report the server is alive even when access is denied, so it does not prove credentials or query privileges. Use an authenticated test query separately.","e":"mysql -u lab_user -p -e \"SHOW DATABASES;\"","s":"https://docs.oracle.com/cd/E17952_01/mysql-8.4-en/mysqladmin.html","l":"Scenario","k":["mysqladmin","mysql","lab_user","authenticated","databases","credentials","listener"]},{"i":"adv-advanced-security-0","t":"Security","q":"chmod looks correct but a web app still gets Permission denied. What do you check?","a":"Check SELinux context and AVC denials before changing broad permissions or disabling SELinux. Use ls -Z, restorecon, semanage fcontext where appropriate, and ausearch -m AVC. Also review ACLs, sudo rules and fail2ban status when relevant.","e":"ls -Z /var/www/html","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/restorecon.8.html","l":"Scenario","k":["semanage","fcontext","var/www/html","restorecon","ausearch","avc","denials"]},{"i":"adv-advanced-security-1","t":"Security","q":"What are SELinux enforcing, permissive and disabled modes?","a":"Enforcing applies SELinux policy and logs denials. Permissive logs what would be denied but does not block it. Disabled turns SELinux off. Prefer fixing contexts or policy instead of disabling SELinux.","e":"ls -Z /var/www/html","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/getenforce.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["permissive","enforcing","var/www/html","disabled","denials","selinux","prefer"]},{"i":"adv-advanced-security-2","t":"Security","q":"What is an SELinux context?","a":"An SELinux context labels files, processes and ports with security information. A web server can be denied even when Unix permissions look correct. Use ls -Z to view contexts and restorecon to restore expected labels.","e":"ls -Z /var/www/html","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/restorecon.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["var/www/html","restorecon","context","selinux","labels","look","denied"]},{"i":"adv-advanced-security-3","t":"Security","q":"How do you find SELinux denials?","a":"Use ausearch -m AVC -ts recent or check audit logs. AVC messages show what was denied and help you choose a targeted fix instead of disabling SELinux.","e":"sudo ausearch -m AVC -ts recent","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ausearch.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ausearch","avc","recent","disabling","selinux","audit","messages"]},{"i":"adv-advanced-security-4","t":"Security","q":"Why should sudoers be edited with visudo?","a":"visudo checks sudoers syntax before saving, reducing the chance of locking yourself out of sudo. Grant least privilege instead of broad ALL rules.","e":"sudo visudo -c","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/visudo.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["visudo","sudoers","privilege","yourself","reducing","chance","least"]},{"i":"adv-advanced-security-5","t":"Security","q":"What are ACLs in Linux?","a":"ACLs extend normal owner/group/other permissions by allowing specific users or groups to have their own permissions. Use getfacl to view ACLs and setfacl to change them.","e":"getfacl ./lab-file","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/getfacl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["owner/group/other","getfacl","lab-file","setfacl","acls","extend","normal"]},{"i":"adv-advanced-security-6","t":"Security","q":"What does fail2ban do?","a":"fail2ban watches logs for repeated authentication failures and can temporarily block offending sources using firewall rules. Check jail status with fail2ban-client status sshd.","e":"sudo fail2ban-client status sshd","s":"https://www.fail2ban.org/wiki/index.php/Main_Page","l":"Beginner","k":["fail2ban","fail2ban-client","temporarily","offending","jail","sshd","failures"]},{"i":"adv-identity-0","t":"Identity","q":"What is the difference between local Linux accounts and LDAP/Active Directory accounts?","a":"Local accounts exist only on one machine, while LDAP or Active Directory provides centrally managed identity. Linux can integrate through SSSD and realmd, often using Kerberos for authentication. Use id, getent, klist and SSSD tools when troubleshooting.","e":"getent passwd labuser","s":"https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html-single/configuring_authentication_and_authorization_in_rhel/index","l":"Beginner","k":["centrally","ldap","integrate","realmd","klist","active","getent"]},{"i":"adv-identity-1","t":"Identity","q":"What is the difference between LDAP and Active Directory?","a":"LDAP is a directory access protocol. Active Directory is Microsoft's directory service and commonly uses LDAP plus Kerberos and other services. Linux can authenticate against either with the right integration.","e":"getent passwd labuser","s":"https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html-single/configuring_authentication_and_authorization_in_rhel/index","l":"Beginner","k":["ldap","active","getent","labuser","kerberos","authenticate","passwd"]},{"i":"adv-identity-2","t":"Identity","q":"What is Kerberos used for?","a":"Kerberos provides ticket-based authentication, commonly in Active Directory environments. Commands like kinit request a ticket and klist shows current tickets.","e":"getent passwd labuser","s":"https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html-single/configuring_authentication_and_authorization_in_rhel/index","l":"Beginner","k":["ticket-based","kinit","kerberos","klist","getent","labuser","passwd"]},{"i":"adv-identity-3","t":"Identity","q":"What are SSSD and realmd?","a":"SSSD connects Linux authentication and identity lookup to directory services such as LDAP or AD. realmd helps discover and join supported domains. Together they allow centralized logins on Linux.","e":"getent passwd labuser","s":"https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html-single/configuring_authentication_and_authorization_in_rhel/index","l":"Beginner","k":["realmd","sssd","centralized","getent","ldap","labuser","passwd"]},{"i":"adv-identity-4","t":"Identity","q":"How do you test whether a directory user resolves on Linux?","a":"Use id username and getent passwd username. If the user does not resolve, check SSSD, domain join status, DNS and network connectivity.","e":"getent passwd labuser","s":"https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html-single/configuring_authentication_and_authorization_in_rhel/index","l":"Beginner","k":["resolve","getent","sssd","labuser","connectivity","passwd","user"]},{"i":"adv-identity-5","t":"Identity","q":"Why is time important for AD or Kerberos login?","a":"Kerberos is sensitive to clock skew. If the Linux server clock is too far from the domain controller, authentication can fail. Check timedatectl and chrony synchronization.","e":"getent passwd labuser","s":"https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html-single/configuring_authentication_and_authorization_in_rhel/index","l":"Beginner","k":["skew","kerberos","chrony","getent","labuser","timedatectl","synchronization"]},{"i":"adv-monitoring-0","t":"Monitoring","q":"What is the difference between monitoring and troubleshooting?","a":"Monitoring collects metrics, checks availability, shows trends and sends alerts. Troubleshooting finds the reason behind the alert using Linux tools such as top, vmstat, iostat, pidstat and logs. Zabbix, Grafana and Nagios help detect change; they do not replace diagnosis.","e":"systemctl status zabbix-agent","s":"https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/fundamentals/","l":"Scenario","k":["zabbix","monitoring","vmstat","grafana","detect","zabbix-agent","troubleshooting"]},{"i":"adv-monitoring-1","t":"Monitoring","q":"What are Zabbix, Grafana and Nagios used for?","a":"Zabbix collects metrics and sends alerts, Grafana builds dashboards and visualizations, and Nagios runs host/service checks with plugins. They can work together or separately depending on the environment.","e":"systemctl status zabbix-agent","s":"https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/fundamentals/","l":"Beginner","k":["zabbix","host/service","grafana","visualizations","nagios","dashboards","zabbix-agent"]},{"i":"adv-monitoring-2","t":"Monitoring","q":"What is the difference between metrics, logs and alerts?","a":"Metrics are numeric measurements over time, logs are event records, and alerts notify when a rule or threshold is triggered. Good troubleshooting uses all three together.","e":"systemctl status zabbix-agent","s":"https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/fundamentals/","l":"Beginner","k":["metrics","zabbix-agent","alerts","triggered","troubleshooting","numeric","notify"]},{"i":"adv-monitoring-3","t":"Monitoring","q":"How do you check a monitoring agent?","a":"Check the agent service, such as systemctl status zabbix-agent, then inspect its logs with journalctl -u zabbix-agent. Also verify the monitoring server is allowed to reach the agent port.","e":"systemctl status zabbix-agent","s":"https://assets.nagios.com/downloads/nagioscore/docs/nagioscore/4/en/plugins.html","l":"Scenario","k":["agent","monitoring","zabbix-agent","journalctl","status","systemctl","inspect"]},{"i":"adv-monitoring-4","t":"Monitoring","q":"What is a Nagios plugin?","a":"A Nagios plugin is a small check program that tests a host or service, such as check_ping or check_http. It returns a status that Nagios can alert on.","e":"systemctl status zabbix-agent","s":"https://assets.nagios.com/downloads/nagioscore/docs/nagioscore/4/en/plugins.html","l":"Beginner","k":["plugin","check_ping","check_http","nagios","zabbix-agent","status","systemctl"]},{"i":"adv-monitoring-5","t":"Monitoring","q":"Why should monitoring ports be checked?","a":"Dashboards and agents listen on network ports. If the web UI or agent is unreachable, use ss -tuln on the server and a remote curl or nc test to separate service, firewall and routing issues.","e":"systemctl status zabbix-agent","s":"https://assets.nagios.com/downloads/nagioscore/docs/nagioscore/4/en/plugins.html","l":"Scenario","k":["agent","dashboards","unreachable","zabbix-agent","tuln","curl","issues"]},{"i":"adv-ansible-0","t":"Automation","q":"What are inventory, modules and playbooks in Ansible?","a":"Inventory lists the managed hosts, modules perform actions, and YAML playbooks describe the desired state. Good playbooks are repeatable and preferably idempotent, so running them again does not make unnecessary changes.","e":"ansible-inventory -i inventory.ini --graph","s":"https://docs.ansible.com/projects/ansible/latest/inventory_guide/intro_inventory.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ansible-inventory","graph","inventory","preferably","inventory.ini","repeatable","idempotent"]},{"i":"adv-ansible-1","t":"Automation","q":"Why is Ansible called agentless?","a":"Ansible normally manages hosts over SSH and does not require a permanent agent on each managed Linux server. The control node runs modules against hosts from inventory.","e":"ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml --syntax-check","s":"https://docs.ansible.com/projects/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_intro.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ansible","agent","inventory","inventory.ini","ansible-playbook","site.yml","syntax-check"]},{"i":"adv-ansible-2","t":"Automation","q":"What is the difference between an ad-hoc command and a playbook?","a":"An ad-hoc command runs one module against selected hosts, such as ansible all -m ping. A playbook is a YAML file that describes an ordered set of tasks and desired state.","e":"ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml --syntax-check","s":"https://docs.ansible.com/projects/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_intro.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ad-hoc","playbook","ansible","inventory.ini","ansible-playbook","site.yml","syntax-check"]},{"i":"adv-ansible-3","t":"Automation","q":"What does idempotent mean in Ansible?","a":"An idempotent task converges to its desired state without unnecessary changes when repeated. Many Ansible modules support this, but arbitrary shell/command tasks and some playbooks do not. Test repeated runs in a disposable inventory; do not infer idempotency from YAML alone.","e":"ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml --syntax-check","s":"https://docs.ansible.com/projects/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_intro.html","l":"Beginner","k":["shell/command","ansible","idempotent","converges","idempotency","infer","inventory"]},{"i":"adv-ansible-4","t":"Automation","q":"What does ansible-playbook --check do?","a":"--check simulates changes for modules that support it, but it is not a security sandbox or a universal no-write guarantee. A task with check_mode: false can still make real changes, unsupported modules may not predict anything, and registered-variable-dependent tasks can differ. Inspect the entire playbook, inventory and roles before running check mode.","e":"ansible-playbook -i lab.ini site.yml --check --limit labhost","s":"https://docs.ansible.com/projects/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_checkmode.html","l":"Scenario","k":["no-write","registered-variable-dependent","lab.ini","labhost","playbook","ansible-playbook","simulates"]},{"i":"adv-ansible-5","t":"Automation","q":"How can Ansible install a package and start a service?","a":"Use the package module to ensure the package is present and the service module to ensure the service is started and enabled. A playbook can apply the same state to many servers.","e":"ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml --syntax-check","s":"https://docs.ansible.com/projects/ansible/latest/collections/ansible/builtin/package_module.html","l":"Beginner","k":["playbook","inventory.ini","ansible-playbook","site.yml","syntax-check","ensure","present"]},{"i":"adv-virtualization-0","t":"Containers & VMs","q":"What is the difference between a Docker container and a KVM virtual machine?","a":"Containers isolate processes while sharing the host kernel; KVM virtual machines run a separate guest kernel/OS with hardware virtualization support. Images, namespaces and cgroups are not a blanket security guarantee. The isolation needed depends on kernel, runtime configuration and workload; VMs also require hardening.","e":"docker ps -a","s":"https://docs.docker.com/get-started/docker-concepts/the-basics/what-is-a-container/","l":"Beginner","k":["kernel/os","kvm","docker","blanket","hardening","virtualization","isolation"]},{"i":"adv-virtualization-1","t":"Containers & VMs","q":"What is the difference between a Docker image and a container?","a":"An image is a packaged filesystem and configuration template. A container is an instance created from an image and can be running or stopped. Many containers can be created from one image.","e":"docker ps -a","s":"https://docs.docker.com/get-started/docker-concepts/the-basics/what-is-a-container/","l":"Beginner","k":["docker","image","container","template","packaged","instance","stopped"]},{"i":"adv-virtualization-2","t":"Containers & VMs","q":"How do you publish a Docker container port?","a":"docker run -d --name lab-web -p 127.0.0.1:8080:80 nginx maps a container port to loopback port 8080, limiting ordinary access to the host. Without an explicit host address, publishing commonly binds all host interfaces and may expose the service externally. This pulls an image if absent, starts a container and changes network state; review image and firewall policy first.","e":"docker port lab-web","s":"https://docs.docker.com/engine/network/port-publishing/","l":"Beginner","k":["lab-web","docker","externally","absent","explicit","container","loopback"]},{"i":"adv-virtualization-3","t":"Containers & VMs","q":"How do you debug a running container?","a":"Use docker logs -f name to follow logs and docker exec -it name sh to open a shell when the image includes one. Also inspect ports and configuration with docker inspect.","e":"docker logs --tail 50 lab-web","s":"https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/container/","l":"Beginner","k":["lab-web","exec","docker","tail","inspect","logs","image"]},{"i":"adv-virtualization-4","t":"Containers & VMs","q":"How do you stop and remove a container?","a":"Use docker stop name for a graceful stop and docker rm name to remove a stopped container. Use docker ps -a to see stopped containers.","e":"docker ps -a","s":"https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/container/","l":"Beginner","k":["docker","container","graceful","stop","remove","stopped","see"]},{"i":"adv-virtualization-5","t":"Containers & VMs","q":"How do you manage KVM virtual machines with virsh?","a":"Use virsh list --all to list VMs, virsh start name to start one, virsh shutdown name for a graceful shutdown, and virsh console name for console access when configured.","e":"virsh list --all","s":"https://libvirt.org/manpages/virsh.html","l":"Beginner","k":["virsh","vms","shutdown","graceful","configured","console","all"]},{"i":"adv-time-0","t":"Time","q":"Why is time synchronization important on Linux servers?","a":"Correct time matters for ticket authentication, TLS validity periods, log correlation and distributed work. Chrony is a common NTP implementation. timedatectl status reports system time settings; chronyc tracking and chronyc sources -v report Chrony state. Enabling a synchronization service is not by itself proof that the clock is synchronized.","e":"chronyc tracking","s":"https://chrony-project.org/doc/4.6/chronyc.html","l":"Beginner","k":["chrony","validity","correlation","chronyc","tracking","synchronized","ntp"]},{"i":"adv-time-1","t":"Time","q":"How do you check Linux time and timezone?","a":"Use timedatectl. It shows current time, timezone and whether network time synchronization is enabled.","e":"timedatectl status","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/timedatectl.1.html","l":"Scenario","k":["timezone","timedatectl","time","synchronization","status","enabled","current"]},{"i":"adv-time-2","t":"Time","q":"How do you set the Linux timezone?","a":"Use timedatectl set-timezone with an IANA timezone, such as sudo timedatectl set-timezone Asia/Kolkata. Verify with timedatectl afterward.","e":"timedatectl list-timezones","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/timedatectl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["set-timezone","asia/kolkata","list-timezones","timezone","timedatectl","iana","afterward"]},{"i":"adv-time-3","t":"Time","q":"What is chronyd?","a":"chronyd is the daemon for the Chrony NTP implementation. It keeps the system clock synchronized with configured time sources.","e":"chronyc tracking","s":"https://chrony-project.org/doc/4.6/chronyc.html","l":"Beginner","k":["chrony","chronyd","chronyc","tracking","synchronized","ntp","daemon"]},{"i":"adv-time-4","t":"Time","q":"How do you check chrony synchronization?","a":"chronyc tracking reports estimated offset, reference source and synchronization state. chronyc sources -v lists sources and selection markers. Reachability alone does not prove accurate time; check the selected source and measurement history.","e":"chronyc sources -v","s":"https://chrony-project.org/doc/4.6/chronyc.html","l":"Scenario","k":["markers","chronyc","synchronization","sources","reachability","source","selection"]},{"i":"adv-time-5","t":"Time","q":"When would you use chronyc makestep?","a":"chronyc makestep steps the clock when there is a significant offset and the administrator decides an immediate correction is appropriate. Use caution because time jumps can affect applications and logs.","e":"chronyc tracking","s":"https://chrony-project.org/doc/4.6/chronyc.html","l":"Beginner","k":["makestep","chronyc","decides","caution","tracking","significant","immediate"]},{"i":"adv-aws-0","t":"Cloud","q":"Which AWS services should a Linux administrator know first?","a":"Start with EC2 for virtual servers, EBS for disks, VPC/subnets/route tables for networking, security groups for traffic control, IAM for permissions, S3 for object storage and CloudWatch for monitoring. Know how these map to normal Linux admin work.","e":"man ssh","s":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/concepts.html","l":"Beginner","k":["vpc/subnets/route","know","cloudwatch","iam","admin","ebs","man"]},{"i":"adv-aws-1","t":"Cloud","q":"What is EC2?","a":"EC2 is AWS's virtual server service. A Linux admin can choose an instance, attach storage, configure networking and connect with SSH, subject to security groups and key access.","e":"man ssh","s":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/concepts.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ec2","aws","man","attach","ssh","configure","instance"]},{"i":"adv-aws-2","t":"Cloud","q":"What is the difference between EBS and S3?","a":"EBS provides block storage volumes that attach to EC2 instances like disks. S3 is object storage for files and backups, accessed through APIs or tools such as aws s3 cp and sync.","e":"man ssh","s":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ebs/latest/userguide/what-is-ebs.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ebs","instances","volumes","aws","man","sync","attach"]},{"i":"adv-aws-3","t":"Cloud","q":"What is a security group?","a":"An EC2 security group is a stateful allow-rule filter for traffic associated with supported resources/network interfaces. It has no explicit deny rule; return traffic for allowed flows is permitted. It is separate from network ACLs and the Linux host firewall. Review both inbound and outbound exposure.","e":"man ssh","s":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/ec2-security-groups.html","l":"Beginner","k":["allow-rule","resources/network","stateful","inbound","outbound","deny","explicit"]},{"i":"adv-aws-4","t":"Cloud","q":"What is IAM and why are roles preferred for EC2?","a":"IAM controls AWS identity and permissions. An EC2 instance role lets the instance call approved AWS APIs without storing long-term access keys on the server.","e":"man ssh","s":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/iam-roles-for-amazon-ec2.html","l":"Beginner","k":["long-term","iam","role","ec2","aws","man","storing"]},{"i":"adv-aws-5","t":"Cloud","q":"What is CloudWatch used for?","a":"CloudWatch collects AWS metrics, logs and alarms. A Linux admin can use it to monitor EC2 health, EBS performance, logs and alert thresholds.","e":"man ssh","s":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/prerequisites.html","l":"Beginner","k":["cloudwatch","ebs","metrics","aws","man","ec2","ssh"]},{"i":"concept-653","t":"Kernel","q":"What is the kernel versus a Linux distribution?","a":"The kernel manages hardware, scheduling, memory and system-call interfaces. A distribution combines a kernel with user-space tools, package management and defaults. A kernel version is not a distribution release number.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/index.html","l":"Beginner","k":["system-call","user-space","distribution","combines","uname","scheduling","manages"]},{"i":"quiz-topic-653","t":"Kernel","q":"What does uname -r identify?","a":"Kernel release. The kernel manages hardware, scheduling, memory and system-call interfaces. A distribution combines a kernel with user-space tools, package management and defaults. A kernel version is not a distribution release number.","e":"uname -r","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/index.html","l":"Beginner","k":["system-call","user-space","uname","combines","scheduling","manages","release"]},{"i":"concept-655","t":"Kernel","q":"What do loadable kernel modules do?","a":"Modules add kernel functionality such as drivers without always needing a new kernel build. modprobe resolves module dependencies and configuration; lsmod shows loaded modules. Loading or unloading drivers can disrupt hardware or services.","e":"lsmod","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/modprobe.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["lsmod","modprobe","unloading","disrupt","module","modules","resolves"]},{"i":"quiz-topic-655","t":"Kernel","q":"What does modprobe handle beyond inserting one module file?","a":"Dependencies and configuration. Modules add kernel functionality such as drivers without always needing a new kernel build. modprobe resolves module dependencies and configuration; lsmod shows loaded modules. Loading or unloading drivers can disrupt hardware or services.","e":"lsmod","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/modprobe.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["modprobe","lsmod","unloading","disrupt","module","resolves","dependencies"]},{"i":"concept-657","t":"Kernel","q":"How should persistent sysctl changes be handled?","a":"sysctl reads or writes kernel tunables. Read a value first, understand the kernel/version and workload, test in a lab, and use the distro sysctl.d configuration for persistence when appropriate. A runtime write alone does not establish reboot persistence. Never bulk-apply copied tuning values.","e":"sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/sysctl.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["sysctl","kernel/version","bulk-apply","net.ipv4.ip_forward","tunables","sysctl.d","persistence"]},{"i":"quiz-topic-657","t":"Kernel","q":"What does sysctl inspect?","a":"Kernel parameters. sysctl reads or writes kernel tunables. Read a value first, understand the kernel/version and workload, test in a lab, and use the distro sysctl.d configuration for persistence when appropriate. A runtime write alone does not establish reboot persistence. Never bulk-apply copied tuning values.","e":"sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/sysctl.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["sysctl","kernel/version","bulk-apply","net.ipv4.ip_forward","tunables","sysctl.d","persistence"]},{"i":"concept-659","t":"Kernel","q":"What is a Linux namespace?","a":"Namespaces isolate a view of resources such as PIDs, mounts, network devices, IPC, hostname and user IDs. They do not automatically constrain resource consumption or provide a complete security boundary on their own.","e":"lsns","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/namespaces.7.html","l":"Beginner","k":["lsns","ipc","constrain","namespaces","isolate","hostname","consumption"]},{"i":"quiz-topic-659","t":"Kernel","q":"What is the central purpose of namespaces?","a":"Resource views. Namespaces isolate a view of resources such as PIDs, mounts, network devices, IPC, hostname and user IDs. They do not automatically constrain resource consumption or provide a complete security boundary on their own.","e":"lsns","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/namespaces.7.html","l":"Beginner","k":["lsns","namespaces","ipc","constrain","isolate","hostname","consumption"]},{"i":"concept-661","t":"Kernel","q":"What is cgroup v2 used for?","a":"The unified cgroup hierarchy organizes processes for resource accounting and control. Controllers can govern CPU, memory and I/O. Availability and delegation depend on kernel and service-manager setup. Read the current hierarchy before changing limits.","e":"cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cgroup.controllers","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/cgroup-v2.html","l":"Beginner","k":["service-manager","cgroup","controllers","sys/fs/cgroup/cgroup.controllers","organizes","govern","delegation"]},{"i":"quiz-topic-661","t":"Kernel","q":"What do cgroups primarily provide?","a":"Resource accounting and control. The unified cgroup hierarchy organizes processes for resource accounting and control. Controllers can govern CPU, memory and I/O. Availability and delegation depend on kernel and service-manager setup. Read the current hierarchy before changing limits.","e":"cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cgroup.controllers","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/cgroup-v2.html","l":"Beginner","k":["service-manager","cgroup","controllers","sys/fs/cgroup/cgroup.controllers","organizes","govern","delegation"]},{"i":"concept-663","t":"Kernel","q":"How are memory.high and memory.max different?","a":"In cgroup v2, memory.high throttles and applies reclaim pressure when exceeded; memory.max is a hard limit and can lead to a cgroup OOM kill when reclaim cannot reduce usage. Both need workload testing; changing a limit can hurt or kill running work.","e":"cat /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.stat","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/cgroup-v2.html","l":"Beginner","k":["sys/fs/cgroup/memory.stat","memory.high","memory.max","throttles","reclaim","cgroup","oom"]},{"i":"quiz-topic-663","t":"Kernel","q":"Which cgroup v2 file sets the hard memory limit?","a":"memory.max. In cgroup v2, memory.high throttles and applies reclaim pressure when exceeded; memory.max is a hard limit and can lead to a cgroup OOM kill when reclaim cannot reduce usage. Both need workload testing; changing a limit can hurt or kill running work.","e":"cat /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.stat","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/cgroup-v2.html","l":"Beginner","k":["sys/fs/cgroup/memory.stat","cgroup","memory.max","memory.high","throttles","reclaim","oom"]},{"i":"concept-665","t":"Kernel","q":"What does cgroup v2 cpu.max contain?","a":"For applicable schedulers it expresses CPU bandwidth as quota and period. The string max means no bandwidth limit. This is separate from relative CPU weight and CPU affinity; it is not a count of physical cores.","e":"cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cgroup.controllers","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/cgroup-v2.html","l":"Beginner","k":["max","sys/fs/cgroup/cgroup.controllers","cores","schedulers","affinity","quota","expresses"]},{"i":"concept-666","t":"Security","q":"How are capabilities different from unrestricted root?","a":"Linux divides privileged operations into capability checks. Capabilities can be held in process sets or file attributes, subject to namespace and security rules. Giving a binary a capability is a security change, not a harmless convenience. CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE permits binding privileged Internet ports.","e":"getcap /usr/bin/ping","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/capabilities.7.html","l":"Beginner","k":["getcap","usr/bin/ping","capabilities","namespace","harmless","cap_net_bind_service","divides"]},{"i":"quiz-topic-666","t":"Security","q":"Which capability concerns privileged Internet ports?","a":"CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE. Linux divides privileged operations into capability checks. Capabilities can be held in process sets or file attributes, subject to namespace and security rules. Giving a binary a capability is a security change, not a harmless convenience. CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE permits binding privileged Internet ports.","e":"getcap /usr/bin/ping","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/capabilities.7.html","l":"Beginner","k":["getcap","usr/bin/ping","capability","cap_net_bind_service","namespace","harmless","internet"]},{"i":"concept-668","t":"Security","q":"What does PAM do?","a":"PAM is a framework for pluggable authentication-related modules. Service-specific stacks distinguish auth, account, password and session work. A broken stack can lock out users; keep tested recovery access and use distro-supported tools. PAM and NSS are different layers.","e":"man pam.conf","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/pam.conf.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["pam","authentication-related","service-specific","distro-supported","pam.conf","pluggable","distinguish"]},{"i":"quiz-topic-668","t":"Security","q":"What is PAM?","a":"Pluggable authentication framework. PAM is a framework for pluggable authentication-related modules. Service-specific stacks distinguish auth, account, password and session work. A broken stack can lock out users; keep tested recovery access and use distro-supported tools. PAM and NSS are different layers.","e":"man pam.conf","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/pam.conf.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["pam","authentication-related","service-specific","distro-supported","pam.conf","pluggable","distinguish"]},{"i":"concept-670","t":"Identity","q":"What does NSS do during an id lookup?","a":"The Name Service Switch chooses sources for databases such as passwd, group and hosts according to nsswitch.conf. getent follows the configured lookup path; reading /etc/passwd alone misses directory-provided identities. Authentication is a separate step.","e":"getent passwd labuser","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/nsswitch.conf.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["directory-provided","nsswitch.conf","identities","lookup","etc/passwd","getent","labuser"]},{"i":"quiz-topic-670","t":"Identity","q":"What can getent passwd include beyond local /etc/passwd?","a":"Configured identity sources. The Name Service Switch chooses sources for databases such as passwd, group and hosts according to nsswitch.conf. getent follows the configured lookup path; reading /etc/passwd alone misses directory-provided identities. Authentication is a separate step.","e":"getent passwd labuser","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/nsswitch.conf.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["directory-provided","etc/passwd","getent","nsswitch.conf","identities","passwd","labuser"]},{"i":"concept-672","t":"Networking","q":"How do DNS resolution and routing differ?","a":"DNS maps names to records. Routing chooses a path for packets to an address. A name-resolution failure and an unreachable IP are different problems; test the configured resolver and then the route without assuming ping failure proves DNS is broken.","e":"ip route get 192.0.2.10","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-route.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["name-resolution","unreachable","proves","get","resolver","assuming","chooses"]},{"i":"concept-673","t":"Networking","q":"What does a TCP LISTEN socket prove?","a":"It shows that a local socket is listening on a bound address and port. It does not prove remote reachability, firewall permission, successful TLS negotiation or application health. A loopback-only listener is normally unavailable directly from another host.","e":"ss -lnt","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["loopback-only","lnt","negotiation","unavailable","tls","socket","reachability"]},{"i":"quiz-topic-673","t":"Networking","q":"What does ss -lnt establish?","a":"A local listening socket. It shows that a local socket is listening on a bound address and port. It does not prove remote reachability, firewall permission, successful TLS negotiation or application health. A loopback-only listener is normally unavailable directly from another host.","e":"ss -lnt","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["lnt","loopback-only","negotiation","unavailable","tls","reachability","listener"]},{"i":"concept-675","t":"Networking","q":"What are Linux bridges and VLAN interfaces?","a":"A bridge connects layer-2 interfaces and forwards frames. A VLAN interface handles tagged traffic for a VLAN on a parent link. They solve different tasks and require matching network configuration; creating or changing them can sever remote access.","e":"ip -d link show","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-link.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["layer-2","bridge","sever","vlan","interface","interfaces","link"]},{"i":"concept-676","t":"Networking","q":"Why does a tcpdump capture require privacy care?","a":"Packet captures can contain credentials, application data and personal traffic. Capture only authorized interfaces/filters, limit duration or packet count, and protect the output. Reading a saved capture is safer than collecting unrelated traffic.","e":"tcpdump -nn -r lab.pcap -c 10","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/tcpdump.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["tcpdump","interfaces/filters","lab.pcap","capture","captures","unrelated","safer"]},{"i":"concept-677","t":"Networking","q":"What is policy routing?","a":"Linux can select routing tables using routing-policy rules, not only a single destination table. Inspect ip rule show and relevant tables before editing. Changes can affect management connectivity and depend on routing priorities and selectors.","e":"ip rule show","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ip-rule.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["routing-policy","rule","selectors","connectivity","routing","priorities","tables"]},{"i":"concept-678","t":"Security","q":"How are nftables and firewalld related?","a":"nftables is a packet-filtering framework and nft is its management tool. firewalld manages firewall policy with zones and services and can use nftables as its backend. Mixing unmanaged rules with a manager can produce unexpected policy; inspect ownership and avoid blindly flushing rules.","e":"sudo nft list ruleset","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/bookworm/nftables/nft.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["nft","packet-filtering","ruleset","unmanaged","flushing","nftables","firewalld"]},{"i":"concept-679","t":"Security","q":"Why is an SSH host-key warning not a prompt to delete known_hosts blindly?","a":"A changed host key can mean a legitimate rebuild or a connection to an impostor. Verify the new key fingerprint through an independent trusted channel before updating the specific known_hosts entry. ssh-keyscan retrieves keys but does not authenticate them.","e":"ssh-keygen -F server.example","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh-keygen.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ssh-keyscan","known_hosts","server.example","ssh-keygen","impostor","retrieves","key"]},{"i":"quiz-topic-679","t":"Security","q":"What should precede accepting a changed SSH host key?","a":"Independent verification of the new key. A changed host key can mean a legitimate rebuild or a connection to an impostor. Verify the new key fingerprint through an independent trusted channel before updating the specific known_hosts entry. ssh-keyscan retrieves keys but does not authenticate them.","e":"ssh-keygen -F server.example","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh-keygen.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["ssh-keyscan","server.example","ssh-keygen","impostor","known_hosts","retrieves","key"]},{"i":"concept-681","t":"Storage","q":"What is LUKS encryption and what does it not solve?","a":"LUKS manages encrypted block-device metadata and key slots through cryptsetup. Once opened, the mapped device can be used by a filesystem or LVM. Encryption at rest does not replace backups, access controls or protection while unlocked. Formatting a device destroys existing data.","e":"sudo cryptsetup luksDump /dev/loop0","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/cryptsetup.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["luks","block-device","cryptsetup","luksdump","dev/loop0","mapped","unlocked"]},{"i":"concept-682","t":"Storage","q":"How do ext4 and XFS administration differ?","a":"Both are Linux filesystems but their maintenance tools and supported operations differ. Use tune2fs/dumpe2fs for ext-family metadata and xfs_info/xfs_repair for XFS. Do not assume a filesystem can shrink or be repaired with an unrelated tool. Check filesystem type and the installed manual first.","e":"findmnt -no FSTYPE /","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/xfs_repair.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["tune2fs/dumpe2fs","ext-family","xfs_info/xfs_repair","fstype","findmnt","unrelated","differ"]},{"i":"quiz-topic-682","t":"Storage","q":"Which tool is intended for XFS repair?","a":"xfs_repair. Both are Linux filesystems but their maintenance tools and supported operations differ. Use tune2fs/dumpe2fs for ext-family metadata and xfs_info/xfs_repair for XFS. Do not assume a filesystem can shrink or be repaired with an unrelated tool. Check filesystem type and the installed manual first.","e":"findmnt -no FSTYPE /","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/xfs_repair.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["tune2fs/dumpe2fs","ext-family","xfs_info/xfs_repair","fstype","findmnt","xfs_repair","unrelated"]},{"i":"concept-684","t":"Storage","q":"Why is RAID not a backup?","a":"RAID redundancy can preserve operation after some disk failures, but deletion, corruption, compromise and site loss can affect all members. A separate backup with retention and tested recovery solves a different failure class. RAID 0 has no redundancy.","e":"cat /proc/mdstat","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/mdadm.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["compromise","solves","proc/mdstat","raid","preserve","corruption","redundancy"]},{"i":"quiz-topic-684","t":"Storage","q":"Why can RAID not replace backups?","a":"It does not protect retained copies against deletion or corruption. RAID redundancy can preserve operation after some disk failures, but deletion, corruption, compromise and site loss can affect all members. A separate backup with retention and tested recovery solves a different failure class. RAID 0 has no redundancy.","e":"cat /proc/mdstat","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/mdadm.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["compromise","solves","proc/mdstat","raid","corruption","preserve","retained"]},{"i":"concept-686","t":"Storage","q":"How do loop devices help storage labs?","a":"A loop device exposes a regular file as a block device, useful for isolated filesystem experiments. Inspect existing mappings first and use a dedicated disposable file. Attaching, formatting or detaching the wrong device is destructive.","e":"losetup --list","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/losetup.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["losetup","loop","formatting","isolated","disposable","detaching","destructive"]},{"i":"concept-687","t":"Storage","q":"What does discard or fstrim do?","a":"Discard tells a supporting storage stack that blocks are no longer in use. fstrim issues discard for unused filesystem blocks; effectiveness and performance depend on the device and stack. It is a state-changing maintenance operation, not a secure erase guarantee.","e":"lsblk -D","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/fstrim.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["state-changing","discard","fstrim","erase","lsblk","unused","effectiveness"]},{"i":"concept-688","t":"Backup","q":"Why should a restore be tested separately from archive creation?","a":"Successfully writing an archive does not prove every required file, permission or application state can be recovered. List the archive, verify a trusted checksum, and perform a restore into an isolated destination. Application-consistent database backups need their own plan.","e":"tar -tzf backup.tar.gz","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tar.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["application-consistent","backup.tar.gz","recovered","tzf","checksum","isolated","archive"]},{"i":"concept-689","t":"Boot","q":"What is an initramfs?","a":"An initramfs supplies early userspace needed before the real root filesystem is available, often including drivers or storage setup. The contents and rebuild procedure are distro-specific. A missing driver, encryption hook or wrong root parameter can stop boot before normal services run.","e":"cat /proc/cmdline","s":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/initrd.html","l":"Beginner","k":["distro-specific","proc/cmdline","initramfs","userspace","encryption","rebuild","hook"]},{"i":"concept-690","t":"Boot","q":"Why distinguish a systemd target from a service?","a":"A service describes a managed process; a target groups units into a synchronization point or system state. Dependencies and ordering are distinct: Requires or Wants do not alone imply After. Do not isolate a target remotely without understanding which services it stops.","e":"systemctl get-default","s":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.unit.html","l":"Beginner","k":["get-default","imply","remotely","isolate","synchronization","target","distinct"]},{"i":"concept-691","t":"Services","q":"How are systemd socket activation and ordinary startup different?","a":"A socket unit can have systemd listen for traffic and trigger the associated service when needed. Stopping only the service can leave its socket listening and able to reactivate it. Inspect both units when diagnosing a listener.","e":"systemctl list-sockets","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/systemd.socket.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["list-sockets","socket","reactivate","trigger","diagnosing","stopping","listener"]},{"i":"quiz-topic-691","t":"Services","q":"Why may a stopped socket-activated service start again?","a":"The active socket unit can reactivate it. A socket unit can have systemd listen for traffic and trigger the associated service when needed. Stopping only the service can leave its socket listening and able to reactivate it. Inspect both units when diagnosing a listener.","e":"systemctl list-sockets","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/systemd.socket.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["list-sockets","socket","reactivate","trigger","diagnosing","stopping","listener"]},{"i":"concept-693","t":"Scheduling","q":"How do systemd timers differ from cron?","a":"Timers activate units based on monotonic or calendar schedules. OnCalendar uses wall-clock expressions; OnBootSec and related settings use monotonic time. Persistent can catch up missed calendar activations when the timer returns. Check timer and service units, timezone and installed-version behavior.","e":"systemctl list-timers --all","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/systemd.timer.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["wall-clock","installed-version","list-timers","timers","activate","monotonic","oncalendar"]},{"i":"concept-694","t":"Services","q":"How can unit sandbox settings reduce service privileges?","a":"systemd.exec settings can restrict filesystem access, capabilities, privilege escalation and other resources. Options such as NoNewPrivileges and ProtectSystem depend on kernel/service-manager support and workload needs. Test the service, because restrictive settings can break legitimate access.","e":"systemd-analyze security nginx.service","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/systemd.exec.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["kernel/service-manager","nginx.service","privilege","systemd.exec","escalation","nonewprivileges","protectsystem"]},{"i":"concept-695","t":"Logs","q":"Why can journal history disappear after reboot?","a":"Journal retention depends on storage configuration, writable persistent storage and size limits. A volatile journal under /run does not survive reboot. Inspect journald.conf and actual storage; do not assume journalctl -b -1 always has a retained previous boot.","e":"journalctl --list-boots","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/journalctl.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["list-boots","volatile","journald.conf","writable","journal","persistent","survive"]},{"i":"concept-696","t":"Performance","q":"What is MemAvailable versus MemFree?","a":"MemFree is currently unused memory. MemAvailable estimates how much can be used for new applications without swapping, taking reclaimable memory into account. Cache is not automatically a leak. Use pressure, swap activity and workload evidence rather than only the free column.","e":"grep -E \"MemFree|MemAvailable\" /proc/meminfo","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/proc_meminfo.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["memfree","memavailable","proc/meminfo","leak","unused","reclaimable","swapping"]},{"i":"quiz-topic-696","t":"Performance","q":"What does MemAvailable describe?","a":"Estimated memory usable without swapping. MemFree is currently unused memory. MemAvailable estimates how much can be used for new applications without swapping, taking reclaimable memory into account. Cache is not automatically a leak. Use pressure, swap activity and workload evidence rather than only the free column.","e":"grep -E \"MemFree|MemAvailable\" /proc/meminfo","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/proc_meminfo.5.html","l":"Beginner","k":["memavailable","memfree","proc/meminfo","leak","unused","reclaimable","swapping"]},{"i":"concept-698","t":"Performance","q":"How do process limits differ from cgroup limits?","a":"Resource limits such as maximum open files apply to processes and can be inherited. Cgroups account for and constrain grouped workloads. Raising a shell ulimit does not automatically update already-running services; inspect the actual process and its service configuration.","e":"cat /proc/self/limits","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/prlimit.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["proc/self/limits","already-running","ulimit","inherited","limits","constrain","cgroups"]},{"i":"concept-699","t":"Performance","q":"What does CPU affinity do?","a":"Affinity restricts the CPUs on which a task can be scheduled. taskset inspects or changes the allowed set, subject to permissions and cpuset constraints. It is not a CPU quota, guaranteed exclusive core or automatic performance improvement.","e":"taskset -pc $$","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/taskset.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["taskset","affinity","cpuset","quota","restricts","set","task"]},{"i":"concept-700","t":"Troubleshooting","q":"How can an open deleted file keep disk space occupied?","a":"A file that is unlinked can remain open by a process, keeping its data blocks allocated until the last reference is closed. du may not see it while df still reports used space. Inspect lsof +L1 and the responsible service before an approved graceful reopen or restart.","e":"sudo lsof +L1","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/bookworm/lsof/lsof.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["lsof","unlinked","reopen","graceful","restart","space","open"]},{"i":"quiz-topic-700","t":"Troubleshooting","q":"Why might df show space used after deletion while du does not?","a":"A process still holds the deleted file open. A file that is unlinked can remain open by a process, keeping its data blocks allocated until the last reference is closed. du may not see it while df still reports used space. Inspect lsof +L1 and the responsible service before an approved graceful reopen or restart.","e":"sudo lsof +L1","s":"https://manpages.debian.org/bookworm/lsof/lsof.8.en.html","l":"Beginner","k":["lsof","unlinked","reopen","graceful","restart","space","allocated"]},{"i":"concept-702","t":"Troubleshooting","q":"When is strace useful and risky?","a":"strace records system calls and signals, which can reveal missing paths or permission failures. Attaching may slow a process and expose secrets in arguments or data; ptrace permissions apply. Start with a disposable program and capture only authorized diagnostics.","e":"strace -e trace=openat,access stat ./lab-file","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/strace.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["strace","trace","openat","lab-file","ptrace","reveal","disposable"]},{"i":"concept-703","t":"Shell","q":"Why use NUL-delimited filenames with find and xargs?","a":"Filenames can contain spaces and newlines. find -print0 paired with xargs -0 preserves names more reliably than whitespace parsing. GNU xargs -r avoids running the command with no input. Use a non-destructive action while testing.","e":"find ./lab -type f -print0 | xargs -0 -r stat --","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/xargs.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["xargs","print0","non-destructive","filenames","newlines","reliably","parsing"]},{"i":"quiz-topic-703","t":"Shell","q":"Which separator safely represents arbitrary filenames in this pipeline?","a":"NUL-delimited records. Filenames can contain spaces and newlines. find -print0 paired with xargs -0 preserves names more reliably than whitespace parsing. GNU xargs -r avoids running the command with no input. Use a non-destructive action while testing.","e":"find ./lab -type f -print0 | xargs -0 -r stat --","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/xargs.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["nul-delimited","print0","non-destructive","xargs","filenames","newlines","reliably"]},{"i":"concept-705","t":"Shell","q":"How should a script handle pipelines and failures?","a":"A pipeline exit status is usually that of the last command unless pipefail is enabled in a supporting shell. set -e has context-dependent exceptions and is not a complete error-handling plan. Check important statuses explicitly and test failure paths.","e":"bash -c \"set -o pipefail; false | true; printf '%s\\n' 'pipeline tested'\"","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["context-dependent","error-handling","pipeline","true","false","pipefail","printf"]},{"i":"concept-706","t":"Shell","q":"What is the difference between sourcing and executing a script?","a":"Sourcing runs commands in the current shell, allowing variables and directory changes to persist. Executing generally runs a separate process and cannot change its parent shell environment. Source only trusted code; it can still perform arbitrary effects.","e":"type source","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["executing","persist","arbitrary","sourcing","source","cannot","type"]},{"i":"quiz-topic-706","t":"Shell","q":"Where does a sourced Bash script run?","a":"The current shell. Sourcing runs commands in the current shell, allowing variables and directory changes to persist. Executing generally runs a separate process and cannot change its parent shell environment. Source only trusted code; it can still perform arbitrary effects.","e":"type source","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["persist","executing","source","arbitrary","sourcing","cannot","type"]},{"i":"concept-708","t":"Text","q":"Why are basic and extended regular expressions different from shell globs?","a":"Shell globs expand filenames in a shell; grep regexes match text. grep -E uses extended regular expressions, while grep -F treats patterns as fixed strings. Quote patterns so the shell does not expand them first.","e":"grep -F -- \"[error]\" ./lab.log","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/grep.1.html","l":"Beginner","k":["lab.log","globs","extended","regexes","expressions","regular","filenames"]},{"i":"concept-709","t":"Packages","q":"Why use package verification with care?","a":"rpm -V compares installed files with package metadata, including attributes selected by the package. A difference may reflect an intentional configuration edit rather than malware. It is one diagnostic input, not a full integrity proof. Check the expected configuration and package origin.","e":"rpm -V bash","s":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/rpm.8.html","l":"Beginner","k":["malware","intentional","rpm","compares","difference","integrity","metadata"]},{"i":"concept-710","t":"Web servers","q":"What does a reverse proxy do?","a":"A reverse proxy receives client requests and forwards selected requests to upstream services. Diagnose its listener/TLS, routing configuration, upstream reachability and response separately. A 502 often indicates an invalid upstream response but logs are needed to determine the actual failure.","e":"curl -I http://127.0.0.1:8080/","s":"https://nginx.org/en/docs/beginners_guide.html","l":"Beginner","k":["listener/tls","reverse","diagnose","invalid","proxy","upstream","http"]},{"i":"concept-711","t":"Databases","q":"Why is a database backup more than copying its files?","a":"A live database can have transactional state not safely represented by an arbitrary file copy. Use a documented logical or physical backup method, required consistency options and privileges, then test recovery. Secure the dump and inspect it before importing into a test database.","e":"mysqldump --help","s":"https://docs.oracle.com/cd/E17952_01/mysql-8.4-en/mysqldump.html","l":"Beginner","k":["mysqldump","transactional","copy","dump","database","safely","consistency"]},{"i":"concept-712","t":"Containers & VMs","q":"Why is the Docker daemon a privileged access concern?","a":"Control of a typical rootful Docker daemon can grant powerful host access through containers and mounts. Do not casually add users to the docker group or publish the daemon socket. Rootless setups reduce some risks but do not make every workload safe.","e":"docker context show","s":"https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/","l":"Beginner","k":["docker","rootful","casually","rootless","context","daemon","powerful"]},{"i":"concept-713","t":"Cloud","q":"Why are cloud firewalls and a host firewall separate?","a":"EC2 security groups filter associated network-interface traffic in the cloud, while Linux firewall rules operate in the guest. Routing, network ACLs, public addressing, listener binding and identity permissions are additional layers. One allowed port does not prove the application is reachable.","e":"ss -lnt","s":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/ec2-security-groups.html","l":"Beginner","k":["network-interface","lnt","cloud","reachable","listener","ec2","operate"]},{"i":"concept-714","t":"Automation","q":"Why review --diff output before sharing it?","a":"Ansible diff mode can show before/after configuration content, including secrets. Use diff: false for sensitive tasks and do not publish inventory, vault content or output that contains credentials. A simulation report is still private operational data.","e":"ansible-playbook site.yml --syntax-check","s":"https://docs.ansible.com/projects/ansible/latest/playbook_guide/playbooks_checkmode.html","l":"Beginner","k":["before/after","ansible","vault","ansible-playbook","site.yml","syntax-check","credentials"]},{"i":"concept-715","t":"Interview practice","q":"How should a fresher answer \"Tell me about yourself\"?","a":"Use a short, truthful outline: your actual education, Linux practice, one lab you can explain, and the role you want. Replace placeholders with your own facts. Do not claim production experience you do not have.","e":"Preparation: write your own truthful example.","s":"https://careerservices.upenn.edu/videos/how-to-answer-the-tell-me-about-yourself-and-greatest-strength-weakness-questions/","l":"Beginner","k":["placeholders","truthful","preparation","outline","education","write","facts"]},{"i":"concept-716","t":"Interview practice","q":"How can you explain motivation for Linux administration?","a":"Connect your real interest to an example of diagnosing or maintaining a system. Explain what you practiced and what you want to learn. This is preparation guidance, not a statement of your personal motivation.","e":"Preparation: write your own truthful example.","s":"https://careerservices.upenn.edu/videos/how-to-answer-the-tell-me-about-yourself-and-greatest-strength-weakness-questions/","l":"Beginner","k":["motivation","practiced","explain","preparation","truthful","diagnosing","interest"]},{"i":"concept-717","t":"Interview practice","q":"How should you answer \"Why should we hire you\"?","a":"Give evidence: a task you completed, how you verified it, and a skill relevant to the vacancy. Avoid unsupported claims about expertise or responsibility. State honestly where you still need mentoring.","e":"Preparation: write your own truthful example.","s":"https://careerservices.upenn.edu/videos/how-to-answer-the-tell-me-about-yourself-and-greatest-strength-weakness-questions/","l":"Beginner","k":["verified","vacancy","honestly","unsupported","mentoring","preparation","truthful"]},{"i":"concept-718","t":"Interview practice","q":"How can you discuss strengths in an interview?","a":"Pick a strength you can support with a real example, such as checking logs systematically or documenting a lab. Explain the action and result rather than using only adjectives.","e":"Preparation: write your own truthful example.","s":"https://careerservices.upenn.edu/videos/how-to-answer-the-tell-me-about-yourself-and-greatest-strength-weakness-questions/","l":"Beginner","k":["strength","systematically","adjectives","preparation","truthful","write","own"]},{"i":"concept-719","t":"Interview practice","q":"How can you discuss a skill gap honestly?","a":"Name a real gap, explain its effect, and show a concrete practice plan. Do not invent a weakness or claim professional experience you have not had.","e":"Preparation: write your own truthful example.","s":"https://careerservices.upenn.edu/videos/how-to-answer-the-tell-me-about-yourself-and-greatest-strength-weakness-questions/","l":"Beginner","k":["gap","invent","weakness","preparation","truthful","concrete","professional"]},{"i":"concept-720","t":"Interview practice","q":"How can you answer a career-goal question?","a":"Describe your actual direction and next skills to build. Treat any sample wording as a template, not a promise about your future role or other people.","e":"Preparation: write your own truthful example.","s":"https://careerservices.upenn.edu/videos/how-to-answer-the-tell-me-about-yourself-and-greatest-strength-weakness-questions/","l":"Beginner","k":["preparation","truthful","direction","promise","people","write","own"]},{"i":"concept-721","t":"Interview practice","q":"How should you answer a shift-availability question?","a":"State your own availability and constraints truthfully, and ask about on-call frequency, rotation and expectations. No learning site can decide or claim that you are comfortable with shifts.","e":"Preparation: write your own truthful example.","s":"https://careerservices.upenn.edu/videos/how-to-answer-the-tell-me-about-yourself-and-greatest-strength-weakness-questions/","l":"Beginner","k":["on-call","truthfully","availability","rotation","preparation","truthful","frequency"]},{"i":"concept-722","t":"Interview practice","q":"What technical questions can a fresher ask the interviewer?","a":"Ask about distributions, service ownership, backup/restore practice, incident review, access controls and mentoring. Choose questions that fit the role rather than implying you already know the team setup.","e":"Preparation: write your own truthful example.","s":"https://careerservices.upenn.edu/videos/how-to-answer-the-tell-me-about-yourself-and-greatest-strength-weakness-questions/","l":"Beginner","k":["backup/restore","questions","mentoring","implying","preparation","truthful","ask"]}];